\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Respond With Skepticism To Limits on Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 1,361 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A majority of the justices questioned whether a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and organizations trying to sharply limit availability of the medication could show they suffered harm.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the Supreme Court appeared deeply skeptical on Tuesday of efforts to severely curtail access to a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, questioning whether a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and organizations had a right to challenge the Food and Drug Administration's approval of the medication.\par \par \uc2 Over nearly two hours of argument, justices across the ideological spectrum seemed likely to side with the federal government, with only two justices, the conservatives \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  and, possibly, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , appearing to favor limits on the distribution of the pill.\par \par \uc2 Describing the case as an effort by ''a handful of individuals,'' Justice \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  raised whether it would stand as ''a prime example of turning what could be a small lawsuit into a nationwide legislative assembly on an F.D.A. rule or any other federal government action.''\par \par \uc2 The challenge involves mifepristone, a drug approved by the F.D.A. more than two decades ago that is used in nearly two-thirds of abortions in the country. At issue is whether the agency acted appropriately in expanding access to the drug in 2016 and again in 2021 by allowing doctors to prescribe it through telemedicine and to send the pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had asked the Supreme Court to intervene after a three-judge panel of a federal appeals court favored curbing distribution of the drug. Until the justices decide, access to mifepristone remains unchanged, delaying the potential for abrupt limits on its availability.\par \par \uc2 Even if the court preserves full access to mifepristone, the pills will remain illegal in more than a dozen states that have enacted near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Those bans do not distinguish between medication and surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case brought the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access back to the Supreme Court, even as the conservative majority had said in the case that overturned Roe v. Wade, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, that it would cede the question ''to the people and their elected representatives.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch's pointed questioning was echoed by other justices, who asked whether any of the doctors involved in the lawsuit could show they were harmed by the federal government's approval and regulation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug.\par \par \uc2 In one instance, Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  asked the lawyer for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups whom they were relying on to show an actual injury.\par \par \uc2 ''You need a person,'' Justice Kagan said. ''So who's your person?''\par \par \uc2 Although the argument contained detailed descriptions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including questions about placental tissue and bleeding, the focus on whether the challengers were even entitled to sue suggested that the justices could rule for the F.D.A. without addressing the merits of the case.\par \par \uc2 Since the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade ended a nationwide right in place for nearly a half-century, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have increasingly become the center of political and legal fights.\par \par \uc2 The case began in November 2022, when a group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and medical organizations sued the F.D.A., asserting that the agency erred when it approved the drug in 2000.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Texas, Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, issued a preliminary ruling last spring invalidating the F.D.A.'s approval of the drug. In August, a panel of federal appeals judges in New Orleans limited his ruling, determining that mifepristone should remain legal but imposing significant restrictions on access. Those focused on the F.D.A. decisions about telemedicine and pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 A ruling for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors could have implications for the regulatory authority of the F.D.A., potentially calling into question the agency's ability to approve and distribute other drugs.\par \par \uc2 Solicitor General Elizabeth B. Prelogar, arguing for the government, warned of the far-ranging consequences, both for the pharmaceutical industry and for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''It harms the pharmaceutical industry, which is sounding alarm bells in this case and saying that this would destabilize the system for approving and regulating drugs,'' she said. ''And it harms women who need access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the conditions that F.D.A. determined were safe and effective.''\par \par \uc2 To bring the legal challenge, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and groups must show that they will suffer concrete harm if the pill remains widely available. Lawyers call this requirement standing.\par \par \uc2 Whether anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups had met this basic threshold took up much of the questioning.\par \par \uc2 The argument zeroed in on the declarations by seven anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors in the lawsuit. They said they have suffered moral injuries from the availability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill because they may be forced to treat women who come to emergency rooms suffering complications from the pill, including heavy bleeding.\par \par \uc2 Erin M. Hawley, the lawyer for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors, claimed that her clients suffered harm from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill and were subjected to acting against their conscience. They were forced to treat women in ''life-threatening situations in which the choice for a doctor is either to scrub out and try to find someone else or to treat the woman who's hemorrhaging on the emergency room table,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawley, who is married to \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , a Republican senator from Missouri who has been involved in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, added that in an emergency, ''it's a lot to ask'' for ''doctors to go up to the top floor and litigate this with the general counsel when the federal government's telling them they don't have a conscience protection.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Prelogar asserted that the claims by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and groups ''rest on a long chain of remote contingencies,'' with scientific studies showing that medical complications from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are very rare.\par \par \uc2 She argued that there was only a slim chance that doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to treat patients. If those doctors wanted to opt out, they can do so under federal conscience protections, policies that allow doctors and other health workers to refrain from providing care they object to.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  challengers had made generalizations, with no specific example of a doctor who had to provide care against their conscience, Ms. Prelogar said, demonstrating ''that the past harm hasn't happened.''\par \par \uc2 She urged the justices to ''put an end to this case.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas asked Ms. Prelogar who could bring such a lawsuit, if she was correct that the doctors could not show a direct injury.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Prelogar demurred, Justice Alito, who wrote the majority opinion in Dobbs, returned to the point.\par \par \uc2 ''Is there anybody who could challenge in court the lawfulness of what the F.D.A. did here in this particular case?'' he asked.\par \par \uc2 ''In this particular case, I think the answer is no,'' Ms. Prelogar responded.\par \par \uc2 ''Well, that wasn't my question,'' Justice Alito said. ''Is there anybody who can do that?''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Prelogar said there was ''a profound mismatch here'' between the injury claimed by the doctors -- that they would be forced to participate in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by treating women who had taken an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill -- and the remedy they sought, which was to end access to the drug for everyone.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  examined the idea that if the justices chipped away at the F.D.A.'s regulatory powers, it may fall to ''judges parsing medical and scientific studies'' to determine whether a drug is safe.\par \par \uc2 Jessica L. Ellsworth, the lawyer for Danco Laboratories, a manufacturer of the drug, agreed that such a system would raise concerns for ''pharmaceutical companies who do depend on F.D.A.'s gold standard review process to approve their drugs and then to be able to sell their products in line with that considered judgment.''\par \par \uc2 A demonstration outside of the Supreme Court on Tuesday during arguments over a challenge to the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MAANSI SRIVASTAVA/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240327ek3r0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
