\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Dueling Rallies and Arrests: The Scene Outside the Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Qiu \par \uc2 647 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thirteen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights protesters were arrested near the Supreme Court as part of a civil disobedience action. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents were less numerous but still vocal.\par \par \uc2 Bearing colorful signs and banners that read ''Doctors Not Doctrine'' and ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Health Care,'' hundreds of activists chanted, marched and rallied for hours outside the Supreme Court starting Tuesday morning, before the justices weighed the availability of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights outnumbered those opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the two factions occasionally sparred with rallying calls, including over the safety of the pill, mifepristone. (Studies show that is, in fact, safe for terminating a pregnancy.)\par \par \uc2 Some had traveled across the country to demonstrate. Courtney Brown, a coffee shop owner who helped found an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group in Amarillo, Texas, where the case originated, described her town as ''ground zero'' in the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She added, ''I'm just ready to fight back because we're so tired of having those rights stripped away.''\par \par \uc2 Circling the court were a handful of small spherical robots containing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, remotely operated and called ''Roe-bots.'' Potential recipients would use the ''Roe-bot'' to complete a telehealth consultation with a provider in a state where the pill is legal, and the machine would then dispense a pill.\par \par \uc2 Three medical students from New York accompanying the ''Roe-bots'' emphasized the importance of the case, as future doctors and OB-GYNs who risked facing restrictions on reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court justices ''didn't go to medical school,'' said Sarah McNeilly, a student at \uc2 Albert Einstein College of Medicine\uc2 . ''You should trust and depend on the F.D.A., which is a world-class institution filled with brilliant people who are very committed to their jobs and you should let them do them and stop trying to politicize science.''\par \par \uc2 About 13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists were arrested for violating a law against crowding, obstructing or incommoding on the grounds of the U.S. Capitol, according to the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 . The arrests were part of a plan of civil disobedience meant to underscore the wide-ranging consequences of the case, which could sharply curtail access to the pill and undercut the regulatory authority of the F.D.A. Getting arrested was intended to ''match the severity of the threat'' to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Rachel O'Leary Carmona, executive director of the Women's March, who was among the 13 detained.\par \par \uc2 The arrests also send a message to the Supreme Court that ''no matter what the outcome is, we are going to make sure that people have access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication when they need it,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 As arguments were underway, activists held dueling rallies on either side of the steps of court. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists decried casting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a women's health issue.\par \par \uc2 One such activist, Melanie Salazar, 31 weeks pregnant, had scrawled a message on her bare belly: ''Fully Human.'' Feeling her baby move, she said, further crystallized her opinions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fueling a hope for ''another win against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industrial complex.''\par \par \uc2 ''My baby is fully human just like I'm fully human and how every child is fully human,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Celeste McCall, 79, who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, interjected, ''I'm fully human, too.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. McCall added that she felt the urge to speak alongside Ms. Salazar as ''she apparently has been getting bad information, saying that the pills are harmful.''\par \par \uc2 ''I thought we settled this all 50 years ago when I was young,'' she said, gesturing toward the court before running off to join a chant. ''Haven't they done enough damage?''\par \par \uc2 ''We're so tired of having those rights stripped away,'' one protester said near the Supreme Court. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MAANSI SRIVASTAVA/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240327ek3r0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
