\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Use of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills Surges Post Roe, Research Shows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 862 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 On the eve of oral arguments in a Supreme Court case that could affect future access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, new research shows the fast-growing use of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally and the many ways women have obtained access to the method since Roe v. Wade was overturned in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 The Details\par \par \uc2 A study, published on Monday in the medical journal JAMA, found that the number of abortions using pills obtained outside the formal health system soared in the six months after the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overturned. Another report, published last week by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, found that medication abortions now account for nearly two-thirds of all abortions provided by the country's formal health system, which includes clinics and telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The JAMA study evaluated data from overseas telemedicine organizations, online vendors and networks of community volunteers that generally obtain pills from outside the United States. Before Roe was overturned, these avenues provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to about 1,400 women per month, but in the six months afterward, the average jumped to 5,900 per month, the study reported.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the study found that while abortions in the formal health care system declined by about 32,000 from July through December 2022, much of that decline was offset by about 26,000 medication abortions from pills provided by sources outside the formal health system.\par \par \uc2 ''We see what we see elsewhere in the world in the U.S. -- that when anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws go into effect, oftentimes outside of the formal health care setting is where people look, and the locus of care gets shifted,'' said Dr. Abigail Aiken, who is an associate professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  and the lead author of the JAMA study.\par \par \uc2 The co-authors were a statistics professor at the university; the founder of Aid Access, a Europe-based organization that helped pioneer telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States; and a leader of Plan C, an organization that provides consumers with information about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Before publication, the study went through the rigorous peer review process required by a major medical journal.\par \par \uc2 The telemedicine organizations in the study evaluated prospective patients using written medical questionnaires, issued prescriptions from doctors who were typically in Europe and had pills shipped from pharmacies in India, generally charging about $100. Community networks typically asked for some information about the pregnancy and either delivered or mailed pills with detailed instructions, often for free.\par \par \uc2 Online vendors, which supplied a small percentage of the pills in the study and charged between $39 and $470, generally did not ask for women's medical history and shipped the pills with the least detailed instructions. Vendors in the study were vetted by Plan C and found to be providing genuine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, Dr. Aiken said.\par \par \uc2 The Guttmacher report, focusing on the formal health care system, included data from clinics and telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services within the United States that provided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to patients who lived in or traveled to states with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  between January and December 2023.\par \par \uc2 It found that pills accounted for 63 percent of those abortions, up from 53 percent in 2020. The total number of abortions in the report was over a million for the first time in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Why This Matters\par \par \uc2 Overall, the new reports suggest how rapidly the provision of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has adjusted amid post-Roe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in 14 states and tight restrictions in others.\par \par \uc2 The numbers may be an undercount and do not reflect the most recent shift:shield laws in six states allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to prescribe and mail pills to tens of thousands of women in states with bans without requiring them to travel. Since last summer, for example, Aid Access has stopped shipping medication from overseas and operating outside the formal health system; it is instead mailing pills to states with bans from within the United States with the protection of shield laws.\par \par \uc2 What's Next\par \par \uc2 In the case that will be argued before the Supreme Court on Tuesday, the plaintiffs, who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are suing the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , seeking to block or drastically limit the availability of mifepristone, the first pill in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen.\par \par \uc2 The JAMA study suggests that such a ruling could prompt more women to use avenues outside the formal American health care system, such as pills from other countries.\par \par \uc2 ''There's so many unknowns about what will happen with the decision,'' Dr. Aiken said.\par \par \uc2 She added: ''It's possible that a decision by the Supreme Court in favor of the plaintiffs could have a knock-on effect where more people are looking to access outside the formal health care setting, either because they're worried that access is going away or they're having more trouble accessing the medications.''\par \par \uc2 Aid Access, which mails \uc2  pills around the country, preparing doses of misoprostol to ship from Massachusetts. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SOPHIE PARK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240326ek3q0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
