\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Visiting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinic, Harris Says Restrictions Are 'Health Care Crisis'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Nicholas Nehamas \par \uc2 1,370 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The trip made history and offered a vivid look at how the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have transformed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  described the flood of laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a ''health care crisis'' as she visited with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and staff members on Thursday at a clinic in St. Paul, Minn.\par \par \uc2 The stop by Ms. Harris at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic was believed to be the first official visit by a vice president to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. No presidents are known to have made such visits, either.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters in the lobby of the clinic, which was open and seeing patients, Ms. Harris assailed conservative ''extremists'' for passing laws that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , resulting in the denial of emergency care for pregnant women and the shuttering of clinics that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''These attacks against an individual's right to make decisions about their own body are outrageous and, in many instances, just plain old immoral,'' she said. ''How dare these elected leaders believe they are in a better position to tell women what they need, to tell women what is in their best interest. We have to be a nation that trusts women.''\par \par \uc2 The image alone of the nation's second-ranking leader walking into an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic provided a vivid illustration of how the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have transformed since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022. In the lobby was a map showing \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in Minnesota and neighboring states. Minnesota had by far the most, with a few in Iowa. Nebraska, North Dakota and South Dakota were almost bare -- all have restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since the overturning of Roe.\par \par \uc2 For decades, many Democrats viewed affirmative support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a political risk, fearing such a position could alienate more moderate voters who were uncomfortable with open discussion of the procedure. The party embraced cautious slogans like ''safe, legal and rare'' and policies like banning taxpayer funding of abortions.\par \par \uc2 But the fall of Roe upended those politics, energizing a new generation of voters energized by their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The issue has become one of the Democrats' biggest strengths, party strategists say. In campaign speeches, as he did in his State of the Union address, President Biden casts the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as one of personal freedom and the right to make private health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 Celinda Lake, a Democratic pollster who has been surveying voters about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than four decades, said she could not recall a time when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were as motivating for their voters.\par \par \uc2 ''It's the No. 1 issue working for Democrats at every level in office,'' Ms. Lake said. ''Everything from county commissioners to presidents are being elected around this issue.''\par \par \uc2 The issue is firmer ground for Democrats. Ms. Harris was visiting Minnesota a week after 19 percent of voters in the Democratic primary voted for ''uncommitted,'' as many of them treated it as a protest against the administration's policies in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 After little discussion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Mr. Biden's 2020 campaign, his strategists are embracing the issue. They've run ads featuring the testimonials of women denied access to the procedure in conservative states and attacked former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for appointing three of the justices who voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Democrats' efforts have been helped by a steady drumbeat of litigation, legislation and court decisions in conservative states that restrict not only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but also other aspects of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including contraception and fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 Tresa Undem, a pollster who tracks public opinion about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said those actions had changed how voters -- particularly women -- view the role of government in their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 Recent polling from KFF, a nonprofit group focused on health policy, found that 86 percent of female voters of reproductive age say decisions about abortions should be made by a woman, in consultation with her doctor. Broad majorities also want laws guaranteeing a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women facing pregnancy-related emergencies and the right to travel to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''They are scared about their own mortality,'' Ms. Undem said. ''And they don't want politicians or the government to have any say in the circumstances and the reasons, the why and the when.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has promised to restore federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and preserve access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which faces new threats from a case set to be argued before the Supreme Court this month. Those assurances represent a notable escalation for Mr. Biden, an observant Catholic who spent decades caught between his religious opposition to the procedure and the policy of his party.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Biden has still expressed some uneasiness with the procedure itself, often avoiding uttering the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 It is Ms. Harris who has emerged as the administration's most forceful champion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, touring the country to highlight the actions taken by the administration to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. She has taken a far more assertive approach than the president, holding meetings about the topic with hundreds of state lawmakers, meeting with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and patients and speaking about the once-taboo issue in plain language.\par \par \uc2 ''Please do understand that when we talk about a clinic such as this, it is absolutely about health care and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. So everyone get ready for the language: uterus,'' she said, speaking outside the clinic in St. Paul. ''Issues like fibroids. We can handle this.''\par \par \uc2 Nearly all of her stops have been in Democratic-led states that have become havens for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers, as broad swaths of the South and the Midwest have ushered in more restrictive laws.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, at St. Paul Health Center, Vandalia, where Ms. Harris was speaking, about two dozen anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters stood in the street outside holding signs that read ''Planned Parenthood = \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not healthcare.''\par \par \uc2 A doctor at the clinic said it had experienced a 25 percent increase in abortions and a 100 percent increase in out-of-state patients since Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''Minnesota has become a bastion of access for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,'' said Dr. Sarah Traxler, the chief medical officer of Planned Parenthood North Central States. ''Our new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape is difficult. It is dangerous. And it is putting my patients and health care providers at severe risk.''\par \par \uc2 White House officials say they have largely reached the limits of their power to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Legislation codifying federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has failed twice and has no chance of passage, given the narrow Democratic majority in the Senate and disagreements within the president's own party over the scope of such a bill.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials have encouraged Democratic state legislators to take a proactive role on the issue. Last year, Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  of Minnesota signed legislation enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into state law, an effort to ensure the procedure remains legal no matter who takes office in the state.\par \par \uc2 The Society of Family Planning, a health research organization, found that the average number of abortions in the state increased by about 36 percent in the year after the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 ''What happened here in Minnesota with the re-election of the governor and the turning of the State Legislature, is what has led to ensuring that these fundamental rights are intact and are protected,'' said Ms. Harris, before leaving the clinic for a Women for Biden-Harris rally in St. Paul. ''Elections matter.''\par \par \uc2 At the campaign rally later in the afternoon, Ms. Harris put the blame for what has happened post-Roe on Mr. Trump, calling him ''the architect of a health care crisis.''\par \par \uc2 The former president, she said, was ''proud that women across our nation are suffering, proud that doctors and nurses could be thrown in prison for administering care.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris at a Planned Parenthood clinic in St. Paul, Minn. ''We have to be a nation that trusts women,'' she said. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JENN ACKERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240315ek3f0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
