\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New Rule Limits 'Forum Shopping' by Plaintiffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mattathias Schwartz \par \uc2 963 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For years, litigants have tried to cherry-pick the judges in sweeping cases on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and immigration. Random judge selection is about to make that harder.\par \par \uc2 When anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists sued the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  in 2022 seeking to overturn the approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone, they filed their suit in the federal court in Amarillo, Texas, where it was all but assured that the case would be heard by Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, an outspoken opponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Judge Kacsmaryk, the sole federal judge in Amarillo, wound up agreeing with the plaintiffs that the drug was ''unsafe.'' In his ruling, he invalidated the F.D.A.'s 23-year-old approval of the drug and opened a new front in the post-Dobbs reckoning over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The suit -- and the role of Judge Kacsmaryk, who handles 95 percent of the Amarillo civil caseload -- was one of the most striking recent examples of ''forum shopping,'' where plaintiffs to try to cherry-pick sympathetic judges.\par \par \uc2 Now, forum shopping is about to get harder.\par \par \uc2 The panel of federal judges who set policy for the rest of federal judiciary on Tuesday announced a new rule intended to curb the practice in civil cases with nationwide implications, like the mifepristone suit.\par \par \uc2 In such cases, where plaintiffs are seeking a sweeping remedy, like a nationwide injunction, the judge will be assigned at random from across the district instead of defaulting to the judge or judges in a particular courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Forum shopping has been used for years by litigants from across the political spectrum who file suit in districts and appellate circuits where they believe the pool of judges will play to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 In challenging federal immigration policies, advocates for immigrants have often sought out federal courts in California, reasoning that in a more liberal state, there's a better chance of the case being assigned to a judge who is sympathetic to arguments against the government.\par \par \uc2 In some cases filed by conservatives, such as the mifepristone challenge, plaintiffs have sought to take forum shopping even further, aiming to have cases assigned to a specific judge they think will take a favorable view of their arguments.\par \par \uc2 While some of the country's 94 federal district courts already assign cases randomly, most sort them into ''divisions,'' judicial subdistricts that funnel cases down to smaller groups of judges. In at least 90 of those divisions, a single judge handled more than half the caseload, according to a 2018 article in the Columbia Human Rights Law Review.\par \par \uc2 Eleven of those, according to the article, are in Texas, where conservatives have won nationwide injunctions against immigration programs, transgender rights and labor policies from the Obama era. The rulings have then been mostly upheld by the conservative U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit. Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling, which was upheld in part by the Fifth Circuit, is on hold while it awaits review by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The effort to address forum shopping comes as the federal judiciary faces mounting pressure to reform itself. After revelations that Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  had failed to disclose lavish gifts and a forgiven loan on his annual financial disclosures, the Judicial Conference tightened the requirements for reporting free travel and gifts, and the Supreme Court adopted a new code of conduct. Critics have said that these self-imposed measures don't go far enough to ensure compliance.\par \par \uc2 Judge Robert J. Conrad Jr., head of the federal courts' Administrative Office, said the new forum-shopping rollback ''promotes the impartiality of proceedings and bolsters public confidence in the federal judiciary.'' While the new policy takes effect immediately, how and when to put it into practice will be left to the district courts, said Judge Jeffrey Sutton, who chairs the Judicial Conference's executive committee.\par \par \uc2 Critics of forum shopping tend to focus on high-stakes political issues, but the practice has far-reaching implications for more arcane areas of the law as well. In a 2021 letter to Supreme Court Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , two U.S. senators raised concerns about a report that a single judge from the Western District of Texas was hearing 25 percent of patent cases nationwide. The senators claimed the judge, Alan D. Albright, was soliciting cases from potential plaintiffs and called the practice ''unseemly and inappropriate.''\par \par \uc2 In his 2021 year-end report, Justice Roberts wrote that geographic divisions were beneficial insofar as federal judges were ''tied to their communities'' but that they should also be ''generalists capable of handling the full range of legal issues.'' Judge Albright did not respond to a request for comment sent to his courtroom aide on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In the days after the Texas mifepristone ruling, two Democrats in Congress introduced a bill that would have mandated random case assignments and restored a practice that was put in place between 1937 and 1976, in which any lawsuit challenging the constitutionality of a state or federal law was heard by a three-judge panel.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  of Oregon, the bill's Senate sponsor, also called on the Judicial Conference to make that change. ''No single judge should have the power to make sweeping decisions that could harm millions of Americans,'' he said. ''If a decision will have national consequences, it should be heard by a panel of judges.''\par \par \uc2 A 2023 protest in Amarillo, Texas, where a lawsuit was filed over the Food and Drug Administration's approval of an \uc2  drug. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MERIDITH KOHUT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk in 2017. He is an outspoken opponent of \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240314ek3e00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
