\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 French Lawmakers Amend Constitution to Guarantee Access to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Porter \par \uc2 1,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legislators passed an amendment giving women the ''guaranteed freedom'' to end their pregnancies, which experts called a global first.\par \par \uc2 French legislators on Monday voted to explicitly enshrine access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Constitution, making their country the first in the world to do so.\par \par \uc2 Acutely aware that they were breaking historical ground from the grand assembly room inside Versailles Palace, the politicians delivered impassioned speeches about women's rights around the world, paid homage to the courageous Frenchwomen who had fought for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights when it was illegal and leaped up time and again to offer standing ovations.\par \par \uc2 ''We are sending the message to all women: Your body belongs to you and no one has the right to control it in your stead,'' Prime Minister Gabriel Attal said, before the gathered lawmakers voted 780-72 for the amendment.\par \par \uc2 The amendment declares \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be a ''guaranteed freedom,'' overseen by Parliament's laws. That means future governments will not be able to ''drastically modify'' the current laws funding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women who seek one, up to 14 weeks into their pregnancies, according to the French justice minister, \u201\'00\'C9ric Dupond-Moretti.\par \par \uc2 Amending the Constitution is not without precedent in France; the current Constitution has been modified over 20 times since it was adopted in 1958. But it is rare. Lawmakers last amended it in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The motivation for the latest change was the decision by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to overturn Roe v. Wade in 2022, an issue raised repeatedly by legislators. But the move also reflects the widespread support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in France, and a successful campaign by a coalition of feminist activists and lawmakers from multiple parties.\par \par \uc2 ''France is showing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer an option, it's a condition of our democracy,'' said M\u233\'00\'E9lanie Vogel, a Green Party senator and major force behind the bill.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Vogel said in an interview, ''I want to send a message to feminists outside of France. Everyone told me a year ago it was impossible.'' She added: ''Nothing is impossible when you mobilize society.''\par \par \uc2 The Vatican and the French Conference of Bishops opposed the amendment, as did anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist groups. But in France, a country where calls to protest regularly bring hundreds of thousands to the streets, opposition was notably scarce.\par \par \uc2 Instead, hundreds of Parisians gathered Monday afternoon in Trocad\u233\'00\'E9ro Square to watch the vote broadcast on a large screen. ''We hope it guarantees our rights, especially in the face of the terrifying threat of the extreme right,'' said Yanna Antigny-Fernandes, 21, moments before the final count was declared, to the sound of celebratory screams and Beyonc\u233\'00\'E9's ''Run the World (Girls).''\par \par \uc2 ''It's Simone de Beauvoir who said that women's rights were the first freedoms to be attacked.''\par \par \uc2 With the vote, France became the first country in the world to explicitly write access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into its Constitution, according to five constitutional experts.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not stating reproductive choices or the right to have children; it's a very different language when you say access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Anna Sledzinska-Simon, a professor of comparative constitutions and human rights law at the University of Wroclaw in Poland. ''The French are calling it by its name -- that's crucial.''\par \par \uc2 Constitutional experts say the amendment broadens the mold of France's fundamental text, written by men for men.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a big milestone, because it goes to the very foundation of this idea that constitutions were about men's autonomy,'' said Ruth Rubio-Mar\u237\'00\'EDn, author of a book on gender and constitutions, noting that historically women were only defined as ''breeders and caretakers.''\par \par \uc2 Other constitutions, like Ecuador's, have been broadened to include things like support for caregiving and the equal division of domestic work. But they often remain aspirational, said Ms. Rubio-Mar\u237\'00\'EDn, who teaches constitutional law at the \uc2 University of Seville\uc2  in Spain.\par \par \uc2 ''That this is happening in the Old World, in an established democracy where the constitution is taken seriously -- in that way, it's historic,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The fight for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in France burst into public view in 1971, when 343 Frenchwomen signed a manifesto written by the French feminist Simone de Beauvoir declaring that they had undertaken clandestine, illegal abortions and demanding that the law change.\par \par \uc2 Four years later, a female minister, Simone Veil, successfully pushed through a temporary law decriminalizing abortions.\par \par \uc2 Throughout Monday's special legislative session, lawmakers paid tribute to Ms. Veil, as well as Gis\u232\'00\'E8le Halimi, the former lawyer whose defense of a 16-year-old student who had endured an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after having been raped led to her acquittal in 1972.\par \par \uc2 ''We have followed in your footsteps and like you, we succeeded,'' said Senator Laurence Rossignol, a former women's rights minister. She added that French feminists would continue to fight internationally against ''those who resist,'' citing politicians including Donald J. Trump and President \uc2 Vladimir V. Putin\uc2  of Russia.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Liberty\uc2 , equality, fraternity,'' she said, citing France's national motto. ''And, if I could add, sorority.''\par \par \uc2 Over the past five decades, the law assuring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has continually been expanded, to the point that it is now considered among the most liberal in Europe. It includes the right to fully funded abortions for women and minors up to the 14th week of pregnancy upon request, with no waiting period or required counseling sessions.\par \par \uc2 Later abortions are permitted if the pregnancy is deemed a risk to the woman's physical or psychological health or if the fetus presents certain anomalies.\par \par \uc2 After the Covid pandemic hit, France quickly ensured that women seeking abortions could receive medical consultations virtually, said Laura Rahm, a researcher at \uc2 Central European University\uc2 , in Vienna, who examined access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in France for a five-year European study.\par \par \uc2 ''A system always shines or cracks when it's put under pressure,'' she said. The French system had clearly shone, she said.\par \par \uc2 Still, studies show that 17 percent of women travel outside their home regions -- known as departments in France -- for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, sometimes because of a growing shortage of medical facilities locally.\par \par \uc2 While the law states that women should have a choice of medical or surgical abortions, in practice that's often not the case, said Sarah Durocher, national co-president of Le Planning Familial, a French equivalent of Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 Putting the ''guaranteed freedom'' to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Constitution means that will have to change, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''This will give birth to other things,'' said Ms. Durocher, noting that 130 centers offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had closed in France over the past decade. ''For example, real policies so there is effective access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Despite the new amendment, French feminists say France remains a male-dominated society where sexism persists. Settling into her perch overseeing the session as the president of the National Assembly, Ya\u235\'00\'EBl Braun-Pivet noted that she was the first woman in French history to preside over such a gathering, a congress of both houses of Parliament.\par \par \uc2 But unlike in the United States, the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in France is not politically charged or highly divisive. Instead, most French people believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a basic public health service and a woman's right. A recent 29-country survey showed France with the second-highest support for legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, after Sweden.\par \par \uc2 But attempts to introduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the Constitution had failed before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. The decision drove French lawmakers to safeguard the practice, presenting multiple bills within months. Last year, the French government introduced its own bill seeking to enshrine it in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, members of a coalition of lawmakers and feminist organizations feared that the Senate, dominated by conservatives, might derail the amendment, but it passed.\par \par \uc2 ''We managed to create this environment, where if you voted against this change, it meant you wanted to maintain the right as a legislator to potentially prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future,'' said Ms. Vogel. ''So if you are not against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you had no reason not to vote in favor of it.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''That narrative penetrated society.''\par \par \uc2 S\u233\'00\'E9gol\u232\'00\'E8ne Le Stradic and Aurelien Breeden contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 S\u233\'00\'E9gol\u232\'00\'E8ne Le Stradic and Aurelien Breeden contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 At left, people gathered Monday at Trocad\u233\'00\'E9ro Square in Paris to watch live coverage of the vote on \uc2 . Above, protesters in Versailles, near Paris, were among a scarce show of opposition. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GONZALO FUENTES/REUTERS; GEOFFROY VAN DER HASSELT/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240305ek350003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
