\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 A Surprise Ruling On I.V.F.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Cottle, Ross Douthat, Carlos Lozada and Lydia Polgreen \par \uc2 1,455 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last month, Alabama's Supreme Court ruled that frozen embryos are considered ''extrauterine children,'' prompting hospitals in the state to suspend in vitro fertilization procedures and kicking off a national conversation about embryos, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I.V.F. and Republicans. Four Times Opinion writers discussed these topics and much more for the podcast ''Matter of Opinion'' last week. This conversation has been edited for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Lydia Polgreen: This decision is coming at a very politically potent moment. Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, fights over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have upended a lot of our fixed ideas about politics. It played havoc with the midterms in 2022. And now it seems as though other areas of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care are up for grabs just in time for the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 But this ruling took me really by surprise. I'm curious: Were you surprised?\par \par \uc2 Carlos Lozada: Often with significant cases that are going to generate either big public controversy or big policy impacts, we hear about them months ahead of time. This one came like a lightning bolt. You've been hearing a lot of warnings that, post-Dobbs, things like I.V.F. could potentially be at risk. There's some I-told-you-isms going around, but not with me. This was very surprising.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Cottle: So I, like Carlos, had not had forewarning that this was in the pipeline. I just had missed it somehow, but what actually happened does not surprise me.\par \par \uc2 Ross Douthat: I'm never surprised by anything.\par \par \uc2 Michelle: Fair.\par \par \uc2 Ross: No, I mean, I was not familiar with all of the details of the specific Alabama laws that were interpreted in this case, so I won't pretend to have had foreknowledge in that sense. But having some kind of I.V.F. flashpoint in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate was pretty much inevitable.\par \par \uc2 Lydia: I think Michelle and I feel strongly that the Alabama ruling is a direct result of Dobbs, that it's all happening under the penumbra of this new post-Roe era. That, to me, feels inescapable. Voters are not going to sit down and read this decision. They're going to be like, ''Oh my god, there's a new thing about my \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' And they are going to think about Dobbs whether or not the decision actually argues that. You can't really run away from it.\par \par \uc2 Ross: Perhaps the way the media covers these things also plays some role in influencing how voters receive rulings.\par \par \uc2 Michelle: Well, that's true of every issue, right?\par \par \uc2 Ross: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 Carlos: Trump is, as with so many things, able to get a free pass on issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  because people look at him and think, ''He doesn't really think this.'' But you know what? There's a lot of video of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking victory laps for putting \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  on the court. It's going to be hard to make too strong a separation. And the way this Alabama ruling plays into that is interesting because, as I read the decision, there's one sense in which it didn't feel like Dobbs played a major part in it. It drew on laws and constitutional amendments that long predated Dobbs. At the same time, it also felt to me that you can have state justices feel more empowered to write rulings such as this one in a Dobbs world. Not because they draw explicitly from it, but because the window has opened more widely.\par \par \uc2 Michelle: That's what I'm saying. It's not the specifics of this particular case, or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being kicked to the states in general. It's the pro-life reaction to it. The pro-life movement was not prepared for Roe to fall. The movement responded in ways like, for instance, thinking about making it a crime for women from a pro-life state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere. Things like that just make people freak out because whatever your moral claims, people are like, ''Wow, that's just punitive and was not what I was expecting.''\par \par \uc2 Ross: Let me say something in defense of the pro-life movement in this context. I think it's fair to say the movement wasn't prepared for the fall of Roe. At the same time, the movement is a relatively small group of people in the context of America, working in activist groups, many of them in Washington, D.C., who are trying to deal with the political reality of this larger thing called the Republican Party. And it's the Republican Party that's totally unprepared to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or handle it publicly in any way.\par \par \uc2 And the pro-life movement doesn't have the power to determine what a random state legislator does or what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  says. And what we're witnessing playing out is the failures of the movement in some way, but it's also a testament to the limits of a discreet movement as an agent operating in a context where most of the people involved in U.S. politics don't want to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think what you see with Republicans is on the one hand, you get the outliers who are saying things like, ''We're going to prosecute women'' and so on. And then on the other hand, you have the Republican legislators who are like, ''What can we do to make this issue magically go away?'' And so you have both things going on, and I don't think the pro-life movement has figured out what to do about that.\par \par \uc2 Lydia: Look, it's useful to compare the two parties in this regard, not specifically on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but think about what is considered to be an extreme left point of view, like defund the police. No one actually defunded the police, right? That was a thing that created this huge uproar. And I think that there is this cohesive sensibleness that is broadly enforced in the Democratic Party, whereas it seems with the Republican Party, there are just all of these things that just keep popping up from judges in particular places who are either elected or appointed or state legislatures that are completely uncompetitive and therefore can play in these highly ideological spaces.\par \par \uc2 To me, it speaks more to the fact that the Republican Party seems much more effectively hacked by people who hold opinions far outside of the mainstream than the Democratic Party is at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Ross: I don't know about that. I think that there are --\par \par \uc2 Lydia: I mean, give me an example, please.\par \par \uc2 Ross: So on the Republican side, let's see what happens with I.V.F. and we can revisit this conversation in six months. If you get a bunch of Republican states that either ban I.V.F. or embrace models that effectively close I.V.F. clinics, then I'll agree that that is a surprising example of a very small faction within the Republican Party working its will politically.\par \par \uc2 I know more people who are anti-I.V.F., I'm sure, than anyone else who works at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , and I am pretty skeptical that their views are going to actually be law in Alabama or Mississippi in two years. But let's see.\par \par \uc2 Lydia: But Ross, I'm sorry -- what's a comparable example on the left?\par \par \uc2 Ross: The way American politics works is that the comparable examples on the left tend to be in city government rather than state government.\par \par \uc2 Lydia: That seems like a pretty big difference.\par \par \uc2 Ross: Well, what is more important to America, Philadelphia and San Francisco or a rural red state?\par \par \uc2 Lydia: The state of Alabama?\par \par \uc2 Ross: I think these are open questions. I think control of the city governments of the Bay Area is more politically important than political control of the government of Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Michelle: I want to take a step back here: In the last Congress, 167 House Republicans co-sponsored the Life at Conception Act, which would confer full personhood rights to fertilized eggs. Then you start up a battle between the rights of the mother and the rights of the fertilized egg at whatever stage that if we're taking this seriously it's going to lead to all kinds of problems.\par \par \uc2 Co-hosts of the podcast ''Matter of Opinion.''\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY SCIENCE  LIBRARY/ZEPHYR, VIA GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page SR4, SR5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240303ek330005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
