\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 France Moves Closer to 'Guaranteed'  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Porter \par \uc2 1,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The vote by lawmakers comes after supporters of the measure in France were galvanized by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 France took a step closer to enshrining access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its Constitution, after senators backed a bill on Wednesday to include it as a ''guaranteed freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Before the constitutional amendment becomes official, it must receive approval by three-fifths of all lawmakers in a special meeting called a congress, which is planned for Monday and considered by many to be a rubber stamp, since both houses have already overwhelmingly supported the bill.\par \par \uc2 While many French politicians consider the move natural for the country that produced the universal rights of man, they also conceded that the trigger came from across the ocean, with the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Within weeks, many proposed bills were presented to entrench \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in France so that it would not be repealed by a future government seeking to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It is always too late, if we wait until a right is threatened to protect it,'' Justice Minister \u201\'00\'C9ric Dupond-Moretti told the senators. He added, ''The freedom of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not like the others because it allows people to decide their future. For democracy to control its destiny, women must be allowed to control theirs.''\par \par \uc2 Instead of declaring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a right, the change would declare \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a ''guaranteed freedom'' overseen by Parliament's laws.\par \par \uc2 In a long debate, it was apparent that senators were cognizant they were making history with the vote and sending a signal to the world.\par \par \uc2 M\u233\'00\'E9lanie Vogel, a senator from the Green Party who was a major force behind the bill, said the decision would ''unambiguously state that the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a sub-right, but a fundamental right. It is a condition of freedom in free and equal societies.''\par \par \uc2 ''Back-street abortionists, coat hangers, knitting needles -- never again,'' she added during the debate. ''Let us say to our daughters, to our nieces, to our granddaughters: You are today and henceforth free to choose your lives, forever.''\par \par \uc2 Few of the 50 senators who opposed the move offered anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  arguments, which are not common in France. Most of the pushback focused on a sense that the change was not necessary since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are not threatened in the country, and that the change might introduce a hierarchy of freedoms in the Constitution. Critics of the measure also argued it would do little to improve access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Frenchwomen who live in medical deserts.\par \par \uc2 ''It's enshrining a symbol in the Constitution,'' said Muriel Jourda, a senator with the conservative party, the Republicans. ''Is it the Constitution's role to send messages to the rest of humanity? Personally, I don't think so.''\par \par \uc2 Though the legislation is much weaker than many earlier bills, feminists and lawmakers still applauded the move.\par \par \uc2 ''Our grandchildren will never have to fight to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Sarah Durocher, national co-president of Le Planning Familial, a French equivalent of Planned Parenthood. ''We want to make this an echo for feminists all around the world. We needed a victory.''\par \par \uc2 France decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1975, when Simone Veil, a Holocaust survivor and health minister, pushed forward a bill centered mostly on public health concerns and not women's rights over their bodies, said Bibia Pavard, a historian who co-wrote a book on the Veil law.\par \par \uc2 After the bill became law, it meshed with the feminist movement, and Ms. Veil emerged as a national hero and feminist icon. But because of opposition within Ms. Veil's own conservative party, the original law was quite restrictive.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Parliament has continually voted to extend and expand the scope of the law, to the point that it is considered among the most supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Europe: It allows fully funded abortions for women and minors up to the 14th week of pregnancy, with no waiting period or required rationale.\par \par \uc2 Later abortions are permitted if the pregnancy is deemed a risk to the woman's physical or psychological health, or if the fetus presents certain anomalies.\par \par \uc2 Since 2001, one in four pregnancies on average are terminated by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in France, according to a 2020 parliamentary report.\par \par \uc2 Unlike in the United States, where the impassioned debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has saturated politics, the courts and personal relationships, in France, the issue is largely considered settled and is not a political flashpoint. There have been no effective political efforts to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country over the past half-century, and most French people support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Protests opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  draw relatively few people.\par \par \uc2 An opinion poll conducted in late 2022 found that 86 percent of respondents favored the ''constitutionalization'' of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While French legislators have proposed writing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the Constitution before, the overturning of Roe v. Wade has supercharged the efforts.\par \par \uc2 In November, French lawmakers in the lower house, the National Assembly, backed a proposal to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Constitution, calling it a right. The right-leaning Senate later amended the bill, replacing the term ''right'' with a woman's ''freedom'' to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The latest version of the bill, presented by the government as a compromise, was passed again by the National Assembly in January.\par \par \uc2 The concerns provoked by the Roe v. Wade decision have led to a drumbeat of French petitions, open letters in the newspapers and pressure campaigns on politicians from constituents, including from their own family members, to pass the bill.\par \par \uc2 ''The rights of women are reversible -- you are never sure to have really won,'' said Genevi\u232\'00\'E8ve Fraisse, a French feminist philosopher. ''The proof is in the United States.''\par \par \uc2 ''The ownership of your body, that must be a right,'' she said in an interview, noting that she was wary of the word ''freedom.'' ''Article 4 of the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen says my freedom ends where another person's begins. There's the question of the other -- does that include what is in a woman's womb?''\par \par \uc2 The term ''guaranteed freedom'' is new to the French Constitution, and its meaning unclear.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not something common in French constitutional semantics,'' said St\u233\'00\'E9phanie Hennette-Vauchez, a professor of public law at the University of Paris-Nanterre who has worked with lawmakers on many different versions of the bill. ''It's hard to say what it means from a technical standpoint, although you can understand politically what it means,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 The big question, she said, is how France's Constitutional Council, a top body charged with ensuring laws conform to the Constitution, would interpret the term ''guaranteed freedom'' when examining new legislation reducing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In that way, the change might offer a false sense of security, she said.\par \par \uc2 Still, compared to the debate in the United States, the French legislation ''does something pretty major,'' Ms. Hennette-Vauchez said, contrasting it to the Roe v. Wade decision. ''This could not happen in France, once you put 'guaranteed freedom' in the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 A small group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists gathered on Wednesday to protest the vote. Many covered their mouths with red-and-white cloth.\par \par \uc2 ''We are gagged like those unborn children,'' said Marie-Lys Pellissier, head of communication of the March for Life, an annual demonstration.\par \par \uc2 But they were a small minority.\par \par \uc2 ''Long live \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' shouted a woman passing on her bike, with a toddler on the back.\par \par \uc2 S\u233\'00\'E9gol\u232\'00\'E8ne Le Stradic contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 S\u233\'00\'E9gol\u232\'00\'E8ne Le Stradic contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Senator M\u233\'00\'E9lanie Vogel, a major force behind the amendment. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CHRISTOPHE PETIT TESSON/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) This article appeared in print on page A6. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240229ek2t0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260