\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Florida Pauses Bill to Protect 'Unborn Child'\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers in Florida sidelined a bill this week that would allow civil lawsuits over the wrongful death of a fetus.\par \par \uc2 Those on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate attributed the pause to fallout from the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2 's ruling that frozen embryos should be considered children.\par \par \uc2 If it moves ahead, the bill would add Florida to the ranks of about a dozen other states that allow parents to receive financial damages in some instances when a fetus has died. The bill says in cases of wrongful death, parents of an ''unborn child'' are considered survivors who can sue in civil court.\par \par \uc2 But in recent weeks, Democrats and others warned that the bill amounts to ''fetal personhood,'' assigning full rights of a person to a fetus. Such a designation, they said, would imperil doctors and anyone who assisted women in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and would also adversely affect fertility treatments.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Republican legislative leaders in Florida announced that they had postponed the bill.\par \par \uc2 ''Although I have worked diligently to respond to questions and concerns, I understand there is still work that needs to be done,'' Senator Erin Grall, a Republican from Vero Beach and the bill's sponsor, said in a statement. ''It is important we get the policy right with an issue of this significance.''\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: I.V.F. ruling presents new challenges for Republicans\par \par \uc2 Last week's ruling in Alabama created a political storm for Republicans who were already battling internal divisions and public opinion over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Alabama ruling prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents like former President Donald J. Trump and several Republican governors to underscore their support for I.V.F.\par \par \uc2 Though the Florida bill does not mention I.V.F., critics feared that it could affect fertility treatments and make it harder for families to have children.\par \par \uc2 ''It's fair to assume that I.V.F. was a problem for this bill from the jump,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor and historian at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who used to teach at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2 . ''But the degree of backlash and concern increased significantly after the ruling. It's kind of like it turned into a firestorm.''\par \par \uc2 Background: Fetal personhood is a goal for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists\par \par \uc2 With the overturning of Roe v. Wade by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have been able to devote more attention to another major goal: fetal personhood.\par \par \uc2 The Florida bill didn't use the term ''personhood." Nor did it mention the word ''doctor.'' But opponents criticized the bill for being a de facto personhood bill. The bill's sponsors said an unborn child is a ''member of the species Homo sapiens, at any stage of development, who is carried in the womb.''\par \par \uc2 On Monday, after the bill was postponed, Florida Voice for the Unborn, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, praised Ms. Grall for pulling what they said was a flawed bill, because the definition should apply to ''those unborn children frozen as embryos.'' The group also said that it expected the bill to reintroduced, and passed, in 2025.\par \par \uc2 What Happens Next\par \par \uc2 With just two weeks left in the legislative calendar, the postponement suggested that the bill's chances of passage were slim for this session.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lauren Book of Plantation, the Democratic minority leader, wrote on X, the social media platform formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , that ''thankfully, it appears personhood will not be the law of the land in our state.''\par \par \uc2 Floridians are awaiting a ruling from the State Supreme Court on the constitutionality of the state law banning most abortions after 15 weeks. Separately, the state more recently passed a law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. If the court affirms the 15-week ban, the more recent six-week ban would be allowed to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Residents are also waiting to see whether they will vote on a ballot measure in November that would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state's Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court is weighing a challenge from opponents of the ballot initiative, who say that the language is too vague. But the court's conservative justices, who have until April 1 to rule on the challenge, seem reluctant to block the ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 The ballot measure would require more than 60 percent support from voters to pass. According to a statewide poll conducted by the \uc2 University of North Florida\uc2  in November, 62 percent of registered voters said they favored the amendment, including 53 percent of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240229ek2t0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
