\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  Opened the Door to Reproductive Hell\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 1,128 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Despite the lofty and expansive rhetoric of his majority opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  insisted throughout the text that the actual decision was more modest than it might appear. The end of Roe, he said, was not the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as much as it was the beginning of a new era of democratic deliberation and decision making. No longer shackled by a prior dictate of the Supreme Court, the people were free to choose. ''It is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the people's elected representatives,'' Alito wrote.\par \par \uc2 But, as the legal scholars Melissa Murray and Kate Shaw (who is also a contributing Opinion writer) argue in a recent article in The Harvard Law Review, it is difficult to square Alito and the Dobbs majority's paeans to democracy with their pointed hostility to both voting rights and equal representation. ''Viewing the Roberts court's many interventions in this sphere in tandem,'' they write, ''it is clear that this is a court that no longer understands itself as largely or primarily functioning to facilitate the exercise of meaningful democracy in these ways; rather, in many instances, it appears to be actively working to undermine these goals.''\par \par \uc2 There was more at work in the Dobbs opinion than the majority's disingenuous concern for democratic participation. Alito and his conservative colleagues did not just open the door to new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions; they took aim at broader rights to bodily autonomy and personal freedom while laying the groundwork for the divisive notion of fetal personhood -- an idea that, for all the court's talk of democracy, is fundamentally incompatible with any modern notion of equal citizenship.\par \par \uc2 As Murray and Shaw observe, ''The court's repeated references to 'fetal life,' 'potential life' and 'unborn human being[s]' may have been designed'' to ''broadcast receptivity to such claims to litigants and lower courts.'' What's more, some courts have already ''eagerly embraced this fetus-forward posture.''\par \par \uc2 One of those courts, it appears, is the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2 , which ruled last week that frozen embryos in fertility clinics were ''extrauterine children'' subject to an 1872 state law allowing parents to sue over the wrongful death of a minor. ''Even before birth, all human beings bear the image of God, and their lives cannot be destroyed without effacing his glory,'' Chief Justice Tom Parker wrote in a concurring opinion, in which he also quoted directly from the Book of Jeremiah.\par \par \uc2 The Alabama court's ruling rests on a broad interpretation of the state law in question. As Justice Greg Cook wrote in his dissent from the 8-to-1 decision, ''I dissent because the main opinion violates this fundamental principle -- that is, that the legislative branch and not the judicial branch updates laws -- by expanding the meaning of the Wrongful Death Act beyond what it meant in 1872 without an amendment by the legislature.''\par \par \uc2 It should be said here that the majority's decision was possible only because of Dobbs, since to free states to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also to free them to touch an even larger set of rights and freedoms.\par \par \uc2 That's all the more true because the goal of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement was not to return the question to the states but to outlaw the practice, as well as roll the clock back on reproductive freedom writ large. Within a week of the Supreme Court's 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade, for example, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  congressman, Representative Lawrence Hogan of Maryland, proposed a ''human life'' amendment that would extend the rights of personhood to the fetus. More recently, in the immediate aftermath of the court's decision in Dobbs, conservative lawmakers across the country began to introduce laws and state constitutional amendments that would establish fetal personhood, rendering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  permanently illegal with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 As we're now seeing in Alabama, the concept of fetal personhood does more than outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; it effectively outlaws in vitro fertilization as well. The University of Alabama at Birmingham Health System, for example, has now paused its I.V.F. treatments in response to the State Supreme Court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 Fetal personhood also touches contraception, potentially outlawing those forms of hormonal birth control that prevent the implantation of a fertilized egg into the uterine lining. (Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists already describe those medications as ''abortifacients'' despite the fact that preventing a pregnancy isn't an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .) It's practically inescapable that a standard that ties personhood to the moment of conception -- the creation of a single-celled zygote -- is a standard that may well make it illegal to take any form of birth control that alters the hormonal balance of the patient to reduce the odds of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Added up, the main effect of fetal personhood is to rob women of their right to control their own reproductive capacity and make a choice about when and whether to give birth. It subordinates the actual personhood of a woman or a teenager -- as captured in her ability to think and reason and act of her own volition and for her own purposes -- to the potential for personhood found in an embryo. It is, in effect, a profound attack on the dignity and equality of women. Proponents of fetal personhood may speak in the language of rights, but this particular right is freedom retracting, not freedom enhancing.\par \par \uc2 You cannot disentangle \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . You cannot disentangle \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  from bodily autonomy. And you cannot disentangle bodily autonomy from basic questions of equal rights and democratic freedom.\par \par \uc2 It is not a coincidence that the lawmakers spearheading the assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also the lawmakers spearheading the assault on other forms of bodily autonomy -- like the right of transgender Americans to exist in public as themselves. They are also the same lawmakers waging a broader campaign to restrict the ability of people in their states to live and think as they please.\par \par \uc2 Alito wanted the public to believe that he was striking a blow for democracy when he led the Supreme Court's conservative majority to overturn Roe v. Wade. But the truth is that the path away from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is less a move toward greater freedom than it is a step toward its negation.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240226ek2q0002