\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris Focuses On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  For Primary In Key State\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Nehamas \par \uc2 1,081 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 During a quiet visit before the state's primary, the vice president spoke to a small group about a subject that is far more comfortable for Democrats than the Mideast war.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has emerged as a leading Democratic defender of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a potent campaign issue for her party, and on Thursday she brought her message to Michigan before the state's presidential primary next week -- her third trip so far this year to a battleground state to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During a stop in Grand Rapids, Ms. Harris assailed Republicans who support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban as ''extremists,'' and said state bans that do not contain exceptions for sexual assault or incest were ''immoral.''\par \par \uc2 ''People have been suffering and we must be explicit about what that is, because this is not a hypothetical point,'' Ms. Harris said at a round-table event at the Fountain Street Church, a progressive house of worship with \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and rainbow flags hanging outside its entrance. ''Women have been having miscarriages in toilets in our country, have been denied access to emergency care because of what has been happening.''\par \par \uc2 But the gathering, which was closed to the public, also reflected the challenges facing President Biden and Ms. Harris in a difficult re-election year. It featured a group of just nine participants in addition to Ms. Harris, including Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2 , both Biden allies.\par \par \uc2 Quiet as it was, the event showed how Mr. Biden and Ms. Harris are trying to keep voters' focus on a far more comfortable subject for Democrats than the one hanging over Michigan's primary on Tuesday: the Israel-Gaza war.\par \par \uc2 The state's large population of Arab Americans, as well as its progressive voters, have expressed deep anger at Mr. Biden over his support for Israel. Its bombardment and invasion of Gaza after the Oct. 7 terrorist attack by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which killed 1,200 people, have led to a death toll nearing 30,000 Palestinians, according to local authorities.\par \par \uc2 Some prominent Democrats in the state have urged voters to protest Mr. Biden's Gaza policy by marking ''uncommitted'' on their ballots rather than voting for him.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris is seen as more critical of Israel than the president, yet her trip to Grand Rapids took her far away from Dearborn and other Detroit suburbs where many of Michigan's Arab American voters live. Even so, about a dozen protesters stood down the block from Ms. Harris's event holding signs that said ''Free Gaza'' and ''Ceasefire Now.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has been dogged by Gaza protesters on many of his trips across the country, and they have frequently interrupted his events. On Thursday, they marched through a hotel in San Francisco where he was staying for a California fund-raising swing and chanted: ''Biden, Biden, you can't hide, we charge you with genocide.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden visited Michigan early this month, but he is not expected to hold a get-out-the-vote event in the state in the days leading up to its primary, as he did shortly before contests in South Carolina and Nevada. Instead, Ms. Harris made the trip on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The vice president may be a more natural messenger on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Mr. Biden, an 81-year-old practicing Catholic. Mr. Biden has sometimes expressed discomfort in talking about the issue, even as it has helped power Democrats to unexpected victories since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 At a fund-raiser this month, he used the language of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement -- saying that he opposed ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand'' -- while expressing support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The comment was noted with concern by some progressives.\par \par \uc2 Lavora Barnes, the chair of the Michigan Democratic Party, said that Ms. Harris was a ''terrific messenger'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''On this particular issue, she's a woman, the first woman vice president,'' Ms. Barnes said. ''It's the perfect conversation for her to be having with voters.''\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the likely Republican nominee, has privately said that he supports a 16-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with exceptions in cases of rape or incest, or to save the life of the mother. His appointees to the Supreme Court helped overturn Roe, giving states led by Republicans the ability to impose strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Since the start of the year, Ms. Harris has held events focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two other battleground states, Georgia and Wisconsin, as part of a nationwide Fight for Reproductive Freedoms tour. At both of those previous appearances, unlike the one in Michigan, she spoke in front of public crowds.\par \par \uc2 The trips reflect Ms. Harris's attempt to play a more prominent role both in the administration and in the re-election campaign, after early struggles to define her role as vice president.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris just returned from a high-profile visit to Munich, where she reassured European allies about the United States' commitment to \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Mr. Trump recently issued a stark threat to the alliance, and Republican members of the House have also criticized it.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a centerpiece of his re-election bid. In January, he headlined a rally in Virginia calling for the restoration of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. And on Thursday, he denounced a recent ruling by the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  that frozen embryos in test tubes should be considered children.\par \par \uc2 ''The disregard for women's ability to make these decisions for themselves and their families is outrageous and unacceptable,'' Mr. Biden said in a statement. ''Make no mistake: This is a direct result of the overturning of Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 He added that he and Ms. Harris would not stop ''until we restore the protections of Roe v. Wade in federal law for all women in every state.''\par \par \uc2 (It will be difficult for Democrats or Republicans to pass federal laws either protecting or restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without 60 votes in the Senate, which is unlikely.)\par \par \uc2 In Grand Rapids, Ms. Harris laid the blame for the Alabama decision and the overturning of Roe squarely on Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 The former president, she said, ''openly talks about how he is proud of what has resulted.''\par \par \uc2 She also accused him of taking pride ''in the fact that so many young women in America now have fewer rights than their mothers and grandmothers.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240223ek2n0004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
