\par\par \uc2 CHARLES M. BLOW\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Embryo Ruling Shows Our Slide Toward Theocracy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charles M. Blow \par \uc2 948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If you don't think this country is sliding toward theocracy, you're not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 The drumbeat of incidents moving us ever closer to the seemingly inescapable future is so steady and frequent that we've developed outrage fatigue -- we've grown numb.\par \par \uc2 For instance, on Tuesday, the \uc2 Alabama Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that frozen embryos are children, and that destruction of those embryos, even by accident, is subject to the state's Wrongful Death of a Minor Act. In his concurring opinion, the chief justice of the court, Tom Parker, wrote, ''Even before birth, all human beings bear the image of God, and their lives cannot be destroyed without effacing his glory.''\par \par \uc2 The ruling could mean less access to reproductive care in Alabama if specialists in the field of in vitro fertilization simply choose to practice in states that don't threaten their efforts.\par \par \uc2 There have been cases before in which embryos were destroyed as a result of negligence, but the Alabama decision significantly ups the ante. It essentially turns cryopreservation tanks into frozen nurseries.\par \par \uc2 The idea is absurd and unscientific. It is instead tied to a religious crusade to downgrade the personhood of women by conferring personhood on frozen embryos.\par \par \uc2 I called Sean Tipton, the chief advocacy and policy officer at the \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine\uc2 , who told me: ''One of the points in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate is, 'Is it really about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or is it about controlling women and controlling sex?' And this clearly exposes the idea that it's not just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' He said, ''There is no more pro-life medical treatment available, ever, than in vitro fertilization, and this decision clearly threatens the ability for that to continue.''\par \par \uc2 Control of women's bodies is the endgame. And some religious conservatives won't stop until that goal is achieved. For that reason, intervening victories -- like the overturning of Roe v. Wade -- will never be seen as enough; they will only intensify a blinding sense of righteousness.\par \par \uc2 There is an array of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases percolating around the country that could make their way to the Supreme Court -- the same court that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brags about transforming, having appointed a third of its justices. The legal and political battles over these issues are far from over, and the preservation of women's remaining rights is far from certain.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that seems to be temporarily stopping congressional Republicans from pushing for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban -- after years of arguing that their goal was merely to allow individual states to make their own laws -- is that the issue of reproductive choice is an electoral loser for their party.\par \par \uc2 But now Trump is reportedly talking privately about supporting a national 16-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with some exceptions.\par \par \uc2 This is what many of his supporters want, and many of them believe he has been singularly chosen by God to advance their theocratic aims. It's one of the reasons that they overlook Trump's glaring flaws and the fact that Trump himself is not a particularly religious man.\par \par \uc2 It's worth noting that many of the right's efforts, including on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are led by men who want births but can't give birth, reflecting an imbalance between power and expectation that may carry over to a younger generation. A fascinating new report from \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  found that although men and women 18 to 34 ''are about equally likely to say they want to get married,'' 57 percent of young men say they want children one day, compared to just 45 percent of young women.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just one front on which this religious fight is being waged. As of last week, the A.C.L.U. was tracking 437 anti-L.G.B.T.Q. bills being considered by state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Then there's the alarming effort by conservative groups to transform and reshape the federal government in ways that curtail American freedoms, but also, according to Politico, to bring Christian nationalist ideas into a second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 To those advancing these ideas, the will of God counts more than the will of the American people, even when Americans object or disagree.\par \par \uc2 Reportedly, one idea among the various proposals is invoking the Insurrection Act on Trump's first day back in office to facilitate deployment of the military against protesters.\par \par \uc2 We are perilously close to all this becoming a reality, potentially aided and abetted by disaffected Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about many Democrats with single-issue objections to President Biden -- whether it's opposition to his position on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war, disappointments about the overall state of the economy or concerns about the president's age -- who haven't committed to supporting his re-election, who don't seem to see that in November the country faces one of the most existential electoral decisions it ever has faced.\par \par \uc2 If these Democrats decide to punish Biden by sitting it out, they could wind up performing one of the greatest acts of self-immolation in recent political history: abandoning an administration committed to the protection of democracy and possibly allowing the ascension of a theocracy intent on destroying the very freedoms that progressives cherish.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240222ek2m0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
