\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump's Allies Plan to Bolster  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 2,274 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 His supporters are seeking to attack \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access from a variety of angles should he regain the White House, including using a long-dormant law from 1873.\par \par \uc2 Allies of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and officials who served in his administration are planning ways to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights if he returns to power that would go far beyond proposals for a national ban or the laws enacted in conservative states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Behind the scenes, specific anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans being proposed by Mr. Trump's allies are sweeping and legally sophisticated. Some of their proposals would rely on enforcing the Comstock Act, a long-dormant law from 1873, to criminalize the shipping of any materials used in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which account for the majority of abortions in America.\par \par \uc2 ''We don't need a federal ban when we have Comstock on the books,'' said Jonathan F. Mitchell, the legal force behind a 2021 Texas law that found a way to effectively ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state before Roe v. Wade was overturned. ''There's a smorgasbord of options.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mitchell, who represented Mr. Trump in arguments before the Supreme Court over whether the former president could appear on the ballot in Colorado, indicated that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists had purposefully been quiet about their more advanced plans, given the political liability the issue has become for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''I hope he doesn't know about the existence of Comstock, because I just don't want him to shoot off his mouth,'' Mr. Mitchell said of Mr. Trump. ''I think the pro-life groups should keep their mouths shut as much as possible until the election.''\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported on Friday that Mr. Trump had told advisers and allies that he liked the idea of a 16-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban but that he wanted to wait until the Republican primary contest was over to publicly discuss his views.\par \par \uc2 But among the people thinking most seriously about actual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should he win the election, very different plans are underway.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's idea is not yet a concrete proposal, and the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers and strategists within his own orbit already have plans in the works that toughen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies using other avenues. They are not waiting for Mr. Trump to pursue turning his discussions about what he ultimately will say about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the G.O.P. primary into reality, especially because they know a 16-week ban is all but certain to never pass Congress and become law. Instead, they are working much faster, and much more sharply, to exceed their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  successes in the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 In their view, their plans seem more achievable and more far-reaching than a ban like Mr. Trump floated, which might have a political impact but not as much of a material one. A 16-week ban would affect only a small fraction of abortions, given that nearly 94 percent happen in the first trimester, before 13 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 It is easier to think of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in terms of a single ban, but the reality is more complex: \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is filled with intricate regulatory details and plays out in the far reaches of the federal bureaucracy, with a host of officials well beyond the president. Mr. Trump is personally disengaged from these efforts and considers any discussions of more hard-line policy to be politically inconvenient. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists are nevertheless putting themselves in position to steer actual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy as they did in his first term.\par \par \uc2 In policy documents, private conversations and interviews, the plans described by former Trump administration officials, allies and supporters propose circumventing Congress and leveraging the regulatory powers of federal institutions, including the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , the Food and Drug Administration, the Department of Justice and the National Institutes of Health.\par \par \uc2 The effect would be to create a second Trump administration that would attack \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access from a variety of angles and could be stopped only by courts that the first Trump administration had already stacked with conservative judges.\par \par \uc2 ''He had the most pro-life administration in history and adopted the most pro-life policies of any administration in history,'' said \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , a leader of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts in Health and Human Services during the Trump administration. ''That track record is the best evidence, I think, you could have of what a second term might look like if Trump wins.''\par \par \uc2 Policies under consideration include banning the use of fetal stem cells in medical research for diseases like cancer, rescinding approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at the F.D.A. and stopping hundreds of millions in federal funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Such an action against \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  would cripple the nation's largest provider of women's health care, which is already struggling to provide abortions in the post-Roe era.\par \par \uc2 The organizations and advocates crafting these proposals are not simply outside groups expressing wish lists of what they hope Mr. Trump would do in a second administration. They are people who have spent much of their professional careers fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including some who were in powerful positions during Mr. Trump's administration.\par \par \uc2 In his first term, Mr. Trump largely outsourced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to socially conservative lawyers and aides. Since he left office, some of those people have remained in Mr. Trump's orbit, defending him in court, suggesting policy plans well beyond issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and attending events at Mar-a-Lago, his private club and residence in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Frank Pavone, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist whom Pope Francis removed from the priesthood for ''blasphemous communication,'' said he had discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at several poolside receptions at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 ''When I'm there at Mar-a-Lago,'' he said, ''I get strong affirmation from everyone I meet there for my work.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has not publicly addressed the extensive list of possible anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive actions or the enforcement of the Comstock Act. Yet, Mr. Trump's official blessing may not matter if his former aides and their networks are returned to key positions in the federal bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 ''The question will then become what can be done unilaterally at the executive branch level, and the answer is quite a bit,'' Mr. Mitchell said. ''But to the extent to which that's done will depend on whether the president wants to take the political heat and whether the attorney general or the secretary of \uc2 Health and Human Services\uc2  are on board.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are enmeshed throughout the ecosystem of organizations that are suggesting policies for the next conservative administration. \uc2 Russell T. Vought\uc2 , a former senior Trump administration official who ran the Office of Management and Budget, is celebrated by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement for successfully blocking funds for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  during the Trump administration. He now runs a think tank with close ties to the former president that has backed arguments in a Supreme Court case attempting to undo the 2000 approval of mifepristone, a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 Some activists and former aides have tried to downplay their plans. Speaking at a church in Gallup, N.M., last spring, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists rallied the crowd to support a local ordinance that would require compliance with the Comstock Act but referred to the law solely by its statute number, 18 U.S.C. 1461 and 1462.\par \par \uc2 In a plan released by a coalition that has been drawing up America First-style policy plans, nicknamed Project 2025, the Comstock Act is also referred to only by the statute number.\par \par \uc2 ''Following the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs, there is now no federal prohibition on the enforcement of this statute,'' the plan states. ''The Department of Justice in the next conservative Administration should therefore announce its intent to enforce federal law against providers and distributors of such pills.''\par \par \uc2 The plan also cites the statute number in a footnote justifying its recommendation that the F.D.A. stop ''promoting or approving mail-order abortions in violation of longstanding federal laws that prohibit the mailing and interstate carriage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs.''\par \par \uc2 Students for Life, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, is not actively pushing Mr. Trump for a gestational ban, at any number of weeks. The group is instead focused on executive actions and changing policies though federal agencies, which they view as both more effective and more politically achievable. ''This is probably the first election where D.O.J., H.H.S., F.D.A. are big-ticket items,'' said Kristi Hamrick, a strategist for the group.\par \par \uc2 When a donor in Ohio recently expressed concern that Mr. Trump personally did not care about ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , the president of Students for Life, offered reassurance. ''We haven't come across a campaign staffer yet who doesn't share our values,'' she said of Mr. Trump's campaign.\par \par \uc2 Some allies think a second Trump administration could move even faster than before to advance anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures because Roe is no longer a roadblock.\par \par \uc2 As president, Mr. Trump in 2019 announced a 440-page rule that strengthened ''conscience protections'' for health care workers who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on religious grounds. The measure allowed medical providers to refuse care if it conflicted with their personal beliefs, and it took over a year to put in place. But at the time, Mr. Severino said, H.H.S. had to consider comments against the rule noting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a constitutional right under Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''Those arguments are now gone,'' Mr. Severino said. ''You cannot say that it is a federal constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so that would simplify the rule-making process significantly.''\par \par \uc2 Similarly, limits to fetal tissue research could also come much more quickly. ''It took longer than necessary to get a resolution on that,'' he said. ''The vetting and the testing and the argumentation has been done already once before.''\par \par \uc2 Polling indicates that plans banning or severely restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would most likely be deeply unpopular. Since Roe fell, support for legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained support. Only about 8 percent of American adults oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Biden administration officials say they have reached the limits of their powers to restore federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. They have pushed Congress to pass legislation that would restore federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but the legislation has repeatedly failed to garner enough support in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, Republicans have been trying to enact a federal ban on abortions after 20 weeks. That legislation, too, has failed to gain enough traction to pass.\par \par \uc2 ''Congress isn't going to pass a ban, but the Comstock Act is already on the books,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor and a historian of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 . ''As interpreted in this way, it doesn't have any exceptions -- it applies at conception. It's any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , full stop.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ziegler said such an action would certainly face litigation from liberal groups and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers that could end up before the country's highest court.\par \par \uc2 Even the advocates are uncertain how far the courts and the public will allow them to go. Some groups have argued for immediate enforcement of Comstock. Others are more cautious about how to enforce it in a politically palatable way. Mr. Mitchell said he believed the enforcement of Comstock would have to ensure provisions to protect the life of a pregnant woman and to address how to care for miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 The Comstock Act made it a federal crime to send or deliver ''obscene, lewd or lascivious'' material through the mail or by other carriers, specifically including items used for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or birth control. The 1973 ruling in Roe, which recognized a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , largely relegated the law to constitutional history.\par \par \uc2 Beyond reactivating the Comstock Act, conservatives believe they can roll back much of what the Biden administration has done to try to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. One example is a plan to eliminate guidance from the Biden administration requiring federally funded hospitals to perform lifesaving abortions, even in the 16 states with near-total bans. They also float ideas about how the Justice Department could direct U.S. attorneys not to prosecute people who violate laws prohibiting the obstruction of clinic entrances.\par \par \uc2 Republican gains in the courts could help lock in their goals. Many executive actions are undone or redone when a new administration takes power. But former officials, including Mr. Severino, are hopeful that the Supreme Court will rule soon to eliminate the \uc2 Chevron\uc2  deference, which he said could allow regulations they enact to remain in place even if a Democratic president were elected in the future.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights leaders have little doubt that a second Trump administration would go as far as possible to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access. While their organizations are publicly hammering Republicans for embracing national bans, they quietly worry more about the damage Mr. Trump could materially do to their cause through executive actions.\par \par \uc2 ''He's trying to masquerade in public as a moderate,'' said \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\uc2 , president of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , formerly \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . ''It's mind-blowing that anyone would imagine he wouldn't do worse in a second term.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''He's going to do whatever Jonathan Mitchell wants.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  activists at the annual March for Life event in Washington last month. The Comstock Act could be enforced to fight abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) This article appeared in print on page A1, A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240218ek2i0005i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
