\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Is Said To Favor Limit For Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Haberman, Jonathan Swan and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In supporting a 16-week ban with exceptions, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears to be trying to satisfy social conservatives who want to further restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and voters who want more modest limits.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald J. Trump has told advisers and allies that he likes the idea of a 16-week national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with three exceptions, in cases of rape or incest, or to save the life of the mother, according to two people with direct knowledge of Mr. Trump's deliberations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has studiously avoided taking a clear position on restrictions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe v. Wade was overturned in the middle of 2022, galvanizing Democrats ahead of the midterm elections that year. He has said in private that he wants to wait until the Republican presidential primary contest is over to publicly discuss his views, because he doesn't want to risk alienating social conservatives before he has secured the nomination, the two people said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has approached \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactionally since becoming a candidate in 2015, and his current private discussions reflect that same approach.\par \par \uc2 One thing Mr. Trump likes about a 16-week federal ban on abortions is that it's a round number. ''Know what I like about 16?'' Mr. Trump told one of these people, who was given anonymity to describe a private conversation. ''It's even. It's four months.''\par \par \uc2 When discussing prospective vice-presidential candidates, Mr. Trump often asks whether they are ''OK on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' He is instantly dismissive when he hears that a Republican doesn't support ''the three exceptions.'' He tells advisers that Republicans will keep losing elections with that position.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump is hoping to vanquish his remaining primary rival, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , in the South Carolina Republican primary on Feb. 24. The state has restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that took effect in 2023 essentially banning the procedure after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June 2022, Mr. Trump told advisers that he believed the decision was going to be harmful to Republicans. Since then, he has formed the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is overwhelmingly responsible for a string of Republican losses in congressional races.\par \par \uc2 And he is acutely aware of his own vulnerability: He appointed the three justices who enabled that decision, a fact he has publicly claimed credit for in several settings. Those statements have already been included in ads, and Democrats plan to spend hundreds of millions of dollars to remind voters of that fact.\par \par \uc2 In backing a 16-week ban, Mr. Trump would be trying to satisfy both social conservatives who want to further restrict access to abortions and Republican and independent voters who want more modest limits on the procedure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently banned at various stages of pregnancy before 16 weeks in 20 states, including Mr. Trump's home state of Florida. The type of ban that Mr. Trump has discussed privately would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the remaining 30 states where it is legal beyond that point. And the question of exceptions limited to the life of the mother is also controversial. In Texas, state courts have ruled that women did not qualify for the limited exceptions for ''life-threatening conditions'' related to pregnancy, even in cases where their fetus faced a severe diagnosis and the woman's future fertility and health were jeopardized.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Karoline Leavitt, a spokeswoman for Mr. Trump, did not address his private remarks.\par \par \uc2 ''As President Trump has stated, he would sit down with both sides and negotiate a deal that everyone will be happy with,'' Ms. Leavitt said, adding that he ''appointed strong Constitutionalist federal judges and Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe v. Wade and sent the decision back to the states, which others have tried to do for over 50 years.'' She attacked President Biden and congressional Democrats as overly permissive of abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, who described himself as supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for most of his adult life, announced in early 2011 as he considered running for president in the Republican primary that he now was anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Trump never appeared comfortable discussing it. In early 2016, in an interview with the television host Chris Matthews, Mr. Trump said there needed to be ''some form of punishment'' for women who had illegal abortions, a comment his campaign quickly walked back.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Mr. Trump had to convince skeptical social conservatives that he would implement anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and pick socially conservative justices, and he selected a deeply conservative vice president in \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to help with the persuasion effort.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Mr. Trump has delivered on that and has formed a powerful connection of his own with evangelical voters, so he has felt less of a need to pander to them. After Roe was overturned, Republicans have struggled to find ways to talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now that they can no longer simply say they oppose it. The concept of a national ban of some sort has become a focus, with a 15-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban emerging as the baseline many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have set for Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 A 16-week ban would not end many abortions: nearly 94 percent of abortions happen before 13 weeks in pregnancy, according to data collected by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 . Nor is such a ban grounded in medical research. Even 15 weeks falls before the point when significant screens take place in a pregnancy to examine the fetus for rare -- but potentially fatal -- conditions. Instead, it has become a position that some Republicans, based on polling, believe will be the most politically palatable to voters.\par \par \uc2 An AP/NORC poll released in July 2023, a year after Roe was overturned, showed a slim majority disapprove of a ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy. In the survey, Democrats mostly supported such a measure and Republicans mostly opposed it. A six-week ban polled poorly among a majority of Americans, including Republicans, while a majority of Americans didn't support allowing abortions up to 24 weeks of pregnancy, according to the survey.\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Trump's allies, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, introduced legislation in 2022 calling for a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother after that window closes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump never backed the bill, which other prominent Republicans distanced themselves from, and he said as recently as last fall that the decision should be left up to states to decide. A leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group criticized him for that statement, but its leader was appeased after meeting with Mr. Trump and Mr. Graham.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that embracing any sort of national ban is unpopular with broad swaths of independent voters, and potentially risky for Mr. Trump. For instance, in Virginia, efforts by Gov. \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  to rally voters around what his campaign called a ''15 week limit'' last November failed and Democrats surpassed expectations in the state's legislative elections.\par \par \uc2 So far in this Republican nominating contest, in which primary voters generally reward candidates for opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Mr. Trump has avoided answering the question of whether he'd support a national ban. Instead, he talks about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as if it's a real-estate transaction. He has taken credit for giving ''great negotiating power'' to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists.\par \par \uc2 ''What's going to happen is you're going to come up with a number of weeks or months,'' Mr. Trump said in an interview on NBC's ''Meet the Press'' in September. ''You're going to come up with a number that's going to make people happy.''\par \par \uc2 In a Fox News town-hall event in January, a week before the Iowa caucuses, a socially conservative voter asked Mr. Trump to ''reassure me'' that he would protect ''every person's right to life, without compromise.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump declined to reassure her. ''I love where you are coming from,'' he told the voter. ''But we still have to win elections. And they have used this -- you know, we have some great Republicans and they are great on the issue, and you would love them on the issue. And a lot of them have just been decimated in the election.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump went so far as to criticize the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban signed by his former Republican rival Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida as ''terrible.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis tried to capitalize on the comment in socially conservative Iowa. ''I don't know how you can even make the claim that you're pro-life if you're criticizing states for enacting protections for babies that have heartbeats,'' he told Radio Iowa soon after Mr. Trump made the comments in September. ''I think if he's going into this saying he's going to make the Democrats happy with respect to right to life, I think all pro-lifers should know that he's preparing to sell you out.''\par \par \uc2 But conservative voters gave Mr. Trump a pass and, ultimately, a record-margin victory in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has been encouraged by the lack of blowback and has privately gone even further in blaming more hard-line Republicans for the party's election losses. He has repeatedly criticized two losing 2022 candidates for governor -- Tudor Dixon in Michigan and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania -- for squandering winnable races by being too ''hard-right'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not allowing for sufficient exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has told advisers and allies that he wants to try to turn the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a positive by talking about what he characterizes as the ''radical Democrat'' position of supporting late-term abortions, which are rare, but unpalatable to a significant number of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Igielnik contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240217ek2h0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
