\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Telemedicine  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Services Are as Safe as Visiting a Clinic, Study Finds\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 Taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills prescribed through telemedicine and received by mail -- a method used by growing numbers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients -- is as safe and effective as when the pills are obtained by visiting a doctor, a large new study found. The method was about 98 percent effective and was safe for over 99 percent of patients, the study reported.\par \par \uc2 The research\par \par \uc2 The study, led by researchers at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , looked at the experience of more than 6,000 patients in the months after the federal government began allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be mailed, from April 2021 to January 2022.\par \par \uc2 The patients used one of three telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations -- Hey Jane, \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand or Choix -- that served 20 states and Washington, D.C. The research, published on Thursday in Nature Medicine, ended five months before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, igniting a wave of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions. Since then, more telemedicine services have opened, and are used by many patients who consider the method more convenient, private and affordable than visiting clinics or doctors, especially if they have to travel to another state.\par \par \uc2 The services in the study prescribed pills to patients who were 10 weeks pregnant or less (one service had an eight-week limit) and screened patients for medical issues that would make them ineligible, like ectopic pregnancies or blood-clotting disorders.\par \par \uc2 In most cases, the services' doctors, nurse practitioners, physician assistants and midwives were able to determine eligibility from patients' written or verbal information about their pregnancy and health, without requiring them to have ultrasounds, which are logistically difficult for some patients to obtain. If medical eligibility was unclear, patients were asked to get ultrasounds -- 486 did and were then prescribed pills, comprising about 8 percent of the 6,034 patients who received pills in the study.\par \par \uc2 The results\par \par \uc2 Researchers reviewed the medical records of the services and were able to determine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outcomes for three-fourths, or 4,454, of the patients. A vast majority -- 4,351 patients, or 97.7 percent -- completed abortions with the standard regimen: mifepristone, which stops a pregnancy's development, followed a day or two later by misoprostol, which causes contractions to expel the tissue.\par \par \uc2 Of the remaining patients, 85 needed additional measures to complete the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , usually with additional medication or a suction procedure in a medical facility.\par \par \uc2 Eighty-one patients visited emergency departments, and 15 patients had serious complications. Ten patients were hospitalized. Six received blood transfusions, two were treated for infections and one had surgery for an ectopic pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Six patients turned out to have ectopic pregnancies, which would have made them ineligible for the pills. Studies show that ectopic pregnancies cannot always be identified early, even by ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 Of the patients who visited emergency departments, 38 percent ended up needing no treatment. Patients sometimes visit emergency departments because ''they don't know whether what they're experiencing is normal and they sometimes don't have anyone to ask and they don't want to tell a lot of people about their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Dr. Ushma Upadhyay, a public health scientist at U.C.S.F. and one of the study's authors.\par \par \uc2 No patients were found to be beyond 10 weeks into pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The effectiveness and safety rates were similar to those in several large studies of in-person medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and of telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where ultrasounds were required. They were also similar to the rates on the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's label for mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Researchers also found no difference in safety or efficacy for patients who received real-time video consultations compared to those who received prescriptions based on written information they provided via text messaging, which most patients did.\par \par \uc2 Two patients asked about ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill reversal,'' a nonscientific theory that abortions can be stopped after taking the first drug. Both were told that ''evidence-based reversal treatment does not exist'' and were referred to urgent in-person care, the study reported.\par \par \uc2 Larger implications\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being challenged in a lawsuit filed against the F.D.A. by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents seeking to curtail mifepristone. One of the plaintiffs' claims is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are dangerous. The F.D.A. has cited overwhelming scientific evidence that the pills are safe, and two studies that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents referenced to support their claims were recently retracted by a scientific journal publisher.\par \par \uc2 In August, an appeals court said mifepristone could remain legal, but ordered significant restrictions that would prevent mailing or prescribing it by telemedicine. The Supreme Court will hear arguments in the case next month. The new study results may be mentioned by those urging the court to keep telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  available.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240216ek2g0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
