\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Election to Replace Santos Has Become Tight Race With a Lot at Stake\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Fandos \par \uc2 1,389 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 February 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The special House election in New York pits Mazi Pilip, a Republican county legislator, against \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , a former Democratic congressman.\par \par \uc2 After nearly a year with George Santos as their local congressman, voters in New York finally have their chance to choose his replacement.\par \par \uc2 Early voting has already begun in the closely watched special House election, and the Feb. 13 contest is considered a tossup with unusually high stakes -- for both the immediate balance of power in the House and November's general elections.\par \par \uc2 It pits Mazi Pilip, a little-known Nassau County legislator running as a Republican, against \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , a former Democratic congressman.\par \par \uc2 Here's what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 How we got here\par \par \uc2 Mr. Santos, a Republican, won New York's Third Congressional District in November 2022 amid a wave of Republican success. But it was just weeks before his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 began to unravel based on reporting by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and other news media outlets.\par \par \uc2 His remarkable array of lies included fabricated academic degrees, a nonexistent career on Wall Street and family fortune, and even a collegiate volleyball championship. Federal prosecutors soon added 23 criminal charges accusing him of campaign fraud, credit card fraud and other crimes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Santos survived all of it until last December, when exasperated colleagues -- armed with a damning bipartisan House Ethics Committee report -- voted to make him only the sixth House member expelled from the body. Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York then called for a special election to fill his seat.\par \par \uc2 Meet the candidates\par \par \uc2 Republicans nominated Ms. Pilip, who boasts a remarkable personal story but a relatively thin political r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9. She was born in Ethiopia, served in the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  and became an American citizen in 2009. She won her first and only office on the part-time Nassau County Legislature in 2021, and is still a registered Democrat despite campaigning for the opposing party.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pilip, 44, has promised to crack down on illegal immigration and address the region's painfully high costs of living. But she has run an under-the-radar campaign, making it difficult to pin down her positions on many proposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or on former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats picked Mr. Suozzi, who has a comparatively long record. He was the Nassau County executive for two terms and represented the Third District for three before stepping aside in 2022 for an ill-fated campaign for governor. He spent the intervening year working as a business consultant.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Suozzi, 61, is considered a political moderate. He has called for state Democrats to tighten New York's bail laws and trumpets his time as a leader of the bipartisan House Problem Solvers Caucus. Yet he was also a reliable vote for President Biden and House Democratic leaders.\par \par \uc2 A referendum on immigration (and other issues)\par \par \uc2 The race has become a proxy fight on many of the most important issues dividing Washington, none more so than immigration. Here's where the candidates stand on key policy questions.\par \par \uc2 Immigration: The massive influx of migrants at the southern border and in New York City has helped fuel a wave of voter discontent in the mostly suburban district. Mr. Suozzi's answer has been a bipartisan deal struck in the Senate that would clamp down on asylum claims and close the border if there are too many attempted crossings. Though it includes a Republican wish list, Ms. Pilip slammed the deal as ''the legalization of the invasion of our country.'' She supports a more stringent, partisan alternative.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights: Mr. Suozzi said he would vote to restore federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and has the endorsement of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Ms. Pilip said she opposes a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. But she is personally anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has not stated positions on other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  constraints Republicans have advanced, like one that would bar the Pentagon from covering costs for service members who must travel for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war: Both candidates have sought to project unflinching support for Israel amid its war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Ms. Pilip, an Orthodox Jew, is attempting to win over Jewish Democrats alarmed by anti-Israel sentiment on the left. Mr. Suozzi has argued he would be more valuable as a bulwark against it within his party.\par \par \uc2 Presidential politics: Mr. Suozzi is a longtime ally of President Biden, but with the president's popularity flagging, he has distanced himself. Ms. Pilip's position on Mr. Trump has seemingly flip-flopped: She initially said she would not support him if he was convicted in his pending criminal trials, but recently told CNN that he was a ''great president'' and ''didn't commit any crime'' when he tried to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Santos: The infamous former congressman has been surprisingly absent from the race. Despite campaigning together in 2022, Ms. Pilip has forcefully denounced Mr. Santos. The dislike is mutual: Mr. Santos has said he will not vote this time because Ms. Pilip is not conservative enough.\par \par \uc2 What do the polls say?\par \par \uc2 Public polling has been scarce, but suggests a statistical dead heat.\par \par \uc2 A Siena College poll conducted for Newsday and released on Thursday showed Mr. Suozzi leading, 48 percent to 44 percent, among likely voters, an edge within the survey's margin of error. The same poll showed Mr. Biden narrowly trailing Mr. Trump in a hypothetical head-to-head.\par \par \uc2 More than $16 million in outside spending has already poured into the race from super PACs, labor unions and other special interests, according to \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  data. As of this week, Democrats have outspent Republicans roughly two to one.\par \par \uc2 But in a race that is expected to draw a low turnout, Republicans could overcome the spending disadvantage with their enviable Nassau County political machine.\par \par \uc2 Here's what it means for the House\par \par \uc2 Republicans currently control a narrow 219-to-212 majority in the House, meaning they can afford to lose only three votes on any partisan bill. That tight margin and the party's warring ideological factions have made the chamber almost ungovernable at times.\par \par \uc2 A Democratic pickup in New York would chip away another vote; a Republican hold could offer a small, but welcome, amount of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 The difference could have a profound impact on House Republicans' election-year ambitions. Their ability to impeach \uc2 Alejandro N. Mayorkas\uc2 , the homeland security secretary, or even Mr. Biden could be made or broken by a single vote.\par \par \uc2 But it could also shape more basic functions of government in the months ahead. Beginning in March, Congress faces a series of deadlines to keep the government open and funded, and reauthorize key programs Americans rely on, like the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  and cash assistance for needy families.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead to November\par \par \uc2 Stretching from the outskirts of Queens to the suburbs of Nassau County, the district encompasses a socially and economically diverse group of voters. That makes it an important bellwether for this fall's general elections.\par \par \uc2 The district is among the most affluent in the country, with a median income of $130,000 a year and Gold Coast mansions that sell for tens of millions of dollars. Almost 60 percent of the district is white, but there are sizable ethnic or religious groups that could have outsize sway on the race. Roughly 20 percent of registered voters are Asian American, and almost as many are Jewish.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden won the Third District by eight points in 2020, and Democrats still enjoy a slight partisan enrollment advantage. But its immigrant enclaves and suburban commuters have shifted rightward in successive elections since. Both he and Mr. Trump are now deeply unpopular there.\par \par \uc2 Whoever wins, the outcome will most likely shape the playbook for candidates in a handful of coveted suburban swing districts on Long Island, in New York's Hudson Valley and more across the country.\par \par \uc2 Tom Suozzi, known as a moderate Democrat, was a three-term congressman before taking a run at the governor's office in 2022. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEPHANIE KEITH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Mazi Pilip, a member of the part-time Nassau County Legislature, has promised to crack down on illegal immigration. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ADAM GRAY/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240209ek290003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
