\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Race for Santos's Seat, Candidates and Voters Focus on National Issues\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Fandos \par \uc2 1,414 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Here's what you need to know about the special House election on Feb. 13, pitting former Representative \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , a Democrat, against the Republican Party's candidate, Mazi Pilip, a local legislator.\par \par \uc2 The Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 National issues have dominated a special House election to replace George Santos in New York, as Republicans and Democrats take voters' temperatures on issues that could tip November's general election.\par \par \uc2 The race pits \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2 , a former Democratic congressman who represented the Queens and Long Island swing district for three terms, against Mazi Pilip, an Ethiopian-born local legislator. The open seat was created after the House voted to expel Mr. Santos, a Republican facing federal criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 The Feb. 13 contest carries unusual weight: A Democratic victory would narrow Republicans' barely governable House majority to just two votes.\par \par \uc2 Here's what you need to know about the race.\par \par \uc2 A well-known veteran vs. a party machine\par \par \uc2 After decades in office, Mr. Suozzi is one of the most recognizable and well-liked figures on Long Island, but his party is deeply unpopular.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pilip has a powerful local Republican machine behind her, but voters know very little about her.\par \par \uc2 Those inverse challenges are shaping the way both candidates are campaigning.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Suozzi has spent precious time and advertising money trying to separate himself from the Democratic brand. He has opposed his party's position on local criminal laws and taxes, called for hardening border security and his television ads (which are running on Fox News) never mention his party affiliation -- a gamble in a race where he needs base Democrats to turn out.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pilip is running just as hard toward her party. She has not appeared in public without better-known local Republicans, and has been willing to tolerate the criticism that has come from ducking televised debates and other unscripted moments that could trip her up as a first-time candidate.\par \par \uc2 That strategy could backfire, particularly with voters wary of electing another Santos-like candidate. But unlike Mr. Suozzi, Ms. Pilip has a powerful and effective party apparatus to lean on. Republicans have swept nearly every major election on Long Island since 2021 and their turnout operation could be critical in a midwinter election.\par \par \uc2 ''Being a special election, ground game means a lot,'' said \uc2 Peter T. King\uc2 , a popular former Republican congressman. ''There is no organization in the country like this, right now.''\par \par \uc2 A migrant influx at the border and in New York City\par \par \uc2 Republicans have made fears about immigration a centerpiece of their campaigns for years. But at a time when record numbers of migrants are crossing into the southwestern border, the issue is particularly potent.\par \par \uc2 Unlike past immigration surges, this one has intimately affected New York. More than 150,000 asylum seekers have come to the city seeking shelter, draining billions of dollars in public funds that Mayor Eric Adams has threatened to cut from other social services. Polls show suburban voters are incensed.\par \par \uc2 Republicans want them to blame Mr. Suozzi. They have blanketed the district with ads claiming he voted to weaken border security and recycling clips that show him boasting that he ''kicked ICE out of Nassau County,'' a reference to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement. Glossy mailers call him ''Sanctuary City'' Suozzi.\par \par \uc2 The approach echoes a successful strategy that Republicans used to dominate Long Island in the 2022 midterm elections, when they were able to blame Democrats for another complex bipartisan problem: crime.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Suozzi is clearly feeling the pressure. But unlike other members of his party, he has not shied from the issue. He cut his own ad featuring a 2018 clip from Fox News in which he praises ICE, and has repeatedly pointed to a 2019 essay written with Mr. King proposing a ''grand compromise on immigration.''\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Pilip held a recent event outside a migrant shelter in the Queens portion of the district, Mr. Suozzi showed up to try to flip the script -- blaming Republicans for scuttling a potential bipartisan border security deal in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Will \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights matter?\par \par \uc2 Democrats are turning to their familiar playbook on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in an attempt to sour moderate women against Ms. Pilip.\par \par \uc2 ''Pilip is part of the extreme wing of the Republican Party that wants to take away your rights and benefits,'' the narrator of one ominous ad from the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee warns.\par \par \uc2 The issue has not shown up high on the list of voters' electoral priorities in New York, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are considered safe. But Democrats believe the data is misleading, particularly among the district's affluent suburban voters.\par \par \uc2 ''The pollsters have not learned the lesson of their missing the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on voting patterns in 2022,'' said Bruce Gyory, a longtime Democratic strategist. ''When it comes to voting, pro-choice women will not support a candidate they see as not dependable on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pilip's rush to defend her position underscores the point. While she has said that as a mother of seven, she is personally opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and accepts the endorsement of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Conservative Party, she insists she would vote against a federal ban on the procedure. (She has deflected questions from journalists seeking more detail on her position.)\par \par \uc2 ''Protecting women's rights and Social Security are my top priorities,'' she said in a recent video.\par \par \uc2 A post-Oct. 7 test for Jewish voters\par \par \uc2 The fallout from Israel's deadly war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has upended the political landscape for American Jews. The race, in one of the most Jewish congressional districts in the country, may show just how much.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Pilip's allies believe she is the ideal candidate to meet the moment. An Orthodox Jew, she moved to Israel as a refugee and served in the \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2 . She first ran for office in New York to fight antisemitism and has been a defender of Israel's war effort.\par \par \uc2 Those positions are expected to drive high turnout in Great Neck, Ms. Pilip's home base, where thousands of Persian and Orthodox Jews have moved sharply rightward. But political strategists are also watching to see if she can pick off more religiously moderate Jewish voters who have historically voted Democratic but fear that the party's commitment to Israel is slipping.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a simmering frustration with the Democratic Party,'' said Jon Kaiman, a former Democratic official who watched some Jewish voters swing hard against his campaign for town supervisor of North Hempstead last November. ''I'd go to a train station and hear, 'Sorry, it's not personal. I can't vote for a Democrat.'''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kaiman is now working with Mr. Suozzi to try to reassure voters that he is a staunch ally of Israel who will fight back against left-leaning Democrats pushing to put conditions on American military aide.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are so high that Mr. Suozzi took a last-minute trip to Israel just before Christmas to meet with the families of Israeli hostages. He also ran ads saying he would put ''no conditions'' on American aid to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Whither George Santos?\par \par \uc2 There would be no special election without Mr. Santos and his spectacular downfall. But his shadow over the election has been surprisingly limited.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are hoping that the anger pent up during the year he spent in office will still push their voters to the polls and depress Republican turnout.\par \par \uc2 But two months after his ouster, Mr. Santos appears to be a rapidly diminishing political force.\par \par \uc2 His face has come down from billboards that once spotted the district. Neither campaign has used him in paid advertising. And voters appear unmoved by photos of Ms. Pilip embracing him in 2022 (she has since denounced him).\par \par \uc2 The disinterest is mutual. Speaking to reporters outside a courtroom where he is preparing to stand trial, Mr. Santos said he planned to sit the election out, taking a dig at Ms. Pilip's decision to remain registered as a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't vote for Democrats,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Grace Ashford contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Grace Ashford contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Tom Suozzi, a well-known former Democratic congressman, is running against Mazi Pilip, an Ethiopian-born local legislator. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY STEPHANIE KEITH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; DAVE SANDERS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240131ek1v0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
