\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Trump Win Could Bring a Backdoor Federal  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mary Ziegler \par \uc2 1,539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For many Americans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the election in November will most likely come down to a choice between voting for President Biden and staying home. They won't vote for the man who ensured Roe v. Wade's demise, but many of them are unenthusiastic about the prospect of Mr. Biden being the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 So for the Democratic Party, motivating voters on this issue will be key. Mr. Biden's advisers clearly know this and have sent him and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on something of a speaking tour this month to highlight their commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But it's odd how the Biden-Harris campaign has chosen to speak about this issue, with a heavy focus on their plan to ''codify Roe.'' Bold congressional action on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely from either side: A federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban under a second Trump administration is implausible, but it is also unlikely that this Congress, or the next, passes legislation, like the Women's Health Protection Act, to bring back the protections that were in Roe.\par \par \uc2 What is plausible is that, returned to the White House, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would seek to use his executive power -- power that his allies are aiming to increase on his behalf -- to further curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. He might seem uninterested in doing much of anything about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, on the campaign trail, which is a shrewd political move, given how unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have proved to be since Roe's reversal. But that could well change when he is in office again. And Mr. Biden and Ms. Harris would be wise to make clear to voters the real risks of a second Trump presidency for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 This election is the most consequential for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in half a century -- a referendum on whether voters continue to be the ones to decide on this issue, or whether instead conservatives in the executive branch will decide for them.\par \par \uc2 The destruction of Roe in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization was never going to be the end of the battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in America. Since Dobbs, progressive as well as swing states have passed ballot initiatives protecting or enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Polls demonstrate near record support for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is focused on fetal personhood -- ensuring full constitutional rights for fetuses. This singular goal has drawn activists to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement for decades. The contemporary movement argues that recognizing fetal personhood requires the full criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the punishment of doctors and those who ''aid or abet'' them. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''abolitionists'' even argue that personhood requires retribution against women who seek abortions. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups believe they are advancing the human rights cause of our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 Fetal personhood may be the ultimate goal of the movement, but the agenda has not proved popular with voters. That's why plans for a second Trump term's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy will probably rely less on voters than on the idea of a muscular executive branch. A playbook for an incoming Republican president created by Project 2025, a well-coordinated effort supported by more than 90 conservative groups, includes attempting to strip access to mifepristone, a drug used in a majority of U.S. abortions.\par \par \uc2 The availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills has made the enforcement of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans difficult, as Americans can order pills online or travel out of state to get them. That's why \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have been gunning for mifepristone, one of two drugs typically used in a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including in a 2022 lawsuit, Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine v. \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 . That case, which could severely curb access to the drug nationwide, has now made its way to the Supreme Court, with a decision expected in June.\par \par \uc2 A second Trump administration could still try to eliminate access to the drug nationwide even if the court sides against the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plaintiffs in Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine. The road map created by Project 2025 calls on the F.D.A. to limit access to mifepristone and ultimately withdraw it from the market as a drug ''proven to be dangerous to women and by definition fatally unsafe for unborn children.''\par \par \uc2 The scientists at the F.D.A. might not even need to be on board with this plan for it to work. The secretary of the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , a presidential appointee, can override the F.D.A.'s drug approval decisions -- a fact that raised red flags at the height of the Covid pandemic. As the authors of Project 2025's road map recognize, Republican control of Health and Human Services could mean the end of the most common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  method in the United States in blue as well as red states.\par \par \uc2 Leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups also have coalesced around plans to revive the 1873 Comstock Act; what remains of the broad and archaic law could, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups claim, punish anyone receiving or mailing any ''obscene, lewd, lascivious, indecent, filthy or vile article'' with up to five years in prison for a first offense. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have reimagined Comstock for a Trump Department of Justice as a way to effectively ban most abortions everywhere, pointing to language in the statute that makes it a federal crime to mail or receive any item ''designed, adapted or intended for producing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The statute hasn't been enforced much, if at all, in cases of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in about 100 years and has been construed as protecting the ordinary practice of medicine since the 1930s. But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, which are cherry-picking words to turn the statute into a no-exceptions ban, hope that a second Trump administration would ignore federal precedent.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025's road map argues that a Republican Justice Department should enforce Comstock ''against providers and distributors'' of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. A Trump administration could follow through on these plans by prosecuting doctors and drug companies anywhere in the country: The Comstock Act, as a federal law, could be read to override state protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, like the former Texas solicitor general Jonathan Mitchell, argue that Comstock should be interpreted as an effective ban on all abortions because every procedure that takes place in the United States relies on some item placed in the mail, from a surgical glove to a curet. Mr. Mitchell and his allies read the law to exclude explicit exceptions for the life or health of the patient.\par \par \uc2 Understood in this way, the law could punish women who receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related items or information using the Postal Service or another carrier or even websites. If a Trump Justice Department began prosecuting doctors for prescribing or shipping pills in New York or California, that would certainly draw a court challenge, and the administration may not have the legal authority to follow through on the plans drawn up by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents like their chances in the Supreme Court and have prepared arguments for Mr. Trump to use that are tailor-made for its conservative supermajority.\par \par \uc2 It is tempting to dismiss the possibility of such a backdoor federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban as far-fetched. Mr. Trump hardly sounds like a passionate supporter of fetal rights at the moment, and his embrace of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement always seemed more a matter of political expedience than a sincere conversion.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump will have different incentives once in office. Pleasing the social conservatives who donate to his political organizations -- the sort of people who could be key to securing his post-presidential future -- may strike him as more important than pleasing the majority of American voters. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups expect Mr. Trump to deliver if he is re-elected and view his current reluctance to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a short-term political necessity.\par \par \uc2 The choice in this election is not simply between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The choice is between the status quo -- or a chance for more protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and the possibility of an effective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that would be all but impossible to achieve using democratic means.\par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court transformed by Mr. Trump promised that with the demise of Roe, it would be up to the voters of each state to decide on the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If plans for institutionalizing Trumpism come to fruition, the future of American \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be up to the voters of each state for much longer. It may be up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler is a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , and a 2023-24 Guggenheim fellow. She is the author of ''Roe: The History of a National Obsession.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY MUSTAFA HUSSAIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240131ek1v00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
