\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Amid Gaza Protesters, Biden Raises His Voice For Right to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Rogers \par \uc2 1,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The event was interrupted by at least 10 people protesting the war in Gaza, but the president focused on calling attention to restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 President Biden headlined a rally in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on Tuesday, trying to focus on an issue that has galvanized his supporters even as he was interrupted every few minutes by at least 10 people protesting the war in Gaza, an issue that has just as quickly divided Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, a practicing Catholic who has been a reluctant supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, has leaned on Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to be the most vocal activist in his administration. But on Tuesday, in front of a banner reading ''Restore Roe,'' Mr. Biden raised his voice to a yell in support of Roe v. Wade, which guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the Supreme Court overturned it in 2022.\par \par \uc2 His yelling also helped drown out the voices of people waving Palestinian flags, shouting ''Genocide Joe'' and demanding a cease-fire. ''Please don't jump,'' he said to one protester waving a sign in a balcony.\par \par \uc2 ''They feel deeply,'' Mr. Biden said at another point. (The antiwar group CodePink later took credit for organizing the protest.)\par \par \uc2 The moment was a convergence of a promising election-year issue and another that has threatened to overshadow Mr. Biden's presidency and dim his chances with more progressive corners of the Democratic electorate. Different portions of the crowd -- supporters and protesters -- were either clapping or screaming, illustrating the conflict in real time.\par \par \uc2 Faced with yelling protesters, Mr. Biden read quickly from his prepared remarks and did not respond to individual protesters the way he has at past events.\par \par \uc2 ''We can make Roe again the law of America,'' Mr. Biden said, his voice rising. ''Are you ready to make it happen? To do that, we need a new Congress. Are you ready to make that happen?''\par \par \uc2 It was a striking appearance compared with Mr. Biden's comments on the issue just seven months ago, when he told supporters of his re-election campaign that he was ''not big on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' because of his faith, but that Roe had ''got it right.'' Navigating his personal beliefs, Mr. Biden has focused on the issue as one rooted in personal freedom and privacy.\par \par \uc2 The differing styles of the president and the vice president were on display beginning on Monday, when Ms. Harris held an event in Wisconsin to call attention to Republican-led measures in the state to institute an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban from 1849. She criticized former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and asked the audience to applaud a couple who had been denied health care by doctors while experiencing pregnancy complications.\par \par \uc2 When he arrived for his rally on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. Biden met privately with Dr. Austin Dennard, who had to leave Texas to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when her pregnancy developed complications.\par \par \uc2 He also met with Amanda Zurawski, who is suing the State of Texas after she was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when her water broke at 18 weeks. She developed sepsis and said she nearly died from the infection. According to the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which argued the case for Roe before the Supreme Court, 21 more women have joined as plaintiffs in that lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 ''I had to wait until I got so sick until my life was considered in danger'' before doctors would provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Zurawski told the crowd of losing her daughter, whom she named Willow. ''It took three days and a near-death crash into septic shock before my doctor could finally provide the health care I desperately needed,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Zurawski blamed Mr. Trump, who has bragged recently of overturning Roe. ''It is unthinkable that anyone could cheer on these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that nearly took my life.''\par \par \uc2 She received a hug from Mr. Biden, who told the crowd that he would continue fighting for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But any sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  action remains unlikely given the divided control of Congress, and Mr. Biden is unable to grant Roe's protections through executive action.\par \par \uc2 His administration has tried to preserve access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone, but that quickly became mired in legal challenges. The Supreme Court announced that it would hear a case challenging the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the commonly used pill.\par \par \uc2 The event on Tuesday was largely attended by volunteers and employees with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups. Onstage, Mr. Biden and Ms. Harris were joined by Jill Biden, the first lady, and Doug Emhoff, Ms. Harris's husband.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Emhoff has hosted events on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said on Tuesday that reproductive freedom was ''not just a women's issue, it's an everyone's issue.'' Dr. Biden, who has rebuked ''extremist Republicans'' before, repeated a story she shared before the 2022 midterms of a friend who had gotten pregnant when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal and had stayed with Dr. Biden's family after the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''Secrecy, shame, silence, danger, even death,'' Dr. Biden said, repeating the message she has shared before. ''That's what defined that time for so many women, and because of Dobbs, that's where we're finding ourselves back again,'' she said, referring to the 2022 Supreme Court decision that overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris also echoed much of what she said in Wisconsin on Monday, sharing stories of women who had endured miscarriages in toilets because they were refused health care, and women who were turned away from emergency rooms because doctors were wary of providing care.\par \par \uc2 ''Do we trust women? Do we believe in reproductive freedom? Do we believe in the promise of America?'' she yelled, as members of the audience rose to their feet. ''Are we ready to fight for it?''\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju, the president of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2  -- previously known as \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  -- said in an interview that she had seen Mr. Biden ''evolve'' on his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But she said the Biden administration needed to do more to emphasize what is needed to codify Roe's protections, and to warn of what could happen for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Mr. Trump won the presidency.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to need everybody singing from the same songbook,'' she said, and ''talking about what a Trump presidency would do.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Jennifer McClellan, Democrat of Virginia, said that Mr. Biden and Ms. Harris had different campaign styles when it came to Roe, but that they both spoke to different parts of the Democratic electorate.\par \par \uc2 ''I think they're very different people with different styles on just about everything,'' Ms. McClellan said. ''But Democrats are not a monolith. This country's not a monolith. We need people who can talk to every generation and every demographic group.''\par \par \uc2 ''We can make Roe again the law of America,'' President Biden told the crowd at the rally on Tuesday in Manassas, Va. Left, he was interrupted multiple times by activists protesting the war in Gaza. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY PETE MAROVICH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240124ek1o0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
