\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Opponents, Facing Obstacles Ahead, March in Washington\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,628 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A year after celebrating the reversal of Roe v. Wade, the annual March for Life gathered Friday in a very different political climate.\par \par \uc2 Last year, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists descended on the National Mall in triumph for the annual March for Life, eager to enter a new era for their ambitions to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the reversal of Roe v. Wade, the landmark Supreme Court ruling that established federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 But this year, the first presidential election year in post-Roe America, the movement finds itself marching once more in Washington not in triumph, exactly, but grasping to advance their cause after a series of political defeats, fewer powerful allies, and setbacks in the court of public opinion.\par \par \uc2 ''We are experiencing the reverberations of that massive historic shift,'' said Jeanne Mancini, president of the March for Life. ''We certainly do have our work cut out for us, but that's why we started.''\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe has greatly shifted the political calculus. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have proven to be a mobilizing force for a new coalition of Democrats, independent voters and even some moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 That leaves the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement fighting protracted state-by-state battles over politics and policy. The thousands of people from churches, schools, and activist groups who marched on Friday represent a movement that is splintered over strategy and immediate goals.\par \par \uc2 Four years ago, as the 2020 presidential campaign was gathering steam, Donald J. Trump addressed the march, the first sitting president to do so in person, in an effort to galvanize conservative Christian voters. In recent months, while he has boasted that he made the overturning of Roe possible, he has also avoided highlighting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue in his campaign, a recognition of its political volatility.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, a favorite candidate of leading \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, ran a distant second to Trump in the Iowa caucus earlier this week. (Mr. Trump has called Mr. DeSantis's decision to sign a state ban on abortions after six weeks ''a terrible mistake.'')\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement now faces a reversed dynamic: In presidential campaigns for the past half century, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a motivating issue largely for Republican voters fighting to overturn Roe, not for Democrats -- but now it is the Democrats who are fighting a landmark Supreme Court decision on the issue. With Roe already overturned, Republicans and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents no longer have it as an urgent rallying cry, the kind that motivates people to show up and march.\par \par \uc2 On Friday morning, hours before the start of the march in unusually cold, snowy weather, Nick Baker, 22, passed out signs to students arriving at the National Mall.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baker, an assistant editor at the Young Americas foundation, a conservative youth organization in Reston, Va., said: ''With the overturning of Roe, things are a lot different now. We are hoping to encourage states to adopt pro-life positions.''\par \par \uc2 Ask how he would handle the growing opposition to those positions, he said his group would adopt \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's ''happy warrior ways.''\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party is marshaling its forces in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights this week for the 51st anniversary of the establishment of Roe. Ahead of the primary next Tuesday, the New Hampshire congressional delegation plans to hold an event in Concord on Saturday with \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, on the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have been enacted in a number of states.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Vice President Harris plan to hold their own events next week to mark the Roe anniversary, in the political battlegrounds of Wisconsin and Northern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 The reversal of Roe and its aftermath have also confronted the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement with a new political reality in the states. Even voters who are against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally have been troubled by how new state bans have worked in practice. Restrictive laws have prompted thousands of women to seek abortions out of state, and have drawn complaints that the laws put at risk the lives of women who had wanted their pregnancies but whose fetuses received fatal diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 ''The G.O.P. can't and won't stop losing elections, and their answer is to double down on their horrific policies by celebrating an increasingly extremist, shrinking minority with a march,'' said Mini Timmaraju, president of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 . ''They're so out of touch.''\par \par \uc2 About 70 percent of voters nationwide, a record high, now say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, according to a recent Gallup poll. About 60 percent think overturning Roe was a ''bad thing,'' the poll found.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a contradiction between being for some reasonable, humane limit and wanting Roe to stay as it was,'' Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, said of the poll results.\par \par \uc2 Her group favors a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Under Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was allowed until the fetus could survive outside the womb, usually around 23 or 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement continues to have influential supporters in the highest ranks of conservative power. House Speaker Mike Johnson, a Republican and the most powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  elected official in the country, addressed this year's march from the stage. He has attended the march in the past, and his unexpected rise to the speakership last fall was a boost for conservative Christians who share his views.\par \par \uc2 ''I am myself the product of an unplanned pregnancy,'' Mr. Johnson said to the crowd, telling the story of how his parents, who were teenagers in 1972, ''chose life, and I am very profoundly grateful that they did.''\par \par \uc2 ''We have to build a culture that encourages and assists more and more people to make that same decision,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Instead of talking about a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban or hard-line restrictions, he rallied the crowd against a proposed Biden administration spending rule that would restrict federal funds from flowing to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 Many activists and politicians who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have said that the movement now needs to focus on the needs of pregnant women and their families, and to offer a more compassionate message. The theme for this year's March for Life is ''With every woman, with every child.''\par \par \uc2 The House passed two bills on Thursday that were high priorities for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders. One would prevent the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  from excluding anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers from receiving federal funding; the other would require that pregnant college students receive information about resources and alternative options, including adoption.\par \par \uc2 The bills are meant to shift the public's focus away from Republican \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and toward less politically charged measures and messaging. Both bills passed along party lines, and have yet to be considered by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Concerned Women for America, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says its top legislative goal was to pass legislation to create a federal website listing resources for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a definite priority for the new speaker,'' said Penny Nance, the group's president. ''It matches his heart.''\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins\uc2 , president of Students for Life, is urging Republican candidates to challenge medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , instead of focusing on gestational-limit bans.\par \par \uc2 If the annual march has revealed anything over the years, it is that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is persistent and built for longevity. Supporters have turned out even after significant losses like President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's re-election and past setbacks at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The March for Life's goal is to expand its reach at the state level, and transfer decades of persistence on the Mall into local marches that push for state action. Last year the group organized marches in eight states; this year the number rose to 16, and organizers hope to expand to all 50 states within six years or so.\par \par \uc2 Marching students and activists waved flags on Friday afternoon representing the Vatican, \uc2 the Knights of Columbus\uc2  and various Roman Catholic dioceses around the country. A giant screen on the main stage played video from state marches, and pockets of the crowd on the Mall cheered when their state was shown or mentioned. The snowfall became heavier as the group turned to march toward the Capitol, which seemed to disappear against the white sky.\par \par \uc2 Feelings in the crowd were hopeful, but mixed.\par \par \uc2 Suzanne Fox, 31, came to the march with her husband and four children, all five or under, who played in the snow as she spoke of the hope she had for a future without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We are here to show the world we support life as much as we can,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Lyn and Mike Dobyns, 73 and 74, drove from Wilmington, Ohio, and met their granddaughter, who came to the march with a school group.\par \par \uc2 They said the fight against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has felt more difficult since Roe was overturned, and noted that voters in Ohio passed new constitutional protections for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Dobyns wanted a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception. But her husband, a retired lawyer, said, ''I don't know if I am there.''\par \par \uc2 He said he hoped the country and the states would set ''at least a reasonable limit -- 13 weeks, or a number everyone can agree on.''\par \par \uc2 ''He wants to be realistic,'' Ms. Dobyns said.\par \par \uc2 And she acknowledged that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement was one for the long haul: ''You have to take one step at a time.''\par \par \uc2 Thousands gathered for the annual March For Life in Washington on Friday. The movement is grasping to advance the cause. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240120ek1k0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
