\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans Aren't the Best Way to Save Unborn Lives\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel K. Williams \par \uc2 1,331 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents did not expect the overturning of Roe v. Wade and the passage of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans to lead to the expansion of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in much of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But this in fact is what happened: While 16 states ban most or virtually all abortions a year and a half after the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization ruling in June 2022, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers went up in states where it is legal. As anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups prepare for their annual March for Life on Friday, they face the reality that the past year brought a string of defeats for their cause, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters winning victories in every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum submitted to voters, even in conservative states. Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 That's largely because their strategy has focused on passing bans, which have been politically polarizing and have alienated members of the Democratic Party -- a party that only a few decades ago included many supporters of the pro-life cause.\par \par \uc2 It didn't have to be this way. As a historian of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics, I wrote in early 2021 that the end of Roe may ''only marginally reduce the number of legal abortions'' in the United States and ''at worst, may lead to a pro-choice Democratic backlash that will expand the number of legal abortions.''\par \par \uc2 I also pointed to another way to reduce abortions in the United States -- expanding the social safety net so more pregnant women choose to keep their babies. Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents advocated this strategy nearly 50 years ago, but it was largely forgotten after the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement allied with the political right in the Reagan era. Today, as the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement faces new challenges amid rising national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers, it may be time to rediscover this forgotten path.\par \par \uc2 After Roe, in 1973, resulted in the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in every state, opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were divided as to how best to defend unborn human life. Most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations favored a constitutional amendment that would protect human life from the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 But a few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents -- especially those who were liberal Democrats -- believed that instead of embarking on what appeared to be a politically impossible quest for such an amendment, they could prevent abortions by offering help to women facing pregnancies amid hardship.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Eunice Kennedy Shriver, whose husband, Sargent Shriver, was a candidate for the Democratic presidential nomination in 1976, campaigned for federal legislation that would establish a nationwide network of crisis pregnancy and infant-care centers. ''The best way to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is really to offer alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she declared.\par \par \uc2 This was not a new idea for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations, which before Roe v. Wade had floated proposals like ''birth insurance'' to care for infants needing expensive medical treatment, subsidized adoption and government-funded day care.\par \par \uc2 But Roe interrupted those plans and prompted nearly all anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations to make support of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  constitutional amendment the litmus test by which they evaluated political candidates, regardless of their stances on other measures that might reduce the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by offering assistance to pregnant women in need of support.\par \par \uc2 In the 1980s, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations shifted their focus to the more achievable goal of reversing Roe through a Supreme Court decision -- a change in tactics that solidified their support for the Republican Party, since Republican presidents and senators were far more likely than Democrats to favor judicial nominees who objected to Roe.\par \par \uc2 Now that Roe is gone, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have pinned their hopes on bans as the primary way to protect unborn human life. But those bans have proved more unpopular than they expected, and have had less effect on the national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate than they would have liked. Reports in late 2023 indicated that the annual number of abortions in the United States may have been higher after Dobbs than before the decision, since bans in some states were offset by increased protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement believed that legal prohibitions could move the cultural consensus in a pro-life direction, but it seems that the opposite has happened. New \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics were constructed in Illinois and New Mexico to serve women from nearby states with restrictive laws, as clinics across the nation (even in conservative Florida) reported an increase in demand for abortions. After voters approved an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights amendment to the Ohio Constitution in November, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights redoubled their efforts to put the issue on the ballot in other states, from Florida to Arizona.\par \par \uc2 In such a political climate, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents must focus on changing hearts and minds before changing laws. They need to win public trust by demonstrating that their respect for life does not end with birth.\par \par \uc2 A 2023 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  study showed that 42 percent of the people who had abortions had incomes below the poverty line. Another study showed that 40 percent of those having abortions cited financial considerations as a primary reason for their decision -- which suggests that economic assistance would probably enable some women who have abortions to instead choose to carry their pregnancies to term. Paid family leave policies may be particularly effective at reducing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, according to one 2010 study.\par \par \uc2 Many religious and private charitable organizations are already providing assistance to pregnant women at a limited scale, but support for comprehensive national programs would extend the benefits of this strategy to a far greater number of women.\par \par \uc2 In the 1970s, when the movement included a substantial number of Democrats and many supporters of the social programs of the New Deal and Great Society, proposals like the ones the Shrivers presented might have made sense to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents -- but what will today's conservative Republican \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes think of them?\par \par \uc2 To save unborn lives, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement may have to move beyond partisan thinking and support any proposals that are likely to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even when they come from Democrats. In at least a few conservative states, some Republican legislators are already supporting free college tuition benefits, state Medicaid expansions for new mothers, reforms in the adoption and foster care systems, and paid family leave -- all of which have the potential to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates as they reduce the economic burden on those caring for children, or empower mothers to advance their economic prospects and secure higher-paying jobs.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents should demand that lawmakers at both the state and national levels prove their pro-life bona fides by supporting measures such as these that will offer positive alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- just as the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents (including myself) joined with supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in pushing for protections for pregnant workers. The Senate passed the Pregnant Workers Fairness Act a year ago with strong bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 Since the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's narrow focus on bans has alienated potential allies and failed to reduce the number of abortions, it needs to look again at a strategy that holds greater promise. A paid family leave plan or a state Medicaid expansion might not offer the movement the same immediately satisfying sense of victory as a state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban -- but, in the end, it might save a greater number of unborn lives.\par \par \uc2 Daniel K. Williams teaches American history at Ashland University and is the author of ''The Politics of the Cross: A Christian Alternative to Partisanship.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240119ek1j0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
