\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Voter Support For  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Faces Limits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 2,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Post-Roe voting might bring America to a new consensus -- but only if the voters keep getting their say.\par \par \uc2 As long as the Supreme Court's decision in Roe v. Wade was the law of the land, conservatives complained that it had squelched the democratic process -- that unelected men in black robes had handed down a national edict rather than letting the American people sort out a consensus.\par \par \uc2 Celebrating Roe's reversal in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, they declared that democracy would finally be allowed to take over and settle the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once and for all. ''Now the American people get their voice back,'' Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Republican leader, said. While the court in Roe had ''inflamed debate and deepened division,'' Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2  wrote for the majority in Dobbs, ''the people and their elected representatives'' would reach the ''national settlement'' that had eluded the country for so long.\par \par \uc2 Eighteen months later, the American people are indeed using their voices, but not in the way anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates had hoped. In a steady march of ballot measures, even in conservative states like Ohio, they have codified a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rejected attempts to restrict it. Polls show increasing support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in all 50 states, with majorities in nearly all states -- even deep red states -- saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 The entire dynamic of the debate has shifted: Democrats who once dreaded speaking the word \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now running on it, while Republicans struggle to define what exceptions they would allow to bans on the procedure, and former President Donald J. Trump now says overturning Roe was a mistake.\par \par \uc2 And yet a ''national settlement'' seems more elusive than ever. One reason is simply American federalism, which allows states to set their own standards where there is no federal one. But a bigger reason is that Republicans around the country -- the same people, in many cases, who once complained about Roe blocking the democratic process and imposing a one-size-fits-all rule on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide -- have turned much of their energy to keeping the issue away from voters.\par \par \uc2 Republican-controlled legislatures, shocked by the results of ballot measures that put the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to the people, are trying to make those measures harder to pass, and even abolish them as an option. The issue is now in a different set of courts, in the states, where anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have searched out like-minded judges in an attempt to take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills off the market. Last week, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  -- the black robes who were supposed to have put themselves out of the business of deciding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- announced that it would take that case. And some of the same Republicans who once argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be settled by the will of the people in the states now argue that what is needed is for Congress to pass a uniform federal law.\par \par \uc2 Few people predicted how much \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would define the elections of 2022; now it is the defining issue for 2024. But anyone hoping for democracy to resolve the debate will need to wait far longer than that. If the goal of the Dobbs decision was to let the will of the people decide the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the question now is whether, in much of the country, the people's opinions will be allowed to count.\par \par \uc2 A Clear Pattern\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups always said the country would become a patchwork of laws if Roe went away, and they have been proven correct. But so far, it's not the patchwork anyone expected.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, the will of the people has protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. After voters affirmed a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state constitutions in Kansas and Ohio, women in those states can get abortions well into the second trimester. In Michigan, a similar ballot measure led to a newly Democratic legislature enshrining Roe. And in purple Virginia, Gov. \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 , a Republican, tried to get voters to give his party control of the legislature by campaigning on a promise to pass what he pitched as a consensus bill allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 15 weeks. Voters rejected the bid and elected a legislature that now stands to join others in sponsoring a ballot measure to codify a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Voters in Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, too, came out to elect state Supreme Court justices who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not explicitly on the ballot. In Wisconsin, doctors had stopped performing abortions immediately after Roe fell for fear of running afoul of an 1849 ban, but the justice's election gave \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  enough confidence to reopen its clinics there.\par \par \uc2 Even Republican voters in red states are increasingly supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but their elected representatives show little interest in changing the law to reflect that.\par \par \uc2 In July, Christine Matthews, a Republican pollster, surveyed people in nine states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned except to save the life of the pregnant woman, and found that few knew the specifics of their law, but when they were told, the majority said they supported adding exceptions. The sentiment was highest in Texas, which banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nine months before Roe fell, through a civilian-enforced law, which suggests that the more people know and see, the more they want to liberalize the laws.\par \par \uc2 More recently, another polling firm, PerryUndem, tested two similar ballot measures to see which found more support: One would establish an individual right to reproductive freedom, including the right to make ''all decisions'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the other would establish that right but allow the state to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, unless the procedure was ''medically necessary.'' The version without any gestational restriction polled better.\par \par \uc2 Tresa Undem, one of the firm's founders, was so surprised by the result that she asked her staff to quadruple-check the numbers, and then conducted another poll and follow-up interviews to ask voters why they responded the way they did. Those polls found that respondents who had heard stories about women with pregnancy complications being forced to travel out of state for abortions were overwhelmingly likely to support a ballot measure -- that included 57 percent of Republicans. Even 46 percent of voters who said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in most cases were likely to support a ballot measure establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if they had heard these stories. And over and over, voters explained their preference by saying they did not want the government involved in deciding who gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the potential for ballot measures is limited; fewer than 10 states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also allow citizen-sponsored ballot measures. And in those states, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and lawmakers are leaning hard into trying to keep the measures from making it onto the ballot. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups in Missouri have spent nine months in court fighting the state's Republican officials over their attempts to have the language on the ballot say that a yes vote would allow ''dangerous and unregulated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until birth.''\par \par \uc2 In Arizona and South Dakota, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that once saw ballot measures as their preferred instrument are running ''decline to sign'' campaigns against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups seeking signatures on ballot petitions. After Ohio voters decisively established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution in November, \uc2 Rick Santorum\uc2 , the former Pennsylvania senator and longtime anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crusader, expressed relief that not many states allowed ballot measures, ''because pure democracies are not the way to run a country.''\par \par \uc2 Victories for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups on ballot measures haven't necessarily resulted in changes to the law, or opened up access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Kentucky, even as voters rejected a ballot measure attempting to say there was no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and then re-elected a Democratic governor who campaigned on his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a court has upheld a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  passed by the Republican legislature.\par \par \uc2 So even as voters in Kentucky turn out to sound a lot like voters in California on the ballot questions, Americans will be living in two different countries on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In one state, it is a crime that strips doctors of their licenses and sends them to prison for 99 years; next door, it is not only legal but publicly funded.\par \par \uc2 'A Lot of Gray'\par \par \uc2 There are hints of a possible democratic consensus in the way many voters in conservative states have explained their support for ballot measures enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Those voters do not talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the way pollsters and advocates have for five decades, as a debate between moral absolutes. After 18 months of stories about women denied care or pushed to the brink of death before they can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more and more voters who might still describe themselves as ''pro-life'' or say they would not choose an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for themselves also say they do not want to stop someone who needs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from getting one.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Undem said she has watched support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase in her focus groups since about 2017, after Trump's election and the women's marches that followed. For too long, she said, pollsters have described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a deeply divisive issue. In fact, she said, voters are not divided, if the question they are asked is who should decide whether to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Seven in 10 will say the person who is pregnant. The same percentage say they do not struggle with their views.\par \par \uc2 ''Is it complex, is there a lot of gray? Yes,'' Ms. Undem said. ''That's why they don't want politicians making their decisions.''\par \par \uc2 Even as Republican legislatures are doing everything they can to block more moderating public opinion from finding a voice in the law, there are some cracks in the wall.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, for instance, Richard Briggs, a Republican state senator and heart surgeon, went along in 2019 when the legislature passed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting at conception because he assumed that Roe was firmly in place, so the law would never go into effect. ''I just didn't want to waste a lot of time on something we didn't think would happen,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Last year, after that ban took effect, he polled high-frequency voters in his district in and around Knoxville and found remarkable support for some legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  among all but about 20 percent -- and the results looked the same whether the respondents identified as ''Trump Republicans'' or ''traditional Republicans.'' He filed legislation earlier this year seeking exceptions for women facing serious medical emergencies, and in cases of rape, incest or severe fetal anomalies. He couldn't get the package past a critical committee, after the state Right to Life group revoked its endorsement of him and threatened to do the same to any lawmaker who backed his bill.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think we're listening to the Republican voting public,'' Dr. Briggs said. He now plans to introduce the legislation in steps over the next several years, beginning next year with a bill he calls the ''Right to Have Children'' act, which would allow women to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of ''medically futile pregnancies'' or when they face conditions that can limit their future ability to have a child.\par \par \uc2 He expects a difficult road, though: Right now, he said, his Republican colleagues are more afraid of primary challenges from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups than they are of voters in their safe districts.\par \par \uc2 And Republican voters are not showing an inclination to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the deciding factor in their vote: While Democrats say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely to drive their votes post-Dobbs, Republicans say it is less so. Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights probably aren't going to vote against their party if they still agree with it on taxes. The American tradition of straight-ticket voting endures.\par \par \uc2 For the law to reflect what voters want, Dr. Briggs said, ''What needs to happen is for some people to lose elections.''\par \par \uc2 Signs before Ohio voted to codify the right to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE McGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); Left, an anti-\uc2  demonstrator in Amarillo, Texas, in February. Right, Ohioans United for \uc2  after the state voted for a constitutional amendment enshrining the right to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MERIDITH KOHUT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; MADDIE McGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); An exam room at Red River Women's Clinic, which was the only \uc2  provider in North Dakota. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JENN ACKERMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A12) This article appeared in print on page A1, A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231218ejci00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
