\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Proves  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Exemptions Are a Sham\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 1,005 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This essay has been updated to reflect news developments.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, horror stories started emerging of women denied medically urgent abortions for pregnancies gone dangerously awry. In response, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement developed a sort of conspiracy theory to rationalize away the results of their policies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists, they argued, were deliberately misconstruing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, leading doctors to refuse to treat women who obviously qualified for exceptions. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are spreading the dangerous lie that lifesaving care is not or may not be permitted in these states, leading to provider confusion and poor outcomes for women,'' said a report by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Charlotte Lozier Institute. The Catholic conservative Richard Doerflinger accused ''pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups'' of spreading ''false and exaggerated claims in order to 'paralyze' physicians and discredit the laws.''\par \par \uc2 Whether this argument stemmed from genuine denial or a cynical desire to mislead the public, a shattering case in Texas shows how absurd it is. Late last month, Kate Cox, a 31-year-old mother of two, learned that her latest, much-wanted pregnancy was doomed because of a severe genetic disorder. If the pregnancy continued, she was likely to have a stillbirth, and if she didn't, the baby had virtually no chance of surviving long outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 She'd made several trips to the emergency room for severe cramping and what seemed to be leaking amniotic fluid. Her doctor told her that carrying the pregnancy to term could jeopardize her future fertility, and Cox very much wants more children. So she, her husband and her doctor sued the state, seeking a court order to allow her to terminate her pregnancy in Texas. If the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban had workable medical exceptions, it's hard to see how they wouldn't apply to Cox. But it doesn't, and the state attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , fought the Cox family and their doctor every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 This case is unusual for \uc2 Cox\uc2 's willingness to wage a legal fight while suffering a medical catastrophe, but not for the cruel bind the state placed her in. The day Cox received the terrible news about her fetus, the Texas Supreme Court was hearing oral arguments in Zurawski v. Texas, a suit brought by two doctors and 20 women who had been denied medically necessary abortions and were seeking to clarify the scope of emergency exemptions to the state's ban. Among the plaintiffs is Amanda Zurawski, who was 18 weeks pregnant after a year and a half of fertility treatments when her water broke. Although her pregnancy had no chance of surviving, she was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until she became septic. Zurawski ended up spending days in an intensive care unit and has been left with damage to her reproductive tract that will most likely make it harder for her to become pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 Turning to \uc2 Google\uc2  in the midst of her own crisis, Cox learned about the Zurawski case and got in touch with the legal organization representing the 20 women, the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That group quickly filed suit on her behalf, and last week, a Texas judge sided with \uc2 Cox\uc2 , issuing a temporary restraining order that would have allowed her doctor to end the pregnancy without facing criminal or civil penalties. But Paxton appealed the order and warned three hospitals where \uc2 Cox\uc2 's doctor holds admitting privileges not to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  go forward. The restraining order, he wrote, ''will not insulate you, or anyone else, from civil and criminal liability for violating Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, including first-degree felony prosecutions.'' On Friday, Texas' Supreme Court blocked the lower court ruling, pending its decision on the merits of the case.\par \par \uc2 In desperation, Cox finally traveled out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not disclosing where she went. It can't have been easy, and not just because Cox has two young children at home. Even for people with financial resources, it's painful to endure a medical trauma in an unfamiliar place.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  attorney representing Cox, points out, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures at 15 weeks of pregnancy or later are generally done over two days, with patients sent home in between. Most people would rather not endure this process in a hotel room. ''Truly, she just wanted to get health care in Texas,'' Duane said on Friday. But she couldn't. Late on Monday, the Texas Supreme Court declared that Cox wasn't entitled to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and vacated the lower court's order.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it's the clearest message you could have possibly received from an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state that they never meant the medical exemption to mean anything at all,'' Duane said.\par \par \uc2 An irony here is that if the State Supreme Court had allowed Cox to end her pregnancy in Texas, it might have benefited hard-line \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. Were the state to codify clear exemptions for people in extreme medical distress, offering a sliver of mercy to women like Zurawski and Cox, its callous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban might seem slightly more politically palatable. That, after all, is why \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents falsely insist that such clarity already exists.\par \par \uc2 But right-wing politicians and those who support them would rather inflict unimaginable suffering on women than relax the tiniest bit of control over their medical decisions. I asked Duane if any anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups had filed amicus briefs on Cox's behalf. I wasn't surprised that the answer was no.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A23. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231213ejcd00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
