\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Attorney General Tries to Reverse A Court's Decision to Allow an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 1,253 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court, responding to an appeal from Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , put on hold a lower court order allowing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a woman whose fetus has a fatal condition.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court late Friday temporarily halted a lower court order allowing a Dallas woman to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in spite of the state's strict bans, after she learned her fetus has a fatal condition.\par \par \uc2 The state court's ruling was in response to an appeal from Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  of Texas, who opposed the woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court said that, ''without regard to the merits'' of the arguments on either side, it had issued an administrative stay in the case, to give itself more time to issue a final ruling.\par \par \uc2 The stay meant that, for the moment, the order from a judge in Travis County district court permitting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on hold. That order allowed the woman, Kate Cox, to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protected her doctor from civil or criminal liability under Texas's overlapping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 ''We fear that justice delayed will be justice denied,'' said \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , a senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is representing Ms. Cox.\par \par \uc2 The action by the Texas Supreme Court was the latest twist in an unusual saga unfolding over the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, which are some of the strictest in the nation, about what is and what is not permitted under their medical exceptions.\par \par \uc2 In his appeal, Mr. Paxton urged the court to act and wrote that if an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was allowed,\par \par \uc2 ''Nothing can restore the unborn child's life that will be lost as a result.''\par \par \uc2 While the Texas bans allow for exceptions to protect the health and life of a pregnant woman, doctors have said that vague legal language created fear of prosecution and an unwillingness to perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton's filings came hours after district court judge, issued a temporary restraining order barring Mr. Paxton and others from enforcing the state's overlapping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans against Ms. Cox's doctor, Damla Karsan, or anyone who assisted her with providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Ms. Cox.\par \par \uc2 In granting the order, the judge, a Democrat, found that Ms. Cox, 31, a mother of two young children living in the Dallas area, met the criteria for an exception to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Her fetus was diagnosed with trisomy 18, a fatal condition in all but a small number of rare cases; Ms. Cox, who is 20 weeks pregnant, had been to the emergency room several times for pain and discharge during her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, lawyers from the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is also representing Dr. Karsan, filed a response to Mr. Paxton with the state's highest court.\par \par \uc2 ''The State's mandamus petition is stunning in its disregard for Ms. Cox's life, fertility, and the rule of law,'' the lawyers for Ms. Cox wrote. ''Plaintiffs respectfully request that this Court deny the writ and instruct the Attorney General to comply with binding orders from a Texas court.''\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  said a decision could come as soon as later in the day. A ruling would apply only to Ms. Cox and her current pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the Texas Supreme Court has also been considering a broader lawsuit by women and doctors, including Dr. Karsan, and backed by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That suit, Zurawski v. State of Texas, seeks to clarify the medical exemption that would apply across the state. Arguments in that case took place last month.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade last year, the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a political liability for Republicans in many states.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Paxton, a Republican re-elected last year, has relied on strong support from social and religious conservatives, who helped him both return to office for a third term and survive a Republican-led impeachment trial against him.\par \par \uc2 ''The political winds are at his back right now,'' Matt Mackowiak, the head of the Travis County Republican Party, said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton has gained national prominence among hard-right conservative activists and voters for his willingness to use legal action, as well as official opinions and official letters, to advocate for their causes -- including backing the effort to overturn the 2020 presidential election and declaring that certain medical care for transgender youth constitutes ''child abuse.''\par \par \uc2 His appeal to the Texas Supreme Court in Ms. Cox's case followed his letter to three Houston hospitals where he said Dr. Karsan is authorized to admit patients and could perform the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , warning them that the judge's order would not shield them from eventual prosecution or civil lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Dr. Karsan have said in legal filings that she believes her patient's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is medically necessary to preserve her health and future fertility.\par \par \uc2 But in his letter, Mr. Paxton warned the order would not constrain state officials or private citizens from filing criminal or civil lawsuits against the hospital or others, such as Ms. Cox's husband, who might help her obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He reiterated that position in his filings to the Texas Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ''Nothing will prevent enforcement of Texas's civil and criminal penalties once the T.R.O. erroneously prohibiting enforcement is vacated,'' the filings from his office read.\par \par \uc2 Two of the hospitals targeted by Mr. Paxton in his letter did not respond to a request for comment. A spokeswoman for a third hospital confirmed that Dr. Karsan had admitting privileges but said that the hospital was ''not involved in this case.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton's letter, and his subsequent legal filings, appeared to thrust Ms. Cox, her doctor and others involved in her care back into the same state of uncertainty and fear of prosecution that had prompted the initial lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The letter puts Dr. Karsan ''in an awful position,'' said Judy Levison, a obstetrician-gynecologist in Houston who has known Dr. Karsan for years. ''They named her and so, it's intimidating,'' she added. ''It's trying to intimidate somebody to not act.''\par \par \uc2 In Texas, the attorney general does not have the ability to bring criminal charges directly under the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and must instead rely on local district attorneys to do so.\par \par \uc2 No doctors or providers have been prosecuted for performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas, and only a very small number of civil lawsuits have been filed under a 2021 state law, Senate Bill 8, that allowed for lawsuits against those who assist with abortions.\par \par \uc2 In a few cases, doctors have gone forward with abortions after determining they were necessary and permitted under the law.\par \par \uc2 ''There are hospitals in Texas similar to this where abortions have been done, and hospitals have supported their doctors, and it hasn't been in the public eye,'' Dr. Levison said.\par \par \uc2 Through the first nine months of the year, Texas recorded 34 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures across the state, according to state health statistics. In 2020, before the first of the severe state restrictions went into place, there were more than 56,000.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Ken Paxton's move is the latest twist in an unusual saga unfolding in Texas over the state's \uc2  bans, which are some of the strictest in the nation. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIC GAY/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231209ejc900044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
