\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Gets Approval, In a Rare Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 1,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A state court judge said a woman whose fetus was diagnosed with a fatal condition could legally obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the state's bans.\par \par \uc2 A Texas judge granted a request on Thursday to allow an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the state's strict bans, ruling in the case of a pregnant woman whose fetus was diagnosed with a fatal condition.\par \par \uc2 The case is believed to be among the first attempts in the nation to seek a court-approved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade last year and allowed states to enact their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Maya Guerra Gamble of Travis County district court, sided with the woman, Kate Cox, who is 20 weeks pregnant, and issued a temporary restraining order to permit her doctor to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without facing civil or criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 The judge, a Democrat, agreed with Ms. Cox's lawyers that the procedure was necessary to protect Ms. Cox from a potentially dangerous birth, and to preserve her future fertility.\par \par \uc2 ''The idea that Ms. Cox wants desperately to be pregnant, and this law might actually cause her to lose that ability, is shocking, and would be a genuine miscarriage of justice,'' the judge said at the conclusion of a roughly 30-minute video hearing. ''So I will be signing the order, and it will be processed and sent out today.''\par \par \uc2 The ruling applies only to Ms. Cox, though it represents another front in an effort to force Texas, which bans most abortions from conception, to allow abortions under the medical exceptions to its prohibitions. A separate lawsuit, brought by a group of Texas women who say they were denied abortions under state law, asks the state to clarify the conditions in which medical exceptions would apply.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court eliminated the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, more than a dozen conservative states have enacted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or severely restricted the procedure. The bans generally allow limited medical exceptions. In some of those states, women represented by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have sued to clarify when the procedure could be performed, or to have the bans overturned.\par \par \uc2 Those suits were filed after women were denied abortions. In some cases, the women claimed that they suffered harm to their health as a result, or were forced to leave the state, at significant cost, disruption or risk, to seek abortions elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 What made Ms. Cox's case different was that she sought a court order while still pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cox's fetus was found to have trisomy 18, a genetic condition that in all but very rare cases leads to miscarriage or stillbirth, or to the infant's death within the first year. Her lawyers said she had visited the emergency room four times because of pain and discharge -- including once after her suit was filed on Tuesday -- but that doctors had told her that under Texas law, she had to continue her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cox, 31, could be seen wiping away tears from her eyes as she watched the judge issue the decision in the video proceeding with her husband, Justin. She said in an interview on Tuesday that she and her husband, who live in the Dallas area and have two young children, hoped for a big family and never planned on having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general's office, which argued on Thursday against granting the order, could seek the intervention of a higher court.\par \par \uc2 After the ruling, Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  sent a letter to top hospital officials in Houston, where Ms. Cox's doctor practices, saying she and hospital staff could still face criminal and civil penalties, despite the judge's order.\par \par \uc2 ''The T.R.O. will not insulate you, or anyone else,'' Mr. Paxton wrote, adding that it would expire ''long before the statute of limitations for violating Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws expires.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton's letter contrasted with the order issued by the judge, which barred the state from enforcing its laws against Ms. Cox's doctor, Damla Karsan, as well as anyone else involved in the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Marc Hearron of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and one of Ms. Cox's lawyers said that Mr. Paxton was misrepresenting the judge's order. ''He is trying to bulldoze the legal system to make sure Kate and pregnant women like her continue to suffer,'' he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a political liability for Republicans nationally. But Mr. Paxton was recently re-elected to a third term with the backing of hard right Republicans in Texas, where the Republican primary remains the critical election contest for statewide offices.\par \par \uc2 Texas is at the forefront of states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has three overlapping bans that outlaw abortions from the moment of fertilization, and allow private citizens to sue others who help a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The laws provide for some limited exceptions to save the health and life of a pregnant woman. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates argue that those provisions are unclear and put women with pregnancy complications at risk.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court, the state's highest civil court, is currently weighing a broader effort by doctors, women and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates to clarify the medical exceptions under the law in a separate case, Zurawski v. State of Texas, brought by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of the lawyers in that case, \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , also represents Ms. Cox. In the court hearing on Thursday, Ms. Duane argued that her client qualified for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the state bans' medical exceptions and asked the court to bar the state from enforcing the bans against Dr. Karsan, so that the doctor could proceed with the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without fear of punishment.\par \par \uc2 A doctor convicted of performing an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas can face a prison sentence of up to 99 years and fines of at least $100,000.\par \par \uc2 Johnathan Stone, a lawyer for the Texas attorney general's office, argued that Ms. Cox's pregnancy did not meet the ''elements of the medical exception to Texas's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws,'' citing a different doctor's assessment of her condition.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Duane said that the state's argument underscored the confusion over what standard would be used to assess a doctor's determination that a patient meets the medical exception, leading many doctors to avoid performing abortions at all.\par \par \uc2 The issue, Ms. Duane said, is that while the law allows narrow medical exceptions, ''no one knows what it means and the state won't tell us.''\par \par \uc2 In her order, the judge found that Ms. Cox's doctor ''believes in good faith, exercising her medical judgment,'' that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is medically recommended and that the medical exception ''permits an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ms. Cox's circumstances.''\par \par \uc2 The judge said her order would protect Ms. Cox's doctor and other workers at the hospital who would be involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure, as well as Mr. Cox, all of whom might otherwise face legal liability under the Texas bans.\par \par \uc2 Before the ruling, those against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they opposed the effort to interpret the law to permit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ms. Cox's case.\par \par \uc2 Amy O'Donnell, the communications director for Texas Alliance for Life, said that while it is ''heartbreaking to hear of any family facing a tragic diagnosis for their unborn child'' the group ''does not support taking the life of an unborn child because of a life-limiting or fatal diagnosis.''\par \par \uc2 A Travis County judge ruled that a Texas woman who is 20 weeks pregnant could get an \uc2  without facing penalties. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SUZANNE CORDEIRO/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) (A15) This article appeared in print on page A1, A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231208ejc80003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
