\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Pregnant Woman Sues Texas to Allow Her Exception to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. David Goodman \par \uc2 1,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A woman who is 20 weeks pregnant, and whose fetus has been diagnosed with a deadly condition, is suing for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a medical exception to the state's bans.\par \par \uc2 A pregnant Texas woman whose fetus has a fatal condition sued the state on Tuesday seeking an emergency court order to allow her doctor to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite the state's strict bans on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit is believed to be one of the first attempts in the nation to seek a court-ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, allowing states to make their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Texas is at the forefront of states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has overlapping bans that outlaw abortions from the point of fertilization and allow private citizens to sue others who might help a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The laws allow some exceptions to save the health and life of the pregnant woman, though \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates argue that they are unclear, putting women with pregnancy complications at risk.\par \par \uc2 The vagueness of the exceptions in Texas have prevented doctors from performing the procedure in most cases, lawyers for the woman, Kate Cox, have argued. A decision in her case could force the state to more clearly define what is allowed under the law.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cox is 20 weeks pregnant, and her fetus has been diagnosed with trisomy 18, a condition that in all but very rare cases leads to miscarriage, stillbirth or the death of the infant within the first year after birth. Despite trips to the emergency room for pain and discharge, Ms. Cox, 31, a mother of two young children who lives in the Dallas area, has been told by doctors that she is required to continue her pregnancy under Texas law.\par \par \uc2 ''Essentially, it was, as long as the baby has a heartbeat, there was nothing you could do in Texas,'' said Ms. Cox in an interview on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Her emergency appeal comes as the Texas Supreme Court is weighing a broad effort by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and women who have been denied abortions to compel state officials to clarify the medical situations in which doctors can perform abortions without fear of civil or criminal penalties. That suit was filed in March by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is also representing Ms. Cox.\par \par \uc2 A district court judge ruled in their favor over the summer, shielding doctors from prosecution for providing abortions under a range of circumstances, including when pregnancy complications create a health risk, or when the fetus is unlikely to survive. But the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , appealed, putting the district court ruling on hold for now. The case, Zurawski v. State of Texas, could be decided by the state's highest civil court in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 But unlike that court proceeding, which seeks a ruling to apply broadly to future medically necessary abortions in Texas, Ms. Cox is currently pregnant and asking the court to intervene immediately to allow an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in her case.\par \par \uc2 Such cases have been extremely rare. Mary Ziegler, a professor and legal historian at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , School of Law said she was not aware of a similar case being brought since the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. ''Usually the stories of court ordered abortions were people who did not want abortions,'' she said, citing past cases. ''Women or pregnant people pursuing relief in this way are pretty unusual.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cox's lawyer, \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , said she was not aware of another suit like hers filed.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not so easy, in the middle of a health care crisis, to reach out to a lawyer and file a lawsuit,'' she said. Ms. Cox contacted the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  after seeing news about arguments in the Zurawski case on the same day as she received her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cox's suit is seeking to protect her doctor, Damla Karsan, from prosecution, as well as her husband, Justin, who could face civil liability under the state's 2021 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction, known as Senate Bill 8, which allows private citizens to sue those who have assisted others in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after detection of fetal cardiac activity, usually around six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The state also has a trigger ban, which went into effect after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision, and a separate, more-than-a-century-old \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that Mr. Paxton has said he would also enforce.\par \par \uc2 The suit was filed in Travis County district court against the state of Texas, Mr. Paxton and the Texas Medical Board.\par \par \uc2 ''Our contention is that Kate falls within the medical exemption, but her doctors can't proceed and give her the medical care that she needs unless and until a court agrees,'' Ms. Duane said. ''Otherwise,'' she added, ''her doctor will risk life in prison, loss of her medical license and hundreds of thousands of dollars in fines.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton's office and the medical board did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ms. Cox said that she and her husband had been excited at the prospect of a third child joining their 3-year-old daughter and 1-year-old son. The couple only glancingly followed the rapidly shifting legal landscape around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas as Roe was overturned and bans went into effect.\par \par \uc2 ''I thought I would never need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said. ''I want a big family.''\par \par \uc2 ''We knew \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal in Texas,'' Mr. Cox said, but not how limited the exceptions were. ''We had no idea the scope of that.''\par \par \uc2 The diagnosis of trisomy 18 was devastating, Ms. Cox said, because it meant that the fetus would not survive until birth, or, if carried to term, the infant would die within a short time. That feeling was compounded, she said, by her inability to end the pregnancy despite her doctors telling her that continuing it could endanger her own health. Ms. Cox delivered her previous pregnancies by C-section, and doctors have told her that the risk of scarring from a third C-section could threaten her chances of having another child.\par \par \uc2 ''It's deep sadness,'' she said. ''I feel like I should not have to put my body through the risks of continuing this pregnancy through a childbirth and induction with a chance of uterine rupture, a third C-section.''\par \par \uc2 Texas officials, including Gov. Greg Abbott and other proponents of the state's strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, have said that the bans already allow for doctors to perform abortions in cases where the mother's life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 But, the suit argues, state officials have not clarified what sorts of situations would constitute such a risk, leaving doctors -- who must record every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  they perform under the exception -- fearful of going outside of its bounds and facing serious penalties, which include prison sentences of up to 99 years and fines of at least $100,000.\par \par \uc2 According to the suit, during the Zurawski case, lawyers for the state said that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be performed if a patient had received a fatal fetal diagnosis and was facing serious health risks, as the suit argues is the case with Ms. Cox. ''But the state defendants have never said what evidence nor what condition would be enough,'' according to the complaint, discouraging action by the doctors.\par \par \uc2 State officials have argued that women who do not receive proper care -- such as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  permitted under the exceptions -- could sue their doctors. But Ms. Cox said she felt well cared for by her doctor, and stymied by the state.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kate Zernike contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights march near the State Capitol in Austin, Texas, last year. The state bars abortions at the point of fertilization. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231206ejc60003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
