\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Republicans Are Finding Out That 'Pro-Life' Has Too Many Meanings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Liz Mair \par \uc2 1,206 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 24\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Electoral results since the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision should tell a lot of people in the Republican Party something they absolutely do not want to hear: Even rank-and-file G.O.P. voters are not as pro-life as we might have thought when Roe v. Wade was the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 That trend was confirmed last month in Ohio -- the latest sign that the Republican Party needs to figure out a new way of addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many conservatives may call themselves pro-life, but in practice, that may be a more aspirational statement than an accurate reflection of hard policy views. Perhaps by figuring out what it now means to be pro-life -- and recognizing that pro-life policy is easiest to sell only when it amounts to a ban on abortions later in pregnancy -- Republicans can come up with a new approach to the politics of the issue.\par \par \uc2 Before Roe was overturned, the term ''pro-life'' covered a lot of ground -- which was useful over decades in galvanizing a broad coalition willing to use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a political cudgel. As Republicans are finding out today, ''pro-life'' means many things to many people.\par \par \uc2 Depending on which pro-lifer you talk to, ''pro-life" could mean believing Roe was incorrectly decided and that under a correct interpretation of the Constitution, states were free to enact anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws -- though many states would not, and that was fine.\par \par \uc2 Or it could mean believing this but also being determined and committed to working to pass laws in every state banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly with multiple exceptions. Or it could mean believing Roe was wrongly decided and that federal law or the Constitution (or both) should ban abortions, perhaps with exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Or it could mean being pro-Roe but at the same time anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or it could mean strictly opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second and third trimesters, with only cursory concern about Roe.\par \par \uc2 Understanding that ''pro-life'' can mean a variety of things should inform the way Republicans approach this issue. Right now, when many voters -- again, even Republican voters -- hear the term ''pro-life,'' their brains process it as denoting an extreme position. Maybe they think of states like Alabama, Arkansas and Oklahoma, which have imposed near-complete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 This trend -- in which ''pro-life'' equals ''extreme'' -- is what Senator J.D. Vance of Ohio pointed to in explaining why voters in the state resoundingly approved a ballot measure enshrining a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state Constitution. As he put it, the pro-life side got clobbered because voters disliked both options, but they particularly disliked the state's pro-life so-called heartbeat bill, which made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal beyond about six weeks of pregnancy, and voted to keep some forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Vance posted on social media, ''We have to recognize how much voters mistrust us (meaning elected Republicans) on this issue.''\par \par \uc2 He wrote: ''Giving up on the unborn is not an option. It's politically dumb and morally repugnant.'' He's still for advocacy that will change hearts and minds and private opinions that could, in time, result in changes in policy views.\par \par \uc2 But he also recognizes political reality. In that regard, he explained that a position like that taken up by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has to become the G.O.P. default as a matter of policy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump calls himself pro-life: He appointed three pro-life justices to overturn Roe. But he also avoided taking a strong position on, for example, a 15-week federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and has harshly criticized the six-week ban in Florida that Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed into law, calling it ''a terrible thing and a terrible mistake.'' And he has said that ''exceptions are very important.'' (In recent years he has identified three exceptions: rape, incest and protecting the life of the pregnant woman.)\par \par \uc2 This is one area where Republicans' being so enraptured by Mr. Trump might actually come in handy for them. Echoing what he says on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be salable in such a party, and unlike, say, the 2020 stolen-election proposition, it might actually be a winning message -- or at least a less-losing message.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, it's the only potentially favorable path G.O.P. candidates have as they head into 2024. Not for nothing, but Mr. Trump, with his squishy \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position, is running well ahead of the rest of the field.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley has also been squishy. She often calls herself ''unapologetically pro-life.'' But she has also experimented with explaining her position in terms that conservatives, including staunch pro-lifers, find consistent and comprehensible, even if they disagree. As she put it at a debate in November, Roe was a bad decision, and federalism -- letting each state decide its own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, as Ohio just did -- is good.\par \par \uc2 Now, this certainly won't satisfy Republicans who really believe in the Alabama-Arkansas-Oklahoma position. And it's not clear that it will be magically successful in the near term, unless the G.O.P. can get all -- or even many -- candidates who use the pro-life label to sing from the same hymn sheet.\par \par \uc2 It's also unlikely that the party is going to take the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  off the table entirely, as European conservatives (broadly) have. There is still too big a part of the Republican base that cares deeply about the issue.\par \par \uc2 But the party can triage. Focus first on a broadly winning position, which is banning later abortions. And at least for a few years, drop the legislating, especially where it gets into Ohio-like terrain. That is what the pro-life movement will be left with having to do anyway. If the Trump-Vance scenario comes to pass, the pro-life movement will be left with having to change hearts and minds to affect individual behavior based on (shifted) individual opinion rather than focusing mostly on changing laws.\par \par \uc2 Curiously, though it has been overlooked by many people in the party, it is what \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the last Republican president before Mr. Trump, advocated when Roe was still the law of the land. In 2005, Mr. Bush, much more in line with Mr. Vance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than Mr. Trump, said: ''We will work with decency and respect to change hearts and minds, one person at a time. In doing so, we will build a lasting culture of life.''\par \par \uc2 That will need to be the focus for firmly pro-life Americans again.\par \par \uc2 Liz Mair (@LizMair) has served as a campaign strategist for Scott Walker, \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2 , \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , Carly Fiorina and Rick Perry. She is the founder and president of Mair Strategies.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A24. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231206ejc600035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
