\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Red-State Senate Democrats Test  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Issue\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael C. Bender and Anjali Huynh \par \uc2 1,663 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  is betting that the issue will aid his re-election bid in Ohio, which recently upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Allies of Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  of Montana are also hoping it helps.\par \par \uc2 In the opening minutes of a debate during \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 's successful 2006 campaign for Senate, the Republican incumbent attacked him over ''partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' a phrase often weaponized by conservatives at the time to paint Democrats as somewhere between immoral and murderous.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown, a Democrat from northeast Ohio in the House at the time, glanced at his notes. He opposed ''late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he said in a measured voice. He denounced the mere idea that Congress would limit any procedure that could ''save a woman's health.''\par \par \uc2 With that, he quickly pivoted. Mr. Brown used the rest of his time to burnish his political brand as a blue-collar economic populist.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 18 years later, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will again be a central point of contention as Senator Brown fights for re-election against one of three Republicans trying to unseat him next year. One difference, other than that his shaggy dark hair is now shaded with gray, is that he is preparing to fully lean into his defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''This issue's not going away,'' Mr. Brown said in an interview. ''Women don't trust Republicans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they won't for the foreseeable future -- and they're not going to trust these guys running against me.''\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has become an invaluable political asset for Democrats. They have leveraged the issue to hold onto control of the Senate, limit losses in the House and, this month, fuel victories in key state races across the Midwest and the South.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps the toughest test for the issue's power will come in Senate contests like Mr. Brown's in Ohio and Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 's in Montana. The fate of the razor-thin Democratic majority in the chamber could well be sealed in those two places, by the same voters who have installed Republicans in every other statewide office.\par \par \uc2 So far, voters even in conservative states have consistently prioritized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections over their partisanship. That was true last year in Kansas, where 59 percent of voters rejected a measure to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections from the State Constitution, and again this month in Ohio, where 57 percent of voters agreed to enshrine such rights in their Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The open question is whether Mr. Brown, 71, and Mr. Tester, 67, can maintain their invaluable political personas while -- for the first time in their lengthy careers in public office -- persuading their constituents to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights front and center when voting next year.\par \par \uc2 Both Democrats have long supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but their electoral successes trace back to carefully tailored campaigns that catered to local issues over dominant national ones like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That individuality was how both men won re-election in 2018, even though their states voted for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 For Mr. Tester, this has meant campaigning on policies he has focused on in the Senate, where he serves on committees overseeing agricultural, Native American and veterans issues.\par \par \uc2 His first television ads this campaign strike similar tones. One features Mr. Tester -- a paunchy former schoolteacher with a flattop haircut and a left hand missing three fingers from a boyhood accident with a meat grinder -- describing himself as both physically and philosophically different from his congressional colleagues.\par \par \uc2 ''I may not look like the other senators,'' Mr. Tester says, ''but that's not stopping me from making Washington understand what makes Montana so special.''\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Mr. Brown has built his reputation on middle-class economic issues, including fighting corporate tax breaks and the high cost of health care. In a 2004 book, ''Myths of Free Trade: Why America Trade Policy Has Failed,'' he argued that unregulated trade deals had reopened the country's class divide.\par \par \uc2 This year, Mr. Brown's campaign has already released a video attacking his three potential Republican challengers as extreme on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Montana, the Democratic Party has taken a similar approach on behalf of Mr. Tester.\par \par \uc2 ''The thing I think a lot of people miss with Sherrod is that he knows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an economic issue,'' said \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2 , a Democratic former mayor of Dayton, Ohio, who ran for governor last year. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access maybe wasn't discussed as much in previous campaigns, but that's because it was before the fall of Roe.''\par \par \uc2 The task for the two Democrats will be complicated by a political headwind that neither senator has confronted: seeking re-election on a ballot topped by an unpopular president from their own party.\par \par \uc2 Both first won election to the Senate by unseating incumbents in 2006, when discontent over the Iraq war and Republican corruption scandals helped Democrats make gains in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Each was re-elected in 2012, when Democrats scored huge majorities from Black and Hispanic voters as President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  won a second term. They won again in 2018, a Democratic wave year propelled by opposition to Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are already trying to massage their message on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The National Republican Senatorial Committee is coaching candidates to oppose a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and to clearly state their support for exceptions when it comes to rape, incest or a woman's health.\par \par \uc2 But not all Republicans are on board, as the party's Senate primary race in Ohio shows. One top candidate, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , the Ohio secretary of state, has supported a national ban and opposed exceptions for rape and incest -- and also unsuccessfully campaigned against the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot question.\par \par \uc2 Another contender, Bernie Moreno, a businessman seeking his first elected office, has said he supports exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the woman, but he told a reporter from Breitbart News last year that he did not. He has also expressed support for a 15-week federal ban.\par \par \uc2 The third leading candidate, \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2 , a state senator, opposed the state's constitutional amendment this month, but he has a more moderate record on the issue than his opponents. Mr. Dolan opposes a national ban, though he has said he is willing to reconsider that stance. He has criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban proposals in Ohio that haven't included the three main exceptions.\par \par \uc2 ''Most Americans agree there should be reasonable limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy will primarily be made at the state level,'' Mr. Dolan said in a statement, adding that Mr. Brown held ''extreme'' views on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans have said that Ohio's ballot referendum means the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will have less urgency in the state next year. But Democrats contend that Republican support for a federal ban would help keep the issue alive, arguing that such a measure would undermine the will of Ohio voters.\par \par \uc2 A poll commissioned by the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee recommended that messaging focus on G.O.P. support for a ''national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban'' and that politicians should not be involved in ''personal medical decisions.'' \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have encouraged candidates to simultaneously adopt a ''proactive'' platform that calls for expanding access to contraception and maternal health resources while highlighting Republican involvement in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''Campaigns need to quickly define who the villains are here: Republicans overturned Roe, Republicans have been campaigning against Roe for decades, Republicans have been pledging to create a court that would overturn Roe,'' said Mini Timmaraju, the president of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , one of the country's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups. ''They got it, they did it, they're responsible. Pin it on them. Do not flinch.''\par \par \uc2 Neither Mr. Brown nor Mr. Tester has been shy about supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tester campaigned in 2018 with Cecile Richards, who had recently stepped down as the president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . He said recently that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had clear resonance in Montana, where libertarian-leaning voters tend to reject perceived government intrusion.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Tester has mostly tailored his campaigns around issues closer to the Continental Divide in his state than the partisan divide in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown won his first political office in 1974, the year after Roe v. Wade was decided. He has proudly highlighted his 100 percent voting score from \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  and \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''My focus has always been on civil rights and women's rights,'' he said. ''That leads to a better economy, too -- when women have better access to child care and can make decisions for their families.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown was involved in the campaign this year to support the constitutional amendment on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , phone-banking alongside the Ohio Democratic Party and frequently bringing up the measure during campaign events.\par \par \uc2 Hours after Ohioans voted, Mr. Brown posted a video on social media that framed his three potential Republican challengers as sitting on the wrong side of the issue. ''All of my opponents would support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,'' the caption read.\par \par \uc2 If there was any doubt, Mr. Brown made clear in the interview that he saw the political benefit of the issue.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, ''will surely be talked about more than in my other races.''\par \par \uc2 Senator Sherrod Brown of Ohio is seen as one of the most vulnerable Democrats in the chamber, with three Republicans vying to unseat him next year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY T.J. KIRKPATRICK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Frank LaRose, secretary of state of Ohio, has backed a national \uc2  ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK FANCHER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Matt Dolan, a state senator in Ohio, opposes a national \uc2  ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DUSTIN FRANZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Senator Jon Tester of Montana has often focused on local issues, rather than dominant national ones like \uc2 . This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231130ejbu00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
