\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 After Ohio's Vote, Hope in Florida for a Ballot Measure Protecting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 1,308 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A coalition of groups collecting petition signatures for a ballot protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights says its fund-raising got a boost after the Ohio results.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade last year, campaigns to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have galvanized voters in state after state. It has become Democrats' most successful issue ahead of an uncertain 2024 election cycle -- and their biggest hope, especially after voters in Ohio approved on Tuesday a measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 That triumphant streak has propelled campaigns for similar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in swing, or potentially swing, states, including Arizona, Florida, Nevada and Pennsylvania. But none might be as tantalizing a prize as Florida, which has moved increasingly out of Democrats' grasp in electoral contests.\par \par \uc2 But getting a question on next year's ballot in the state is hardly guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 Like in Ohio, Florida's government is controlled by Republicans. Also like Ohio, Florida has put in place a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, with its enactment pending approval by the state's Supreme Court. (That case centers on Florida's existing 15-week ban, but affirming that restriction would then trigger the six-week ban approved by Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in April.)\par \par \uc2 The parallels between the two states give Florida organizers hope for success, despite steep obstacles that include a court review of the proposed ballot measure and a costly petition-gathering process. If voters in Florida get to weigh in on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  question, organizers say, they too are likely to want to protect their rights.\par \par \uc2 ''Florida has always been a deeply libertarian state,'' said Anna Hochkammer, executive director of the Florida Women's Freedom Coalition. '''Find your tribe, find your people, live your life -- we'll leave you alone.' It's part of Floridian culture. And Floridians reject outright that the government should be involved in these decisions. It is deeply offensive to Floridians' sense of independence and freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Since June 2022, when Roe was overturned, states have given voters a direct say on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, either to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, weaken them or explicitly exclude them from state constitutions. Kansas, Kentucky and Michigan all voted to expand or maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, a coalition of groups under the umbrella organization of Floridians Protecting Freedom, including \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , has collected a little more than half of the nearly 900,000 petition signatures it needs for a ballot measure that aims to limit ''government interference with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' before a fetus is considered viable, which is often around 24 weeks of pregnancy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal up to 24 weeks in Florida until last year.\par \par \uc2 The coalition had collected about $9 million by the end of September but says its next report will show that more than $12 million has been raised. Most donations have come from Florida, with limited interest so far from the out-of-state donors who propelled campaigns in Ohio and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The coalition raised more than $300,000 on Wednesday after the Ohio victory, Ms. Hochkammer said, with more people clicking through the group's fund-raising emails or taking calls.\par \par \uc2 ''The phones started ringing, and pledges started coming in,'' she said. ''I think that there were a lot of people that were sitting on their money, waiting to see what happened in Ohio. And we had a great day.''\par \par \uc2 Among the places where volunteers and paid petition-gatherers have found eager supporters are screenings of the ''Barbie'' movie and the Taylor Swift Eras Tour movie, both of which have feminism as a key theme and strong female leads, said Laura Goodhue, executive director of the Florida Alliance of Planned Parenthood Affiliates.\par \par \uc2 The coalition still needs to collect -- and the state must validate -- about 400,000 more signatures by Feb. 1, a difficult and expensive task.\par \par \uc2 The ballot language must also be approved by the conservative-leaning Florida Supreme Court. The state's Republican attorney general, \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2 , announced a challenge to the measure last month.\par \par \uc2 She and several groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have argued that the measure is too broad, vague and misleading. Florida requires that ballot questions be clear and limited to a single subject.\par \par \uc2 ''This effort to hoodwink the Florida electorate should be rebuffed,'' Ms. Moody wrote in a legal brief filed Oct. 31.\par \par \uc2 The ballot question, which would include a summary of the amendment that would be added to the State Constitution if the initiative passes, would read in part, ''No law shall prohibit, penalize, delay or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability or when necessary to protect the patient's health, as determined by the patient's health care provider.'' The question does not adequately define ''viability''; whether ''the patient's health'' would include mental health; and who would be considered a ''health care provider,'' Ms. Moody argued.\par \par \uc2 ''It's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand for any reason,'' said John Stemberger, president of the Florida Family Policy Council, a conservative Christian group. ''It's not only extreme, but it's deceptive -- and that's a problem.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stemberger said there was ''a very good chance'' that the State Supreme Court, whose ideological balance has shifted from liberal to conservative, could strike down the amendment. If not, his organization and others have already formed a political committee, Florida Voters Against Extremism, to prepare for a campaign.\par \par \uc2 ''Ohio is just a reminder that we still have a lot of work to do,'' he said. ''We have to go back to the drawing board and explain to people why unborn children are valuable, why adoption is always the better option.''\par \par \uc2 Unlike in Ohio, where protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights passed with about 57 percent of the vote, Florida requires citizen-led ballot initiatives to obtain more than 60 percent of the vote to pass. A \uc2 University of North Florida\uc2  poll found last year that 60 percent of residents opposed the 15-week ban after they were told that it does not include exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochkammer said the coalition's polling suggested that more than 70 percent of Floridians supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure, including 64 percent of Republicans and 57 percent of voters who supported former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Florida voters have tended to support ballot measures championing liberal causes, even while also electing Republican leaders who in many cases later watered down or undermined the implementation of those measures once in office.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, Florida was considered the nation's largest presidential battleground, with elections decided by tiny margins and former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  winning the state twice. But Republicans have been making gains: Mr. Trump won by more than three percentage points in 2020, and Governor DeSantis by 19 points, a landslide, last year.\par \par \uc2 Still, significant citizen-led constitutional measures have done well once they have overcome the hurdles to make it onto the ballot.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, voters backed a $15 hourly minimum wage -- and Mr. Trump. In 2018, they voted to restore felons' voting rights -- and for Mr. DeSantis. In 2016, they voted to legalize medical marijuana -- and for Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''We are not a deeply conservative, extremist state,'' Ms. Hochkammer said. ''We are a deeply gerrymandered state, and the fact that our divided election results have been skewing a certain way should not mislead people about what the political appetite is in Florida.''\par \par \uc2 Ohio voters on Tuesday approved by double digits a ballot measure enshrining a right to \uc2  in the state's Constitution. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); State Senator Lori Berman, Democrat of Florida, voiced her opposition to the state's near total \uc2  ban in March. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALICIA DEVINE/TALLAHASSEE DEMOCRAT, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A22. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231112ejbc0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
