\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 After Off-Year Losses, Some G.O.P. Lawmakers Balk at  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Curbs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni and Catie Edmondson \par \uc2 1,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Worried about alienating critical blocs of voters, House Republicans from competitive districts are digging in against using spending bills for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Two days after Republicans across the country suffered a drubbing, dragged down by their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the off-year elections, G.O.P. leaders on Capitol Hill appeared not to have gotten the memo.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans tried on Thursday to use a financial services spending bill to chip away at a District of Columbia law aimed at protecting employees from being discriminated against for seeking contraceptive or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. Tucked inside the otherwise dry bill was a line barring federal funds from being used to enforce that law.\par \par \uc2 But minutes ahead of an expected vote, Republicans were forced to pull the legislation from the floor. Mainstream G.O.P. lawmakers from competitive districts -- concerned that their party's opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has alienated women -- appeared unwilling to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related restriction, sapping the measure of the votes necessary to pass.\par \par \uc2 It was the latest reflection of the deep divisions among Republicans that have prevented them, for the moment, from coalescing around a strategy for averting a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But this time, it was also an illustration of yet another disconnect -- between a small group of Republicans in Congress who are trying to pivot away from an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  message that voters have rejected and a much larger coalition, including the party's leaders, who are doubling down.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's election results drove home to some Republicans in Congress what they already know and fear -- that their party has alienated critical blocs of voters with its policies and message, particularly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the results stiffened their resolve to resist such measures, even if it means breaking with the party at a critical time in a high-stakes fight over federal spending.\par \par \uc2 ''The American people are speaking very clearly: There is no appetite for national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law,'' Representative John Duarte of California, a Republican who represents a district that President Biden won in 2020, said on Thursday. ''And there's enough of us in the Republican Party that are going to stand against it.''\par \par \uc2 Given Republicans' tiny majority, which allows them to lose only four votes on their side if all Democrats show up and unite in opposition, that resistance could be decisive. Between mainstream Republicans' resistance to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision in the financial services bill and rising discontent among the hard-right flank that the legislation did not include a measure barring funding for a new F.B.I. building, it became clear the bill did not have the votes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Duarte said he and other more center-leaning Republicans had warned party leaders that they would be inclined to oppose other spending bills that contained ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language not core to a bill.'' He said he would prefer that those provisions be pulled out of the spending bills and voted on separately.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2 , a Pennsylvania Republican who also represents a district that Mr. Biden won in 2020, told reporters that he, too, had opposed the financial services bill because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related language.\par \par \uc2 The rare pushback from members who represent the political middle of the Republican conference came two days after Ohio voters resoundingly approved a ballot measure enshrining a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The message that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains the most potent political issue for Democrats was clear even where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself was not on the ballot. In Kentucky, Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , a Democrat, rode to victory after criticizing his Republican opponent's defense of the state's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And in Virginia, legislative candidates who opposed the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban proposed by Gov. Glenn Youngkin, a Republican, prevailed.\par \par \uc2 In the House, however, gerrymandering has made most Republican seats so safe that lawmakers routinely cater to the far-right wing of their party, and a slim majority has given hard-right lawmakers outsized influence. The result has been that House Republicans continue to draft legislation that is out of step with a vast majority of voters, including some of their own constituents, on social issues.\par \par \uc2 That has forced Republicans from competitive districts to take politically perilous votes that many of them fear will cost them their seats, as well as the House majority, next year.\par \par \uc2 In September, Representative \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , one of six New York Republicans who represent districts that Mr. Biden won in 2020, objected to an agriculture spending bill because it included language that would restrict access to mifepristone, a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 That measure, which would fund the Agriculture Department and the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , ultimately collapsed on the House floor when other Republicans joined Mr. Molinaro in opposing it because of that specific restriction.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had already swung into action to hammer Republicans on the issue. After the legislation was approved by the Appropriations Committee, the House Democrats' campaign arm accused five vulnerable Republicans on the panel who voted to advance the bill of ''putting the health and livelihoods of countless women at risk.''\par \par \uc2 Then, after the bill failed on the floor, the House Democrats' main super PAC hammered politically vulnerable Republicans who supported it, calling them ''anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Mr. Molinaro was part of the small group of Republicans who balked at supporting the financial services bill because of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  language tucked inside.\par \par \uc2 ''There are approximately five to eight who aren't supportive because of these provisions,'' Mr. Molinaro said. ''We must respect and love women faced with such difficult choices.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Molinaro said he opposed a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While he noted that he was against late-term abortions, he said he did not want to impose any further \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at the federal level -- including through spending bills.\par \par \uc2 ''My constituents have reinforced my view, and results in Ohio may well confirm a position for that state,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 Representative Nancy Mace, Republican of South Carolina, has long railed against her party for not doing enough to show compassion to women. She has said that G.O.P. leaders are making Republicans like her from moderate districts ''walk the plank'' with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  votes. Ms. Mace said on Thursday that she was part of the group of lawmakers Mr. Molinaro was referring to who would not support spending bills that quietly tried to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't save lives, if we can't win elections,'' Ms. Mace posted on X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , on Tuesday night as the election results became clear. ''We need to talk about common sense \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, while also promoting expanded access to contraception including over the counter.''\par \par \uc2 Still, there are major minefields ahead. Senior House appropriators are planning as soon as next week to bring up the bill that funds the Labor Department and the Department of Heath and Human Services, which includes multiple anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures. Democrats argue those measures are aimed at defunding \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and making funding for Title X, the nation's family planning program, less accessible. The legislation also would target programs that provide referrals or information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the bill does not single out \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  by name, it includes a provision that would bar sending federal funds to ''community providers'' that are ''primarily engaged in family planning services, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and related medical care.'' It includes exceptions for abortions performed in the case of rape or incest, or in instances in which the mother's life is endangered.\par \par \uc2 It is exactly the type of legislation that mainstream Republicans like Mr. Duarte are warning against.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of us in swing districts -- a lot of us that want to be very respectful of where the American people are and aren't on these social issues -- are standing our ground,'' Mr. Duarte said.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of a ballot measure enshrining a right to \uc2  in Ohio's Constitution after voters resoundingly approved the measure on Tuesday, left. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231111ejbb0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
