\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Night of Insults,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and a Girlfriend\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Goldmacher, Jonathan Swan and Maggie Haberman \par \uc2 1,587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley staked out a clear, hawkish vision. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  avoided risks. And the night's glaring absentee, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , again emerged untouched.\par \par \uc2 It was the undercard that underwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 The third straight Republican presidential debate that former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  has skipped -- choosing instead to rally with supporters a few miles away -- represented a critical and shrinking chance for his rivals to close his chasm of a polling advantage.\par \par \uc2 And with only five candidates on the stage for the first time -- \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Nikki Haley, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  and Tim Scott -- they all had far more time to speak.\par \par \uc2 Yet they had precious little to say about Mr. Trump, even when given the chance just over two months before the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 They sparred in a substantive debate that dissected disagreements over aid to Ukraine, Social Security, confronting China, banning TikTok and how to approach \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  less than 24 hours after Republicans suffered their latest electoral setbacks driven by the fall of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But there was something surreal about such detailed discussions unfolding among candidates who seem so far from the Oval Office -- even Mr. DeSantis and Ms. Haley, who asserted themselves as the leaders of the non-Trump pack.\par \par \uc2 Here are six takeaways from a debate in Miami that may best be remembered for Ms. Haley snapping at Mr. Ramaswamy, ''You're just scum.''\par \par \uc2 Haley came out swinging.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley emerged as a power center on the debate stage, giving a forceful performance that took advantage of the night's focus on foreign policy to present a clear and hawkish vision of America's role in the world.\par \par \uc2 Leaning into her experience as the U.S. ambassador to the United Nations, she staked out expansive, interventionist positions that cut against Mr. Trump's ''America First'' foreign policy vision.\par \par \uc2 She backed Ukraine to the hilt. She said she would support military strikes against Iran. And she said the United States needed to support Israel with ''whatever they need and whenever they need it.''\par \par \uc2 Most of the other candidates gave versions of the same responses -- but Ms. Haley had the edge of having represented the United States on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 When the candidates were asked what they would urge Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  of Israel to do at this moment, Mr. DeSantis said he ''would be telling'' him to eliminate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Ms. Haley said she did, in fact, tell Mr. Netanyahu to ''finish them.''\par \par \uc2 As Ms. Haley vies with Mr. DeSantis to establish herself as the field's Trump alternative, some of the party's biggest donors were closely watching her performance as they weighed whether to spend millions on her behalf in a desperate final effort to beat Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley's competitors recognized her rising status by taking aim at her.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is still playing it safe in a game he's losing.\par \par \uc2 It seemed, for a moment, as if this would be a different kind of debate for Mr. DeSantis. His opening answer affirmatively outlined how he would be better than Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''He should explain why he didn't have Mexico pay for the border wall,'' Mr. DeSantis began. ''He should explain why he racked up so much debt. He should explain why he didn't drain the swamp.'' He went on to say that Mr. Trump promised ''winning'' only to have his party endure years of ''losing,'' including on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ''In Florida, I showed how it's done,'' Mr. DeSantis declared, trying to take hometown advantage of a debate held in Miami.\par \par \uc2 But then he mostly left Mr. Trump untouched, satisfied to prosecute his own case and push back on rivals like Ms. Haley. It was the same strategy he used in the first two debates, with little traction gained.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis is plainly more comfortable than in the first debate. Yet he surprisingly left unsaid a development that his campaign has advertised as a game changer: the endorsement this week of Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 You can't debate someone who isn't there.\par \par \uc2 The candidates again did little to aggressively contrast themselves with Mr. Trump, who has made himself unavailable for direct sparring by refusing to stand onstage with his rivals or, for the most part, appear with them at multicandidate gatherings on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 Without Mr. Trump present, the five contenders were left to tear one another down, with varying levels of nastiness.\par \par \uc2 The first question to the candidates was the fundamental one most of them have struggled to answer to Republican voters: why they, and not Mr. Trump, should be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Christie, as expected, was the sharpest in his attack, arguing that someone who faces Mr. Trump's criminal charges ''cannot lead this party or this country.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. DeSantis took only a brief swipe. Ms. Haley praised Mr. Trump's presidency, then criticized him, saying that he had gone ''weak in the knees'' on Ukraine and that his time had passed. Mr. Ramaswamy defended Mr. Trump in passing. And Mr. Scott talked about himself.\par \par \uc2 That was almost the extent of efforts to chip away at the runaway front-runner. Nearby, Mr. Trump held a rally in Hialeah, Fla., remarking at one point that his rivals were ''not watchable.''\par \par \uc2 For months, the candidates have struggled to find a way to force him into the ring with them, with Mr. Christie threatening to follow him on the campaign trail and Mr. DeSantis, in recent days, lobbing crass responses to Mr. Trump's brutal taunts. In the third debate, none of them figured out how to make it work.\par \par \uc2 This debate got personal.\par \par \uc2 After three debates, this much is clear: Some of the candidates onstage really don't like one another.\par \par \uc2 The most loathed appears to be Mr. Ramaswamy, who from the start fought not just with the rivals flanking him but also with the NBC moderators and the head of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , whom he urged to resign in his opening statement.\par \par \uc2 At times, Mr. Ramaswamy almost seemed to be doing Mr. Trump's bidding, attacking NBC's past coverage of the former president's scandals.\par \par \uc2 He made acidic attacks on Ms. Haley, mocking her foreign policy and calling her ''\uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  in three-inch heels.'' He slipped in a crack about Mr. DeSantis's footwear, suggesting that the Florida governor, too, was wearing lifts. Mr. DeSantis ignored him. Ms. Haley said hers were five inches and ''for ammunition.''\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Ramaswamy later invoked her daughter's use of TikTok, she demanded, ''Leave my daughter out of your voice,'' and then added in almost disbelief about the exchange, ''You're just scum.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a Republican quagmire.\par \par \uc2 After Tuesday's defeats, the Republican candidates knew they would face questions about the way forward on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But they mostly seemed uncertain what to say.\par \par \uc2 ''We're better off when we can promote a culture of life,'' said Mr. DeSantis, who signed a six-week ban in his state. He said little at all about what his party should do or what he would do as president. ''At the same time, I understand that some of these states are doing it a little bit different.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley described herself as opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said that passing national restrictions would be virtually impossible, arguing that it's crucial to be ''honest'' with the public.\par \par \uc2 At times, Ms. Haley seemed to be trying to appeal to general-election voters. ''I don't judge anyone for being pro-choice,'' she said. It was the kind of line that makes Democratic strategists worry about her strength if she were to win the nomination -- but also one that the G.O.P. base is unlikely to welcome.\par \par \uc2 It all amounted to a reminder that Republicans, after decades of campaigning against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, have yet to figure out what to say after finally getting their wish through a Supreme Court that Mr. Trump -- who also won't say where he stands on a national ban -- reshaped.\par \par \uc2 Was this Tim Scott's swan song?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Scott qualified for this debate by the narrowest of margins, with only a single poll -- the legitimacy of which some of his rivals have privately disputed -- ensuring his spot. But the thresholds will be higher for the next debate in December, and Mr. Scott's allies acknowledge that he needs to something, anything, just to remain a factor.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to imagine that he did anything on Wednesday night to change his trajectory. He stuck to the same messages he has been hitting throughout the campaign. He described an America in need of spiritual healing and a return to Judeo-Christian values.\par \par \uc2 He received more attention for what he did after the debate than for anything he said during it. Mr. Scott, 58, has never been married, and entire newspaper stories have been dedicated to a mysterious girlfriend who had never been seen with him in public.\par \par \uc2 Until he brought her onstage.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting from Hialeah, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Michael Gold contributed reporting from Hialeah, Fla.\par \par \uc2 The Republican candidates had little to say about the absent front-runner, Donald J. Trump, even with Iowa voting looming.; Nikki Haley, an ex-ambassador to the United Nations, said there would be no Hamas without Iran. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SCOTT McINTYRE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231110ejba0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
