\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Who Won the Third Republican Debate?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Rappeport \par \uc2 1,101 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley stood out, absorbing attacks and doling out many of her own. But political observers wondered how any of the candidates onstage cut into former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's dominance.\par \par \uc2 The third Republican presidential debate this year, held on Wednesday in Miami, was packed with heated exchanges over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, immigration and China as the candidates tried to shine as the front-runner, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , once again declined to join them onstage.\par \par \uc2 This time around, the hopefuls dialed up the battle for second place and delivered digs at President Biden, but directed only glancing criticism at Mr. Trump. There were also plenty of parries between the candidates. At times, they moved beyond policy and became personal, such as when Nikki Haley called \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  ''scum'' after he referred to her daughter.\par \par \uc2 While Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida also delivered a nimble performance and made more of an effort to show empathy with voters, commentators tended to agree that Mr. Trump's strategy of staying away from the debate fray was still paying dividends.\par \par \uc2 ''That debate pretty much seals it for Trump,'' Michael McDonald, a professor at the \uc2 University of Florida\uc2  who specializes in American elections, said on the social media platform X. ''The Munchkins on the stage were too afraid to take him head on.''\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley, the former governor of South Carolina who served as ambassador to the United Nations in the Trump administration, had been rising in the polls after two strong debate performances. On Wednesday, despite expectations that she would take some hits, she delivered another steady debate, demonstrating her foreign policy chops in an extended discussion about the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and clapping back at her opponents.\par \par \uc2 She exchanged barbs with Mr. DeSantis over who was more accommodating of Beijing, despite both taking a firm anti-China stance.\par \par \uc2 But it was Ms. Haley's exchange with Mr. Ramaswamy, the entrepreneur who had an early surge in the polls, that will perhaps be the most memorable of the evening. After Mr. Ramaswamy mocked Ms. Haley for pledging to ban TikTok while her adult daughter was a user of the app, Ms. Haley snapped back.\par \par \uc2 ''Leave my daughter out of your voice,'' she said. ''You're just scum.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Haley also won plaudits for pragmatism on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a day after Democrats won big victories in elections across the country because of their strength on the issue. She said that she opposes it but argued that a federal ban was unrealistic given the makeup of Congress. She added that people who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should not be criticized.\par \par \uc2 ''Nikki Haley, to her credit, has had nuance and led on the issue from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the outset of her campaign,'' Alyssa Farah Griffin, a former director of strategic communications in the Trump administration, said on CNN after the debate.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\par \par \uc2 After two somewhat stilted performances, Mr. DeSantis gave sharp answers on how he would take on China and offer unwavering support for Israel in its fight to dismantle \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . He continued to use forceful language about how he would combat the flow of drugs across the southern border and said he would punish universities for allowing antisemitic protests.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis also showed a more nuanced side at times, treading carefully when the debate turned to the topic of a federal ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , defending his opposition to drilling in the Everglades and pledging to make sure people who are eligible receive their full Social Security benefits.\par \par \uc2 ''Tonight was a really strong night for DeSantis, punctuated by a pitch-perfect closing statement,'' said Brian Bartlett, a strategist who worked on \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 's presidential campaigns in 2008 and 2012. ''He not only spoke directly to the issues G.O.P. voters care about, but in a personal and empathetic way that distinguished him from the rest of the pack.''\par \par \uc2 Tim Scott\par \par \uc2 Following an impassioned second debate, Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina tried to keep the conservative energy flowing with an aggressive approach toward Iran and a staunch position against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At one point, Mr. Scott even seemed to suggest that the United States should attack Iran.\par \par \uc2 ''You actually have to cut off the head of the snake, and the head of the snake is Iran and not simply the proxies,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Scott pushed his rivals to support a federal ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The position could play into the hands of Democrats, who have been winning on the issue in elections across the country.\par \par \uc2 ''Voters are practically shouting that what they want is control over their own bodies and not MAGA extremism,'' said Alexandra LaManna, a former White House spokeswoman in the Biden administration who focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris Christie\par \par \uc2 A former governor of New Jersey, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  has been the fiercest critic of Mr. Trump, even referring to him as ''Donald Duck'' for skipping the debates.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, however, Mr. Christie mostly laid off Mr. Trump. Instead, he offered moderate views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expressed support for raising the retirement age and offered unwavering support for Israel and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 In his final statement, Mr. Christie said he was tired of division, anger and petty politics but did not mention Mr. Trump by name.\par \par \uc2 To some observers, the less pugnacious approach was lacking.\par \par \uc2 ''Christie has entirely faded into the background in this debate,'' said James Richardson, a former spokesman and adviser for Gov. Haley Barbour of Mississippi and Jon M. Huntsman, a U.S. ambassador to Russia in the Trump administration. ''Highly out of character.''\par \par \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\par \par \uc2 The debate stage has been less hospitable to Mr. Ramaswamy after he made an early splash on the campaign trail this year. On Wednesday night, he was clearly making every effort to stand out.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ramaswamy opened the night by blasting the news media and the NBC debate hosts, and proceeding to lob rapid-fire criticism of all his opponents, except for Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 The attack on Ms. Haley's daughter succeeded in getting under her skin, but most likely backfired with viewers and voters.\par \par \uc2 ''Nikki expressed what many of us in the audience and at home think of your desperate attempt to create 'a moment,''' Michael Steele, a former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  chairman, said on the X platform.\par \par \uc2 The presidential hopefuls seemed content to aim for second place behind former President Donald J. Trump and deliver digs at President Biden. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Maansi Srivastava/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231109ejb900052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
