\par\par \uc2 WHITE HOUSE MEMO\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Elections Buoy Biden for Now, But '24 Looms\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Baker \par \uc2 1,516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's results were a win for the president after demoralizing poll numbers. But some strategists argue the outcomes show only that Democrats are doing well, not necessarily the president.\par \par \uc2 Poll? What poll? The Democratic victories in Tuesday's off-year elections gave President Biden's White House some desperately needed breathing space just when it needed it.\par \par \uc2 After days of deepening angst within the party following a demoralizing survey that showed Mr. Biden losing in five key battleground states, Wednesday morning arrived with a sense of told-you-so vindication at a White House that has long insisted that polls don't matter, only elections do.\par \par \uc2 ''The Democrats have got to quit their whining, quit their hand-wringing and do something,'' \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , the former governor of Virginia and onetime chairman of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , said in an interview. ''Last night was just a huge gust of wind in our sails. This is all doable. There is a lot of excitement out there. Let's just carry this on to next year's election.''\par \par \uc2 The Biden campaign quickly issued a celebratory email meant to capitalize on the results, calm nervous allies and poke at the pessimists. ''Voters across the country overwhelmingly proved the pollsters and pundits wrong once again and turned out to reject the MAGA extremism that has come to define today's Republican Party,'' wrote Michael Tyler, the campaign's communications director.\par \par \uc2 But the moment of validation may provide only a brief respite, as some political strategists have concluded that what the election outcomes really show is that the Democrats do not have a Democrat problem, but a Biden problem. The wave of Democratic success in Virginia, Kentucky and Ohio did not, in this view, change the fundamental weakness of an incumbent in the White House with historically low approval ratings. It only emphasized the disparity between his public standing and the party's.\par \par \uc2 ''It would appear that the Democrats' success in the 2022 midterms and 2023 elections was in spite of Biden, not because of him,'' said Douglas B. Sosnik, who was White House political director for President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Off-year elections are not predictive of future races, and it has long been a hazardous exercise to draw overly broad conclusions from state and local elections around the country in odd-numbered years, when turnout is a fraction of a presidential election and a variety of parochial factors influence results.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the Democrats had plenty to cheer about on Wednesday. They won a hard-fought gubernatorial re-election in the red state of Kentucky, passed a constitutional amendment enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Republican-leaning Ohio and captured the state legislature in Virginia, despite the concerted efforts of the Republican Party's star governor, Glenn Youngkin, possibly dashing his own White House ambitions.\par \par \uc2 The wave of relief washing over the White House was palpable as the president's team welcomed the first good day it has had politically in a while. Mr. Biden spent Tuesday evening watching results with Steven J. Ricchetti, his counselor. He called some of the winners, including Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky; Gabe Amo, a former Biden White House aide and a son of West African immigrants who won a special election for a House seat in Rhode Island; and Cherelle Parker, the first woman to be elected mayor of Philadelphia. Aides spent the evening sending one another ''Dark Brandon'' memes, a playful internet alter ego of Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 The election gains were well timed for Mr. Biden, coming after polling by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College that found him losing to former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  in five swing states that Mr. Biden won in 2020. The results, Biden advisers stressed, reaffirmed that Democrats have overperformed in every election since the Supreme Court's decision overturning a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They pointed to Kentucky, where Republicans spent $30 million trying to tie Mr. Beshear to the unpopular president, and his challenger, Daniel Cameron, closed the campaign with an endorsement from Mr. Trump, all for naught. It is also true that Mr. Beshear spent much of the campaign distancing himself from Mr. Biden. But Biden advisers noted that two-thirds of the 23 Democratic candidates for the state legislature in Virginia endorsed by the president and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  won.\par \par \uc2 In a one-minute statement to reporters at the White House on Wednesday, Ms. Harris focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the critical factor but implicitly acknowledged the uphill challenge ahead for her and Mr. Biden. ''It was a good night, and the president and I obviously have a lot of work to do to earn our re-election,'' she said. ''But I'm confident we're going to win.''\par \par \uc2 David Plouffe, the architect of President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's rise to the White House, agreed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''remains an issue driving voters everywhere'' and that this week's elections showed that strong Democratic candidates can prevail.\par \par \uc2 He added that the results would help the Biden campaign find the voters it needs next year. ''The president's campaign and other Democrats who are struggling now will figure out across these states who voted Democrat or progressive on issues but are not yet with them and discover why and how they can be moved back into their column,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Anna Greenberg, a Democratic pollster, said Tuesday's elections demonstrated not only the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for Democrats but also the failure of Republican culture war issues to resonate with a wider audience.\par \par \uc2 ''I really think people need to stop looking at the pre-Trump era for guidance on what will happen now,'' she said. ''Our electorate is more polarized, more ideological, more energized and fragmented, and I don't see us going back to normal anytime soon.''\par \par \uc2 Cornell Belcher, who was Mr. Obama's pollster, echoed that point, saying the poll numbers that look so bad for the president do not capture the variables that will break his way when the moment of decision actually arrives. ''That's not in the Biden horse race,'' he said, ''but it is in the Election Day results which once again defied the so-called pundits who mostly are just talkers who don't actually build and work campaigns.''\par \par \uc2 Still, those gloomy polls keep on coming, illustrating Mr. Biden's challenge and shaping the environment in which a campaign will play out. A new survey by The Atlanta Journal-Constitution and the University of Georgia on Wednesday showed the president in a dead heat with Mr. Trump in Georgia, a state that was critical to Mr. Biden's victory in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And for the first time in Mr. Biden's presidency, a majority of voters in reliably blue California disapprove of Mr. Biden's job performance, according to another poll by The Los Angeles Times and the Institute of Governmental Studies at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 . Mr. Biden still leads Mr. Trump, and no Democrat is seriously worried about losing California. But the findings underscore the problems of the president, who is 80 years old, with his own base as many Democrats tell pollsters that they think he is too old to run again.\par \par \uc2 As much as they are frustrated by such polls, some Biden advisers see a silver lining in them because they remind the president's supporters that next year's election could be achingly close, thereby motivating them to work harder for his campaign and turn out in greater numbers.\par \par \uc2 The president's allies increasingly have come to the conclusion that they may not be able to sell Mr. Biden and instead need to focus more on scaring voters about the possible return of Mr. Trump. In the meantime, they will savor Tuesday night's elections and use them to bolster Democratic morale.\par \par \uc2 ''The immediate benefits to Biden are mostly narrative,'' said Sarah Longwell, a Republican strategist and a leading opponent of Mr. Trump who supported Mr. Biden in 2020. ''If you're his team, you can wave last night's results in naysayers' faces and say, 'See, Democrats keep winning elections under Biden. You keep underestimating him, but he wins when it matters.' It also disrupts his doomsday news cycle after the bad polls.''\par \par \uc2 ''But ultimately,'' she added, ''I don't think last night tells us anything about 2024.'' While the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll highlighted Mr. Biden's vulnerability, she said next year's election ''is going to be about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .'' The task for the Biden campaign then, she said, will be making sure that ''it galvanizes the anti-Trump vote, which is a different thing than a pro-Biden vote.''\par \par \uc2 Ohioans enshrined \uc2  rights in their constitution. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); Recent polling found President Biden losing to former President Donald J. Trump in five swing states that Mr. Biden won in 2020. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A17) This article appeared in print on page A1, A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231109ejb90003m\par }\page