\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 How  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Lifted Democrats, and More Takeaways From Tuesday's Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman and Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,564 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden is unpopular, but the winning streak for his party and its policies has been extended through another election night.\par \par \uc2 The political potency of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights proved more powerful than the drag of President Biden's approval ratings in Tuesday's off-year elections, as Ohioans enshrined a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state's constitution, and Democrats took control of both chambers of the Virginia General Assembly while holding on to Kentucky's governorship.\par \par \uc2 The night's results showed the durability of Democrats' political momentum since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and ended the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. It may also, at least temporarily, stem the latest round of Democratic fretting from a series of polls demonstrating Mr. Biden's political weakness.\par \par \uc2 After a strong midterm showing last year, a blowout victory in the Wisconsin Supreme Court race in April and a series of special election wins, Democrats head into Mr. Biden's re-election contest with the wind at their backs. The question for the party is how they can translate that momentum to Mr. Biden, who remains unpopular while others running on his agenda have prevailed.\par \par \uc2 Here are key takeaways from Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 There's nothing like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aid Democrats and Biden.\par \par \uc2 Democratic officials have been saying for months that the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has become the issue that best motivates Democrats to vote, and is also the issue that persuades the most Republicans to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, they found new evidence to bolster their case in victories by Gov. \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  of Kentucky, who criticized his opponent's defense of the state's near-total ban; legislative candidates in Virginia who opposed the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban proposed by the Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin; and, above all, the Ohio referendum establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. A Pennsylvania Supreme Court candidate who ran on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Daniel McCaffery, also won, giving Democrats a 5-2 majority.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now so powerful as a Democratic issue that Everytown, the gun control organization founded and funded by \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2 , used its TV ads in Virginia to promote \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before it discussed gun violence.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrat who ran for governor of Mississippi, \uc2 Brandon Presley\uc2 , underperformed expectations, losing by twice the margin that his party's nominee did in 2019.\par \par \uc2 It's a sign that no matter how weak Mr. Biden's standing is, the political environment and the issues terrain are still strong for Democrats running on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and against Republicans who defend bans.\par \par \uc2 The last six Kentucky governor's elections have been won by the same party that won the presidential election the following year. The president may not be able to do what Mr. Beshear managed -- talking up Biden policies without ever mentioning the president's name -- but he now has examples of what a winning road map could look like for 2024.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia, a Republican rising star faces an eclipse.\par \par \uc2 Governor Youngkin had hoped a strong night for his party would greatly raise his stature as the Republican who turned an increasingly blue state back to red. That would at the very least include him in the conversation for the Republican presidential nomination in 2028, if not 2024.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Youngkin's pledge to enact what he called a moderate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law -- a ban on abortions after 15 weeks with exceptions for rape, incest and to save the life of an endangered mother -- gave Democrats an effective counter as he sought full control of state government.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic argument won the day, at least in part. The party seized the majority in the House of Delegates, kept control of the State Senate and definitely spoiled Mr. Youngkin's night. The results offered nervous national Democrats still more evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's power as a motivator for their voters while upending the term-limited Mr. Youngkin's plans for his final two years on office, and possibly beyond.\par \par \uc2 A Democrat can win in deep-red Kentucky, if his name is \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Being the most popular governor in the country turns out to be a good thing if you want to get re-elected.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear spent his first term and his re-election campaign hyperfocused on local issues like teacher salaries, new road projects, guiding the state through the pandemic and natural disasters and, since last summer's Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, opposing his state's total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That made him politically bulletproof when his Republican challenger, Attorney General \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , who was endorsed by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , sought to nationalize the campaign and juice G.O.P. turnout by tying Mr. Beshear to Mr. Biden and attacking him on crime and L.G.B.T.Q. issues. (Mr. Beshear vetoed new restrictions aimed at transgender young people, though G.O.P. lawmakers voted to override him.)\par \par \uc2 It's not as if Republican voters stayed home; all the other Republicans running for statewide office won with at least 57 percent of the vote. Mr. Beshear just got enough of them to back him for governor. A Democrat who can win Republican voters without making compromises on issues important to liberal voters is someone the rest of the party will want to emulate in red states and districts across the country.\par \par \uc2 Attacks on transgender rights didn't work.\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has become the top issue motivating Democrats, and with same-sex marriage broadly accepted in America, Republicans casting about for an issue to motivate social conservatives landed on restricting rights for transgender people. On Tuesday, that didn't work.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, Mr. Cameron and his Republican allies spent more than $5 million on television ads attacking L.G.B.T.Q. rights and Mr. Beshear for his defense of them, according to AdImpact, a firm that tracks political advertising. Gov. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  in Mississippi spent $1.2 million on anti-L.G.B.T.Q. ads, while Republicans running for legislative seats in Virginia spent $527,000 worth of TV time on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in Virginia, \uc2 Danica Roem\uc2 , a member of the House of Delegates, will become the South's first transgender state senator after defeating a former Fairfax County police detective who supported barring transgender athletes from competing in high school sports.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, voters back both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weed.\par \par \uc2 Ohioans once again showed the popularity of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, even in reliably Republican states, when they easily approved a constitutional amendment establishing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The vote in Ohio could be a harbinger for the coming presidential election season, when proponents and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are trying to put the issue before voters in the critical battleground states of Florida, Nevada, Arizona and Pennsylvania.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups entered Tuesday on a winning streak with such ballot measures since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last year. And ultimately, Ohio voters did as voters before them had done -- electing to preserve the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state.\par \par \uc2 And with a margin that was almost identical to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vote, Ohioans also legalized recreational marijuana use. That will make Ohio the 24th state to do so.\par \par \uc2 Where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't an issue, a Republican won easily.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's governor's race was the exception to this off-year election's rule on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The incumbent governor, Mr. Reeves, and his Democratic challenger, Mr. Presley, ran as staunch opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 And in that race, the Democrat lost.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Presley hoped to make the Mississippi race close by tying the incumbent to a public corruption scandal that saw the misspending of $94 million in federal funds intended for Mississippi's poor on projects like a college volleyball facility pushed by the retired superstar quarterback Brett Favre. He also pressed for the expansion of Medicaid under the Affordable Care Act to save Mississippi's collapsing rural hospitals.\par \par \uc2 But in Mississippi, Mr. Reeves had three advantages that proved impenetrable: incumbency, the ''R'' next to his name on the ballot, and the endorsement of Mr. Trump, who won the state in 2020 by nearly 17 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky races beneath the marquee governor's contest, Democrats also did not run on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they, like Mr. Presley, lost.\par \par \uc2 Rhode Island sends a Biden aide to the House.\par \par \uc2 Rhode Island is hardly a swing state, but still, the heavily Democratic enclave's election of Gabe Amo to one of its two House seats most likely brought a smile to Mr. Biden's face. Mr. Amo was a deputy director of the White House office of intergovernmental affairs and as such, becomes the first Biden White House aide to rise to Congress.\par \par \uc2 The son of African immigrants, Mr. Amo will also be the first Black representative from the Ocean State.\par \par \uc2 White House officials said the president congratulated his former aide on his victory. The special election fills the seat vacated by David Cicilline, a Democrat who left the seat to run a nonprofit.\par \par \uc2 A watch party in Columbus, Ohio, celebrated after a measure passed cementing \uc2  rights in the state's constitution. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Maddie McGarvey for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231108ejb800059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
