\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 's Pull vs. Biden's Unpopularity: What to Watch in Tuesday's Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,135 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Important races are happening throughout the country, including contests in Virginia that could determine the fate of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Ohio, Kentucky, Virginia, Mississippi and elsewhere head to the polls on Tuesday for off-year elections that will offer clues to the continued potency of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the drag of President Biden's low approval ratings as politicians prepare for the coming presidential election year.\par \par \uc2 The results may determine whether Democrats find some reassurances on their approach to key issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was a bright spot for the party in a new New York Times/Siena poll that showed Donald J. Trump leading Mr. Biden in five critical swing states one year out.\par \par \uc2 Here is what to watch:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access vs. Biden's unpopularity in Virginia and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 All 140 seats in Virginia's General Assembly are on the ballot Tuesday, with the Democratic-leaning state's relatively popular Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin, hoping to capture the State Senate and secure total Republican control of Richmond. That feat would propel Mr. Youngkin's national ambitions.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats are running on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, warning that G.O.P. control would end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the last state in the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Youngkin is testing a compromise that national Republicans hope will be a winning message after so many party losses since the Supreme Court overturned the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after 15 weeks of pregnancy, with exemptions for rape, incest and the life of a mother. Democrats say that is a ruse, but they must overcome the weight of Mr. Biden's unpopularity.\par \par \uc2 A similar dynamic is playing out in Kentucky, where Democrats have leaned heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, especially to tarnish the Republican challenger for governor, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2 , who, as the current state attorney general, has had to defend Kentucky's total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The incumbent Democratic governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , remains popular, with a family name (his father, Steve Beshear, was also a governor) and a moderate reputation that have insulated him against attacks that he is soft on crime and supports ''radical'' transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Beshear has led consistently in the polls, but in a state that former President Donald J. Trump won by about 26 percentage points in 2020, the ''D'' by Mr. Beshear's name is a liability. The final polls of the cycle pointed to a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 Will voters in Ohio back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights?\par \par \uc2 Ohio has been a reliably Republican state since the rise of Mr. Trump, but a referendum to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the state constitution could be the purest test on Tuesday of where even Republicans stand on the issue. Or not.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have been on a winning streak with ballot measures that put the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  straight to voters since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, doing away with constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Even in deeply Republican states like Kansas, voters have overwhelmingly supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents scored some important victories before the referendum on Tuesday. In this contest, voters will have to affirmatively vote ''yes'' on a constitutional change; Ohioans have historically tended to reject ballot amendments.\par \par \uc2 While the amendment would establish ''a right to make and carry out one's own reproductive decisions,'' it also explicitly allows the state to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after viability, or around 23 weeks, when the fetus can survive outside the uterus, unless the pregnant woman's doctor finds the procedure ''is necessary to protect the pregnant patient's life or health.'' But in the ballot box, voters will see a summary from the secretary of state, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , a Republican who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which says the amendment ''would always allow an unborn child to be aborted at any stage of pregnancy, regardless of viability.''\par \par \uc2 Both sides of the issue have accused the other of misinformation and underhanded tactics.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, a test of expanding Medicaid -- and scandal.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban brought down Roe v. Wade when the Supreme Court sided with Thomas E. Dobbs, Mississippi's health officer, in Dobbs v. Jackson.\par \par \uc2 The Deep South state now faces a pitched battle for governor, but the candidates have not made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the central issue, since the incumbent Republican governor, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , and his Democratic challenger, Brandon Presley, both oppose it.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Mr. Presley's surprisingly potent challenge has been fueled by a push to expand Medicaid under the Affordable Care Act and a public corruption scandal that saw the misspending of $94 million in federal funds intended for Mississippi's poor on projects like a college volleyball facility pushed by the retired superstar quarterback Brett Favre.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Reeves was never directly implicated in the scandal, but he did fire an investigating attorney just after the lawyer issued a subpoena that could have turned up details about the involvement of prominent Mississippians\par \par \uc2 ''If you think \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  will take on corruption, I've got some beachfront property in Nettleton to sell you,'' Mr. Presley said in a debate this month, referring to his hometown in the state's northeast.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Presley, a member of the \uc2 Mississippi Public Service Commission\uc2 , has a unique kind of name recognition; he is a second cousin of Elvis Presley.\par \par \uc2 But in Mississippi, Mr. Reeves has three advantages that could prove impenetrable: incumbency, the ''R'' next to his name on the ballot, and the endorsement of Mr. Trump, who won the state in 2020 by nearly 17 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Ballot initiatives, from wealth to weed.\par \par \uc2 Voters will make numerous direct decisions on Tuesday, bypassing elected officials. Beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the most watched initiative will be, again, in Ohio, where voters will decide whether cannabis should be legalized for recreational use. If voters agree, Ohio would become the 24th state to legalize marijuana. That could put pressure on Congress to move forward legislation at least to ease restrictions on interstate banking for legal cannabis businesses.\par \par \uc2 Texans will decide the fate of 14 constitutional amendments, including one that would bar the state from imposing a ''wealth'' tax, or a tax on the market value of assets owned but not sold. Liberal activists and some prominent Democratic senators, such as Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts, have pushed such taxes as the only way to tap the wealth of billionaires, whose income taxes are minimal but whose vast, untaxed wealth supports lavish lifestyles.\par \par \uc2 Texans will also decide whether to raise the mandatory retirement age of state judges to 79, from 75.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Ohio will decide on enshrining \uc2  rights in the state constitution, as well as legalizing recreational marijuana use. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Carolyn Kaster/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020231107ejb70004w\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 rst=nytf AND (abortion OR "reproductive rights" OR "reproductive health") AND (atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive rights"))\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\c